window.chaptersData = [{"chapter": 0, "title": "Hawaldarni", "content": "<div class=\"kr-cover\"><img src=\"cover.jpg\" alt=\"Hawaldarni book cover\"><\/div>"}, {"chapter": 0, "title": "Title Page", "content": "<div class=\"tp\"><h3><i>What He Never Said, and How Much It Was<\/i><\/h3>\n<h3>A novel<\/h3>\n<p class=\"bold-line\"><b>Sharda Ashok Yaduvanshi<\/b><\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>Hawaldarni: What He Never Said, and How Much It Was<\/p>\n<p>Copyright © Sharda Ashok Yaduvanshi<\/p>\n<p>All rights reserved. This is a work of fiction. Names, characters, places, and incidents are either products of the author's imagination or are used fictitiously. Any resemblance to actual persons, living or dead, events, or locales is entirely coincidental.<\/p>\n<p>No part of this publication may be reproduced, distributed, or transmitted in any form or by any means, including photocopying, recording, or other electronic or mechanical methods, without the prior written permission of the author, except in the case of brief quotations embodied in critical reviews and certain other noncommercial uses permitted by copyright law.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p><i>For eleven years, Naina believed a promise was broken.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>She was twenty-one, on the one night her grief finally became too much to carry quietly, when she wrote it in her own blood — \"I am waiting. You promised me you will come back. Come back alive.\" And he never replied, and she told herself the silence had answered her question long ago.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>How much it was. She would spend the rest of her life understanding exactly that.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>Eleven years later, on an ordinary evening in February 2026, in a small bookshop, a cover catches her eye — and there, pasted inside its pages, she found her own three-line letter, the one she had once written in her own blood.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>He had once promised her a stillness that would arrive without warning — the way the air itself stills before the sky remembers how to weep. In that same moment, it found her — sudden, complete, impossible to explain — and for a moment she did not dare believe it was real.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>This story is for every heart, either men or women, who has ever loved someone and waited for them in silence.<\/i><\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p><\/div>"}, {"chapter": 1, "title": "Interlude — Somewhere, a File Was Already Open", "content": "<h1>Interlude — Somewhere, a File Was Already Open<\/h1>\n<p><i>Long before anyone close to him understood the full shape of his life, a different room, in a different part of Delhi, had already begun deciding things about Vikram Rathore's life without his knowledge.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>Two men sat across a table from a thin file, the fluorescent light overhead doing nothing to soften the room's deliberate plainness — the kind of anonymous office no signage ever explains.<\/p>\n<p>Not even Vikram himself, years later, would ever learn this meeting had taken place.<\/p>\n<p>The senior of the two was well past sixty, silver hair cropped close in the old military style he'd never quite abandoned, the kind of stillness in him that comes only from decades spent being the most patient man in any room. Beside him sat someone younger, unremarkable in exactly the deliberate way this work eventually teaches a man to be. His name, for the purposes of this room and every room like it afterward, was Kamath.<\/p>\n<p>\"Captain Vikram Rathore,\" the senior man said, sliding a photograph across the table. \"Run a full background check. Personal history, not just service record.\" He paused. \"And keep him under quiet watch after that. I want to know exactly who this man becomes — long before we ever let him know we're watching, or why.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Yes, sir,\" Kamath said, already reaching for his own notebook, though even he couldn't yet have said what, exactly, they were watching for.<\/p>\n<p>Neither man spoke of Vikram Rathore again that day. But his name had already begun moving through channels no outsider was ever meant to see.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere far away, still years from any of it, a young soldier named Vikram Rathore had no idea his life had just quietly stopped being entirely his own — or that it would be years yet before he understood exactly why.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 2, "title": "Chapter 1 — The Tray", "content": "<h1>Chapter 1 — The Tray<\/h1>\n<p>He didn't notice her. Not that night. Vikram Rathore sat through another man's wedding at twenty-one, watching a stranger circle the fire in red and gold, with no idea she would, in time, undo him more thoroughly than anything he had yet learned to prepare for.<\/p>\n<p>He was home on leave from the Army that year, though nothing in his manner that evening announced it. Three years of kabaddi for his university had built the frame the uniform later sharpened — broad through the shoulders, lean everywhere else. His skin was fair, his eyes dark and unhurried, watchful in a way that gave little away. More than one girl at that wedding found her gaze drifting back to him, though he seemed entirely unaware of it, something in the way he carried himself never quite leaving the uniform behind.<\/p>\n<p>The drive took the better part of two hours, seventy kilometres down the Pataudi road from Vikram's village of Badshahpur to Kosli, Kavita's family village, for the wedding.<\/p>\n<p>The marriage hall sat back from the highway behind a row of dhabas, a squat building with a corrugated tin roof and a string of bare bulbs along its boundary wall, half of them already dead.<\/p>\n<p>Inside, the mandap sat raised on a wooden platform draped in cheap red cloth, marigold garlands already wilting along the pillars.<\/p>\n<p>Chaudhary found them a spot near the buffet counters, the smell of tempering mustard seeds and hot ghee reaching them before the food did — steel plates, dal, jeera rice, shahi paneer under the heat lamps, tandoori rotis arriving hot in relays, choole, kachumber, puris stacked at the far end.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram filled his plate mechanically, tasting almost none of it once he sat back down, his eyes drifting past the crowd toward the mandap more out of boredom than interest.<\/p>\n<p>The bride sat through the rituals the way most brides do — still, jewellery-heavy, her face angled down toward the fire, red and gold blurring into the general colour of the evening. She was marrying a boy from Vikram's own village; attendance hadn't been a question, though Vikram barely knew the groom beyond a shared surname. He felt nothing watching her — not curiosity, not even idle interest. He finished his plate, went back for a second helping of dessert, and spent most of the ceremony half-listening to Chaudhary's commentary on people neither of them knew well enough to actually be discussing.<\/p>\n<p>It would be months before he understood exactly how much he had failed to notice that evening. The same woman circling a fire in red and gold, entirely unremarkable to him at the time, would one day stand in a village courtyard holding a tray of tea a moment too long — and take apart, piece by piece, every ounce of indifference he was currently, comfortably, entirely unaware of feeling.<\/p>\n<p>She moved into the village after that — a slim woman of medium height, striking enough that heads turned without quite meaning to, her skin fair, with light, unusual blue-grey eyes that stood out startlingly against her colouring, rare enough in their part of Haryana that people remarked on them at her wedding. There was a sharpness behind those eyes too, a quick, assessing look that took in a room before she'd said a single word — the look of a woman who missed very little. Her hair fell long and thick past her waist, oiled and plaited into a single braid, the way most young brides in the village did, though even that plain style couldn't quite make her look ordinary. Even in those early days, there was something in the way she carried herself — spine straight, chin level — that made her look less like a girl newly married and more like a woman waiting for a life that hadn't arrived yet.<\/p>\n<p>It was on his very next leave, only months into her marriage, that he first found a reason to walk up to Ramfal's gate — a message for the father, some errand about a land paper, nothing that couldn't have waited, if he was honest with himself, which he mostly wasn't. She brought the tea out without being asked. Brass tray. Two cups if his father was home, one if he wasn't. The saucer always a fraction too full, as if her hand had hesitated at the pour. And every time, she stood there afterward. Just a moment too long. Watching.<\/p>\n<p>The first time he understood the look was not imagination was in summer, the courtyard empty except for the two of them and a dog that belonged to no one in particular. He had come with a message about a land paper. Ramfal was not home; the father-in-law was asleep in the inner room. She brought the tea as always — sleeve pushed to the elbow, a thin gold bangle knocking once against the tray — and set it on the low stool beside him. He thanked her. She did not leave.<\/p>\n<p>\"Garam hai,\" she said. Hot.<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Drink slowly then.\"<\/p>\n<p>He did. She watched the movement of his throat when he swallowed. When he set the cup down she took the tray. Their fingers did not touch.<\/p>\n<p>\"You come often,\" she said.<\/p>\n<p>\"Work.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Work.\" A small breath that might have been a laugh if either of them had allowed it that name. \"Ramfal is never here for work.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know you know.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I used to imagine, as a girl, exactly what kind of husband I wanted,\" she said once, on a different afternoon, watching him drink his tea rather than looking at him directly. \"Someone steady. Someone who'd actually look at me properly, not just through me the way most men look at their wives after the wedding photographs stop mattering.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Did you get him?\"<\/p>\n<p>Something flickered across her face — quick, unreadable, gone before he could name it. \"No,\" she said simply. \"Not yet, anyway.\"<\/p>\n<p>She turned then. He sat with the empty taste of sugar and cardamom and the knowledge that something had been said without a single improper word.<\/p>\n<p>He rode back to his own house that evening slower than usual, turning the exchange over, telling himself it was nothing worth turning over. He was a farmer's son from a good family, engaged eventually to whoever his father decided suited the land and the name, and a married woman's tea was not a thing a man in his position noticed twice. He noticed it anyway, and hated himself mildly for it.<\/p>\n<p>After that, the visits arranged themselves around the possibility of the tray. He told himself he did not plan them. A message for the father. A question about a cousin's enlistment. The long way back from the tehsil that happened to pass their gate when the sun was wrong for travel and right for tea. Each time the courtyard. Each time the tray. Each time the standing, a little longer than before.<\/p>\n<p>Ramfal was twenty-five that year, though something about him had always carried the staleness of a much older man — barely Kavita's own height, his frame doing nothing to suggest discipline or strength. His skin ran a shade darker than hers, weathered from afternoons spent doing very little in the sun.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram had seen him once, slumped against the courtyard wall on a hot afternoon, half asleep over a plate of food his own mother had had to remind him to eat. He was not, by any honest account, a quick man — conversations moved past him rather than through him, and he'd long since stopped trying to keep pace with them.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram, by contrast, was everything months of watching Ramfal do nothing had quietly, patiently taught Kavita to notice in a man: tall enough that she had to properly lift her chin to meet his eyes, a body shaped by discipline rather than neglect, and a mind that met hers fully, argument for argument, in a way Ramfal's never once had, not even accidentally.<\/p>\n<p>Her husband didn't work — not properly, not ever. The house ran on his father's income from the land and whatever scraps Ramfal picked up, and everyone knew who was actually holding that family together. Quietly. The way it's always the women who do it, in villages like theirs. Her days, from what little he could piece together over those borrowed minutes in a courtyard, ran the same narrow track most days in that house did — water before sunrise, the cattle, the cooking, a father-in-law who spoke to her only to issue instructions, a husband who slept through the afternoons he wasn't drinking through. She never once complained of it directly. She didn't need to. He read it in the quiet economy of her movements, a woman who had learned to waste nothing, not water, not time, not a single unnecessary word, because nothing in that house was hers to waste.<\/p>\n<p>There was an afternoon, a few months in, when Ramfal was drunk in the daytime — not rare — and the father-in-law had gone to the fields. Vikram arrived with a paper that could have waited a week. She met him at the gate instead of the courtyard.<\/p>\n<p>\"He's inside,\" she said. \"Sleeping. Don't go in.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I only came to—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know why you came.\"<\/p>\n<p>She did not step aside. He did not ask her to.<\/p>\n<p>\"If I come in for tea,\" he said, very low, \"what happens?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Tea happens,\" she said. \"And then you leave. The way you always leave. And I wash the cup. The way I always wash the cup.\" A pause. \"Unless you don't want tea.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I want tea.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then come.\"<\/p>\n<p>In the courtyard she poured with a hand that was not entirely steady. When he stood to go, she hadn't stepped back — close enough now that he could feel the warmth radiating off her skin, a single step separating them where there should have been three. For a second his palm hovered near her waist, close enough to feel heat through cotton without contact. She inhaled — sharp, small — as if he had already touched her.<\/p>\n<p>\"Vikram.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know,\" he said.<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't.\" Soft. Almost angry. \"You think you do. You leave. I stay. That is the whole of what you know.\"<\/p>\n<p>He left. At the gate he looked back, and something in him understood, plainly, that a decent man would have stopped coming for tea that same afternoon, and that he was not, on this one matter, going to manage being a decent man. He thought of his own father's name in the village, the quiet respectability of it, and told himself the visits would stop on their own. They did not stop.<\/p>\n<p>His leave ended not long after that, and he went back to his unit. Two months later, a letter from his mother mentioned it in passing — Ramfal's wife had joined the police, constable training, of all things. He read the line twice, then a third time, and told himself, quite firmly, that it didn't matter to him either way. He would not have to wait long to discover exactly how wrong he was.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 3, "title": "Chapter 2 — Kavita's Constable Days", "content": "<h1>Chapter 2 — Kavita's Constable Days<\/h1>\n<p>It was somewhere in the second month of training that his phone rang with a wrong number that wasn't quite a wrong number.<\/p>\n<p>\"Hello?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Sorry — wrong number, I think.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It's fine. Who were you calling?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"No one. No one in particular.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Kavita?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"…Yes. It's me.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't know yet that it wasn't a wrong number at all. He was posted in Srinagar by then, and something in the plain ordinariness of a wrong-numbered voice made it easy, at first, to keep talking past the point where either of them should have hung up.<\/p>\n<p>\"You sound tired,\" he said, on the second call.<\/p>\n<p>\"Training does that. Up before five, running until my legs stop arguing about it, an instructor who enjoys shouting rather more than strictly necessary.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Do you like it?\"<\/p>\n<p>She considered the question longer than it needed. \"More than I expected to. There's a version of me here nobody in my village has ever met. I think I like her better.\"<\/p>\n<p>He asked, over the calls that followed, about the training itself — the drills, the discipline, the particular loneliness and freedom of a barracks full of women learning to want things for themselves. She told him more than she'd told anyone, including Naina. He listened the way he listened to very little else in his own life — simply taking in the shape of a woman quietly rebuilding herself.<\/p>\n<p>He was not married. She was. That fact sat under everything that came after — every risk, every consequence was always going to land on her first, not him. He knew that plainly, even then, and it did nothing to slow him down.<\/p>\n<p>What she was actually describing to him, call after call, was a life he'd never once seen for himself — the Haryana Police Academy at Madhuban, just outside Karnal, nothing like the courtyard she'd left behind.<\/p>\n<p>Concrete barracks instead of her in-laws' courtyard house. A steel cot instead of the cot she'd shared arguments with Ramfal over. A whistle at five in the morning instead of the sound of her mother-in-law's bangles moving through the kitchen before dawn.<\/p>\n<p>It didn't feel like punishment. It felt, for the first time in years, like breathing.<\/p>\n<p>She noticed it in small things — waking without needing to check if anyone else in the house was up first, eating without calculating who else needed to eat before her. Something in her chest that had been quietly clenched began, slowly, to unclench. She slept without dreaming of the courtyard, and hadn't realised, until it stopped, that she'd been dreaming of it at all.<\/p>\n<p>She was not the fastest in her batch, nor the strongest. But she was disciplined in a way the instructors noticed within the first week — a woman who did not need to be told twice, who ran the extra lap without being asked. There was a version of her that had existed only in a village courtyard, holding a tray a moment too long. Here, that version had nowhere to stand. A new one had to be built, and she built it deliberately.<\/p>\n<p>Naina noticed her before Kavita noticed Naina.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're married,\" Naina said one evening, dropping onto the cot beside hers without asking. It wasn't unkind. It was simply bluntness.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm aware,\" Kavita said.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't mean it as an insult. I mean you run like someone who's already decided this matters more than it does for the rest of us. Most of these girls are here because their fathers pushed them. You run like you're here because you chose it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I did choose it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then you're the only one,\" Naina said, and grinned, and that was more or less the beginning of it.<\/p>\n<p>They were an unlikely pair — Naina from a family with enough money that she didn't strictly need the uniform, sharp-tongued, impatient with anyone she considered slow. Kavita from a village, newly married, careful with money and words both, a woman who had learned to say everything with her eyes because her mouth had never been allowed the practice. Naina said the things Kavita had spent a lifetime not saying, and Kavita gave Naina's restlessness somewhere to land.<\/p>\n<p>They shared a room for the better part of a year — bunks reassigned and shuffled until an instructor gave up trying to separate them.<\/p>\n<p>Evenings settled into a rhythm — Naina complaining about whichever instructor had wronged her, Kavita mending a strap or oiling her boots, offering a dry line that made Naina laugh harder than the joke deserved.<\/p>\n<p>Once a week, if duties allowed it, they split a plate of hot samosas at the canteen they weren't supposed to be eating at, arguing over nothing in particular. It was, Kavita would think later, the first friendship in her adult life that had cost her nothing to maintain — no tray to carry into it, no performance to keep up for anyone's benefit but her own.<\/p>\n<p>It was in that room, late one night, that Kavita first told her about Ramfal. Not everything. Enough.<\/p>\n<p>\"He calls when he wants money,\" she said, staring at the ceiling. \"Or when he wants me to hear him breathe and remember I belong to someone.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina didn't offer sympathy. \"Then don't answer.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It's not that simple.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It's exactly that simple. You've just been trained your whole life to think it isn't.\"<\/p>\n<p>Kavita hadn't argued. She'd only turned the words over for a long time after, and something about them had stayed lodged in her the way the right sentence sometimes does — not agreed with yet, but never fully forgotten either.<\/p>\n<p>It was Naina who first noticed the odd quiet that came over Kavita whenever a certain district near Srinagar came up in conversation — a stillness that didn't match her usual bluntness.<\/p>\n<p>\"There's someone,\" Naina said one night. Not a question.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita didn't deny it, which was answer enough. \"Fine,\" Naina said. \"Keep your secrets.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"There isn't much to pry into,\" Kavita said eventually. \"A boy from our village. Army. Posted somewhere near Srinagar, last I heard. That's all.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's all,\" Naina repeated, unconvinced, but let it go.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita never said his name that night, and would not for a long time after.<\/p>\n<p>By then, weeks into the calls, something had shifted — she knew the shape of his silences now, the dryness of his humour, the way he listened rather than waited for his turn to speak. The calls stopped needing an excuse. By the third week, she stopped pretending the dial was an accident.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're free at eight,\" she said. Not a question.<\/p>\n<p>\"I am now.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Good. I hate waiting for a man who might not answer.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll answer.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"See that you do, Major Saab.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was still only a Captain then, a rank short of the one she insisted on using. It had started as a joke back in the courtyard days — she'd heard someone address him with the wrong rank by mistake and decided she liked it better. He let it stand, and it became her way of speaking to the man she'd decided he already was.<\/p>\n<p>The rank in her mouth was not respect. It was heat dressed as mockery, and he felt it in his stomach, glad she couldn't see his face.<\/p>\n<p>\"He calls when he wants money,\" she said once, of Ramfal. \"Or when he wants me to hear him breathe and remember I belong somewhere. I let it ring.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Kavita.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't. Don't say my name like you're sorry for me. I'm not calling you for sorry.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Why are you calling me?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Because I put tea in your hand for so long, and you looked at me like a man who was starving and too well-trained to eat. I wanted to know if the training held when there was no courtyard and no tray.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It doesn't,\" he said.<\/p>\n<p>\"Good.\"<\/p>\n<p>The breaking did not happen in one night. It happened call by call, each one running longer than the last, each one taking a little more of the polite lie that this was only friendship. Somewhere in the third week he understood that he had stopped being able to call this ordinary.<\/p>\n<p>\"I think about you more than I have any right to,\" she said once, her voice dropping into something quieter than he'd heard from her before. \"You can say a thing like that plainly, Vikram. A married woman doesn't get to.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then don't say it,\" he said, though he didn't mean it, and they both knew he didn't.<\/p>\n<p>\"I won't,\" she said. \"Not the rest of it, anyway.\"<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them needed the rest of it said. The silence that followed carried it for them, and both understood exactly what it meant.<\/p>\n<p>They did not meet, not yet, for months of this. The calls alone were enough to unsettle something in both of them that neither had the courage, yet, to name.<\/p>\n<p>Afterward, more than once, he lay awake turning over what kind of man this made him — a family whose name he still carried through every posting, and a married woman telling him things his own mother would not have survived hearing. He told himself he would stop. He never quite managed it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm coming to Gurugram,\" she told him, a few weeks later. She wanted to see him first, before going home to Ramfal's village. He told her he couldn't get leave.<\/p>\n<p>He sat with the lie for the better part of a day, the way a man sits with a wound he's not ready to look at. What it would cost him, professionally. What it would cost her, far more, if their village ever caught even the shadow of this. And what it said about him that neither cost was enough to make him say no properly.<\/p>\n<p>He thought of his father, and the name their family had spent two generations keeping clean in that village. He thought, briefly and without much conviction, of simply not going.<\/p>\n<p>She called back two days later, and asked again, and this time he didn't tell himself anything at all.<\/p>\n<p>So he went.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 4, "title": "Chapter 3 — Gurugram", "content": "<h1>Chapter 3 — Gurugram<\/h1>\n<p>He reached the evening before, telling himself he was going only to end it — to say out loud what distance had made too easy to avoid saying. He was young enough still to believe a man could simply decide a thing was over and have the deciding be enough. He had rehearsed the words on the ride down — a few sentences, clean and final, the kind of thing a disciplined man says once and doesn't repeat. He told himself the trip itself was proof of his own resolve: he was going precisely so he could look her in the eye and close the door properly, rather than let it drift shut the cowardly way, over months of unanswered calls. It would be a long time before he could laugh at how obviously untrue that had been, even as he was telling it to himself on the bike — that underneath the rehearsed sentences, some quieter, more honest part of him had already packed an overnight bag.<\/p>\n<p>She called that night — said she'd be on the Haryana Roadways bus leaving HPA, Madhuban, Karnal at eight, which would put her at the Gurugram bus stand by eleven. He was there early, waiting at the Gurugram bus stand, trying to look like a man with no particular reason to be standing there.<\/p>\n<p>She hadn't needed much of an excuse to leave — training kept her schedule unpredictable enough that a day away, even overnight, rarely drew questions from anyone back home. She'd simply mentioned an errand connected to her training, vague enough to discourage follow-up questions, and left it at that.<\/p>\n<p>She had sat on the bus turning the risk over the whole way — what it would cost her if seen, if recognised, if someone from the village happened to be travelling the same road. She made her peace with it somewhere around the second hour, the way she'd made her peace with most things that mattered to her: only once she'd already decided to do them anyway.<\/p>\n<p>She stepped off in a plain salwar-kameez. No uniform. No jewellery that could mark her as anyone in particular. Dupatta pulled close more out of habit than any real need against the mild morning air. But those eyes found him before he'd even fully seen her — blue-grey in the morning glare — and every line he'd rehearsed on the ride over just went.<\/p>\n<p>His hand found hers as she climbed onto the back of his bike. Their fingers touched for the first time since they'd known each other. Skin against skin. Nothing between them at all, for the first time.<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them said a word about it. They didn't need to. Her fingertips were cool from the metal. His were not. The contact lasted less than a second and it went through him the way a current does when you touch a live wire you thought was dead — quick, complete, no room left for the story he'd been telling himself about endings.<\/p>\n<p>The hotel — Skylark, in Sector 14, barely a kilometre from the bus stand — was decent, not extravagant, but proper, air conditioning, clean sheets, the kind of place with a lobby you wouldn't feel embarrassed walking through. He'd chosen it carefully. He wasn't going to bring her somewhere that made the whole thing feel cheap, whatever else it was. They checked in under a name that wasn't quite hers. Cash. A few polite questions at the desk, nothing more. The lift smelled of disinfectant and someone else's perfume.<\/p>\n<p>The door shut behind them with a soft hotel click. Curtains half-drawn. Afternoon light cut into a hard bar across the carpet.<\/p>\n<p>Outside, a row of neem and amaltas trees lining the hotel's boundary wall had entered their own leaf-fall, old leaves letting go of their branches one at a time in the mild March wind. A dry, papery rustle drifted up through the half-open window along with the ordinary noise of the road — the particular sound of a season quietly finishing itself before anyone had properly noticed it was ending, new growth still weeks away and entirely invisible yet on every bare branch outside.<\/p>\n<p>The room still carried something of the morning's chill in its corners, not yet fully warmed. Neither of them reached to adjust it, too caught up in all the looking and not touching that had finally, entirely, run out of road.<\/p>\n<p>\"I came to end it,\" he said. His voice sounded wrong in the quiet. Too loud. Too thin. She looked at him. The dupatta had slipped. A dark strand of hair stuck loose against the side of her neck, come free somewhere on the ride over.<\/p>\n<p>\"I know,\" she said. \"I came anyway.\"<\/p>\n<p>He crossed the space between them. Not fast. Not slow enough to be called careful. He reached for her gently, feeling her steady herself against him, and brought his forehead down to hers, their breath mixing in the narrow space between them.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere beneath the certainty he was projecting, his own pulse was doing something he hadn't expected it to do. He had never actually done this before — not truly, not the whole of it, whatever quiet reputation two years in uniform and an easy confidence with people had built around him in her imagination.<\/p>\n<p>He'd come close, once or twice, in the ordinary way young men do, and had always found some reason, at the final threshold, to pull back. Nothing he could ever fully explain to himself — only a sense that it hadn't yet been Kavita. Not the room, not the moment, just her, specifically, the one absence he hadn't known how to name until she was standing in front of him.<\/p>\n<p>He hadn't planned to think about that now, standing here. It arrived anyway, a single sharp thought before his body took over from his mind entirely: this is his first time, and it is her.<\/p>\n<p>Then, at last, she closed the distance between them, and there was nothing gentle in it — a tray held a moment too long, looks that had never finished their sentence, eight o'clock calls that had pretended, badly, to be accidents. All of it surfaced at once in that first kiss, like something held underwater finally allowed to break the surface. One kiss. No going back from it now.<\/p>\n<p>\"Lock the door,\" she said, and he did, and when he turned back to her there was no more hesitation left in either of them.<\/p>\n<p>Her hands found him, trembling slightly, unsure where to begin — she had imagined this moment so many times that she'd expected her hands to know exactly what to do, and instead they fumbled, and she found she didn't mind the fumbling at all. Her dupatta slid from her shoulders and pooled on the floor, forgotten, and for a moment they simply stood like that, foreheads nearly touching, both of them breathing as though they'd been running.<\/p>\n<p>\"Vikram,\" she said. Just his name. Nothing needed adding to it.<\/p>\n<p>They came together, unhurried despite how long they'd waited for this, the room going quiet around them in the particular way a room does when two people stop needing words for a while. Whatever passed between them after that was slow, and unpractised in places, and entirely theirs — everything unsaid finally finding somewhere to go.<\/p>\n<p>She would think of it, long after, not as any single moment but as the whole shape of it: his attention, unbroken, the same attention that had once watched her linger too long over a cup of tea, now turned fully on her. And the quiet certainty in both of them that this, finally, was real — not something either of them had only imagined.<\/p>\n<p>Afterward, they lay together for a long while, her head on his chest, his hand resting against the slow rise and fall of her breathing, neither of them in any hurry to say anything at all.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're a wildfire, Kavita,\" he said eventually, something almost dazed still sitting in his voice. \"I don't think I've ever felt anything quite like that.\"<\/p>\n<p>She laughed, a real laugh, propping herself up on one elbow to look at him properly, something delighted and faintly proud in her expression. \"A wildfire. I'll take that.\" She traced a slow line down his chest, still laughing softly. \"All that holding a tea tray a moment too long, and this is what finally comes out of me. I should have set myself loose on you months ago.\"<\/p>\n<p>Later, as the evening light turned gold across the room, they found their way back to each other once more, slower this time, and lay afterward laughing quietly for reasons neither of them could have explained to anyone — not a husband, not a court, not three friends holding whisky glasses in a room years from now.<\/p>\n<p>\"I keep waiting to feel guilty,\" she said quietly, into the silence. \"It hasn't come yet.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Maybe it doesn't come for the parts that were always true.\"<\/p>\n<p>She lifted her head and looked at him — really looked, the way she used to look over a cup of tea, except now there was nothing left unfinished in it. \"All this time,\" she said. \"I used to think I was imagining the way you looked at me. I used to lie awake telling myself a married woman doesn't get to want the things I wanted.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You weren't imagining it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know that now.\" She traced a slow line along his collarbone, not with hunger this time, just with the quiet wonder of someone allowed, finally, to touch something she had only ever been permitted to serve tea beside. \"I don't know what we do with this, Vikram. I only know I'm not sorry I came.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Do you remember,\" she said, after a moment, \"telling you once, in the courtyard, that I used to imagine what kind of husband I wanted, as a girl?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I remember.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I told you I hadn't gotten him yet. That wasn't entirely true.\" She propped herself up slightly to look at him properly. \"The first time I actually saw you — really saw you, not just as Ramfal's occasional visitor — some part of me thought, without meaning to, there. That's him. That's the husband I used to imagine, standing right there in my own courtyard, a year too late to actually be mine.\" She laughed softly, though there was nothing light in it. \"I told myself it was foolish. A married woman doesn't get to want things like that. But I wanted it anyway, Vikram. I've wanted it since that very first afternoon.\"<\/p>\n<p>They stayed the whole afternoon and into the evening, talking more than either of them had expected to — her childhood, a sister she barely spoke to anymore, his years at the academy, the loneliness of a posting where the cold made even friendship feel like an effort. It was, in its way, more intimate than anything that had happened between them that day: two people who had spent all this time saying everything except the truth, finally saying all of it, plainly, with nothing left to protect.<\/p>\n<p>They ate that first evening in the hotel's own restaurant, one floor down — red tablecloths a shade too formal for the rest of the building, a waiter who refilled their water without being asked and otherwise left them entirely alone. Shahi paneer and kadhai paneer arrived in silver bowls too large for two people to finish, more than half of it eventually going back to the kitchen untouched. Their hands brushed once under the table and neither pulled away at once. When the bill came, she'd already paid it before he could reach for his wallet, and wouldn't hear a word of protest.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere past ten, she stepped away to call Ramfal, her voice dropping into the flat, practical register she used for exactly this kind of conversation — training kept her schedule unpredictable enough that nobody back home ever quite knew which day to expect her, and she'd learned, over the months, how little explanation that particular uncertainty actually required. She told him simply that she'd be back by noon the next day, and he took it the way he took most things about her life now — without question, without much interest either. She came back to the bed afterward, the whole exchange already fading behind her, and neither of them spoke of it again that night. There was no rush left in either of them now, no clock counting down to a bus stand. They fell asleep tangled together sometime past midnight, her head on his chest, and woke slowly, unhurried, to a city already loud outside the window.<\/p>\n<p>She left the following morning, back on a bus, back toward Ramfal's house, back into a life that had no idea what had happened in a room outside Gurugram.<\/p>\n<p>The bus ride back felt longer than the one that had brought her, though the distance was exactly the same. Outside, the same bare neem and amaltas branches passed by in a blur, their fallen leaves gathering in dry drifts along the roadside, the evening light thinning toward the particular gold-grey of a March dusk — a season quietly emptying itself out even as, somewhere inside her, something else had just as quietly begun.<\/p>\n<p>She sat with her forehead against the cool glass, watching the fields blur past, and tried to locate the guilt she'd told him hadn't come yet. She tested for it the way you test a tooth with your tongue, expecting pain and finding, instead, only a strange, unfamiliar quiet.<\/p>\n<p>It wasn't guilt that finally arrived somewhere past the halfway mark. It was something closer to grief, though she couldn't yet name what exactly she was grieving — perhaps only the knowledge that she now knew, with total certainty, the precise shape of everything her marriage was never going to give her. And that she would have to walk back into that house that evening and set the table anyway, and lie beside a husband who never once asked her the kind of question that would require her to lie about anything that actually mattered.<\/p>\n<p>She did not yet know, watching those bare branches thin out along the highway, that leaf-fall always ends the same way it had for centuries before her — new leaves arriving, sooner or later, on every tree that looked finished. She would understand only much later, looking back, that whatever had felt like an ending that particular March afternoon had actually been the opposite of one.<\/p>\n<p>By the time the bus reached her stop, she had folded the whole day away somewhere private, the way she'd learned to fold most things, and stepped down into the dust of her own lane with her face arranged into its ordinary shape, a woman coming home from an errand, nothing more.<\/p>\n<p>But something had already changed, and both of them knew that much, even unsaid. His back still carried the thin crescents of her nails under his shirt for days. When he showered in the officers' quarters he saw them in the mirror and stood there longer than he needed to, water running cold, remembering the exact weight of her on top of him when the light had gone gold.<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them said the word <i>again<\/i> out loud. Both of them were already certain there would be one.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 5, "title": "Chapter 4 — The Dhaba on the Highway", "content": "<h1>Chapter 4 — The Dhaba on the Highway<\/h1>\n<p>Vikram picked her up from the Gurugram bus stand again three days later, on a morning she'd engineered with the same practised ease as the last. This time there was no hesitation at all in how naturally she climbed on behind him, no weight in the gesture beyond what it plainly was — a ride back to the academy at Madhuban, offered the evening before over a phone call she'd made specifically to ask for it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to feel the road for once,\" she'd said on that call, \"instead of sitting through it in a bus full of strangers and someone's crying baby. Take me back yourself.\"<\/p>\n<p>He hadn't argued. He'd simply told her what time to be at the stand.<\/p>\n<p>The ride out of Gurugram took the better part of an hour before the city properly thinned into open highway, and by the time they crossed toward Sonipat, both their stomachs had started making their own quiet case for stopping. Amrik Sukhdev Dhaba sat just off the highway at Murthal, near Sonipat, unmissable even from a distance — strings of lights along its frontage despite the daylight, a parking lot already crowded with private cars and families waiting for a table, the reputation of a place that had long since outgrown the word \"dhaba\" in everything but name. Inside, it ran closer to a proper restaurant — clean tables, uniformed staff, a tandoor visible through a service window where two men worked without pause, pulling out paratha after paratha, each one slapped with a generous knob of butter that melted into its layers before it ever reached a plate.<\/p>\n<p>\"You've been here before,\" Kavita said, settling into her chair, watching him order without needing to check a menu.<\/p>\n<p>\"Every trip through Sonipat, if I can manage it. Their paratha's worth the detour.\"<\/p>\n<p>It arrived exactly as promised — thick, layered, butter pooling at its edges, alongside a bowl of curd and a pickle so sharp it made Kavita's eyes water on the first bite, laughing at herself even as she reached for water.<\/p>\n<p>\"This paratha is delicious,\" she said. \"I'm going to want it every time now, and you won't always be the one driving me back.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then I'll make sure I am.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something in her face shifted at that — not quite the closing-door look he'd learned to recognise, but close to it, a woman deciding, in real time, how much of a promise like that she was allowed to let herself want.<\/p>\n<p>They ate slowly, unhurried despite the miles still ahead, talking about nothing that mattered — the paratha, whether the coming summer would be as brutal as the last one, a family at the next table arguing cheerfully over how many plates to order. It was, Vikram thought, watching her wipe a smear of butter from the corner of her mouth without any self-consciousness at all, the closest thing to ordinary either of them had ever been permitted.<\/p>\n<p>\"I like this,\" Kavita said eventually, quietly, into the noise of the dhaba around them. \"Not just you. This. Being somewhere no one is watching to see if I sit too close or laugh too loud.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You could have this,\" Vikram said. \"Not just today.\"<\/p>\n<p>He heard the words land before he'd fully decided to say them, and felt his own pulse catch at the sound of them out loud — not regret exactly, but the unease of a man who has just admitted, however carelessly, to wanting a future he has no right yet to be imagining with this specific woman.<\/p>\n<p>Something closed in her face, gently, the way a door closes when someone doesn't want a draft rather than because they want to keep anyone out.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't,\" she said. \"Not today. Today I only want the paratha and the road and you sitting across from me pretending you didn't hear yourself say that.\"<\/p>\n<p>He let it go.<\/p>\n<p>When the bill came, she reached for it before he could.<\/p>\n<p>\"I paid last time,\" he said.<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't. You only think you did because you always try to.\" She counted out the notes with the same brisk competence she brought to everything.<\/p>\n<p>He'd picked her up at noon, and the ride to Madhuban should have taken three hours at a steady pace — a fact Kavita reminded him of, teasingly, when she finally checked the time and realised how long they'd actually lingered over the paratha and the argument about the bill.<\/p>\n<p>\"We've already lost an hour just sitting here,\" she said, though she made no move to hurry either of them along.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere past the dhaba, the wind had gentled into something slower, almost lazy, drifting along the open highway lined on both sides with tall, broad-crowned safeda trees. Every other tree they'd passed that season had already given up its leaves to the ground, bare branches standing stark against the sky the whole length of the road. The safeda alone had kept every leaf it owned, green and full and entirely unmoved by whatever season had emptied out everything around it, throwing long, unbroken bands of shade across the road every ten metres as though it simply hadn't been told the year had turned.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita let go of his waist with one hand and stretched her arm out wide into the wind, palm open, fingers spread, head tipped back slightly against the rush of air, laughing at nothing in particular. It was the specific, unguarded joy of a woman who spent every other hour of her life keeping her hands folded properly in her lap, her voice pitched low enough not to draw attention, her whole body trained into a smallness she hadn't chosen.<\/p>\n<p>Out here, with nobody watching, with nothing but road and wind and the man in front of her, she let herself take up exactly as much space as she wanted.<\/p>\n<p>\"What are you doing?\" he called back over the noise of the road.<\/p>\n<p>\"Feeling it properly,\" she said. \"The whole road, not just holding on. Just this once.\"<\/p>\n<p>She kept her arm out a long moment, the wind pushing against her open palm, the safeda trees flickering shadow and sunlight over her in quick, rhythmic bands as they passed beneath one after another. Finally, slowly, she drew it back — not out of caution, but because some part of her wanted, just as badly, to hold onto him again.<\/p>\n<p>She wrapped both arms fully around his waist this time, pulling herself closer against his back than the ride strictly required, her cheek resting between his shoulder blades. She stayed like that for the rest of the way — one hand still tingling faintly from the wind, the other pressed flat against his chest, feeling his heartbeat keep its own steady rhythm beneath her palm.<\/p>\n<p>They walked back out to the bike, Madhuban still nearly two hours further up the highway, and Kavita paused a moment before climbing back on.<\/p>\n<p>\"Same road next time I get leave?\" she asked, lightly, as if it were a joke, though something in the way she held perfectly still, waiting for his answer, told him it wasn't a joke at all.<\/p>\n<p>\"Same road,\" he said, and meant it as something closer to a promise than either of them was in any position to make properly.<\/p>\n<p>They didn't reach Madhuban until nearly six that evening, three hours later than the ride alone should have taken — unhurried stops, a slower pace than either of them strictly needed to keep, neither of them in any real rush to end a day that felt, for once, entirely their own.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 6, "title": "Chapter 5 — Her House, His Risk", "content": "<h1>Chapter 5 — Her House, His Risk<\/h1>\n<p>It was on his next leave, months after that ride to Madhuban, that the opportunity found its way to him. Ramfal had gone to Panipat for three days — a cousin's land dispute that needed his presence, or at least his signature, and either way it meant the house would be empty of him for the first time in months.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita told Vikram this over the phone with no particular emphasis, the way she told him most dangerous things — plainly, as fact, and let him decide what to do with the fact himself.<\/p>\n<p>He came the second night. Not the first — the first would have looked deliberate to anyone paying attention, and someone was always paying attention in a village that size, even at hours when most of it was asleep. The second night, well past ten, when even the last dogs had stopped barking, he left his own house on foot rather than the bike, the engine noise too much of a risk for that hour, and walked the long way round through the fields to reach her gate without passing a single other house on the way.<\/p>\n<p>He walked fast, faster than the risk strictly required, and somewhere around the halfway point he caught himself almost smiling into the dark, aware of exactly how reckless this was and unable, for the life of him, to make the recklessness feel like a problem. He had walked into ambush country in Srinagar with a steadier heart than this — had moved through terrain thick with men who wanted him dead and felt nothing close to this particular trembling; some fears, it seemed, only a woman's gate could teach a soldier. What are you doing, he asked himself once, plainly, the way a disciplined man is supposed to ask himself such questions — and got no honest answer back except the plain fact of his own pulse, quick and eager, counting down the fields between himself and her gate.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita had spent the whole of that second day in a state she didn't have a proper word for — not nervousness exactly, though her hands had been unsteady twice while she cooked, but something closer to a low, constant electricity. She was a woman counting down hours toward something she wanted and was finally, actually, about to have again — this time in her own house, instead of a rented room one hour away.<\/p>\n<p>She'd caught herself smiling at nothing at least three times before evening, and had to school her face back into its ordinary shape each time, in case the father-in-law's absence made anyone in the lane more observant than usual.<\/p>\n<p>Then, finally, his footsteps at the gate. No knock. No need for one.<\/p>\n<p>\"You walked,\" Kavita said, opening the door before he'd even knocked, as if she'd been standing just inside it for an hour already.<\/p>\n<p>\"The bike would have woken half the lane.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It would have woken all of it. Come in before someone's dog decides to notice you standing there.\"<\/p>\n<p>The courtyard was different at night without the weight of the father-in-law's presence somewhere in the house — lighter, somehow, though nothing in it had physically changed. A motorcycle stood covered under a tarp in one corner, Ramfal's, unused these three days.<\/p>\n<p>Inside, the house held the hush of a home built for more people than currently lived in it: proper cemented flooring, a tube light in the main room switched off in favour of a softer bulb, its yellow light spilling weakly through the doorway onto the courtyard, throwing long shadows from the furniture. A wooden sofa set with faded upholstery, a steel almirah in one corner, a small television on a stand covered with a cloth, a calendar from some local shop pinned crookedly to the wall.<\/p>\n<p>\"He's not due back until tomorrow evening,\" Kavita said, latching the door quietly behind him. \"I checked twice.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't need to tell me that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I wanted you to know exactly how much risk we're taking, and exactly how much thought I put into taking it anyway.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"How long can I stay?\"<\/p>\n<p>She looked at him steadily, and something in her face settled into a decision she'd clearly already made before he'd even arrived.<\/p>\n<p>\"Until before the sun,\" she said. \"Not a minute longer. The first cart goes out to the fields before it's fully light, and I won't have you seen leaving this gate in daylight for anyone with eyes to make something of it.\" She reached out and took his hand, the first time she'd touched him since he'd stepped through the door. \"But until then — the whole night is ours. Here, in my own house, with my own walls around us instead of a hotel we had to leave money and a false name for. I want to know what that feels like.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't wait for an answer. She led him by the hand through the inner room, past the bed she shared arguments with Ramfal over more often than anything else, into the smaller back room that had once been her own before marriage folded her into someone else's house entirely, and shut the door behind them with a quiet, deliberate click.<\/p>\n<p>The bulb in that room had gone weak, throwing more shadow than light, and neither of them minded. She turned to him the moment the door was shut, and there was none of the courtyard's careful patience left in her — only the same hunger Gurugram had first let loose in both of them, now finding a second night to live in. She closed the gap between them herself, and whatever caution either of them had carried through the courtyard and the doorway simply fell away.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've thought about Gurugram every single night since,\" she said, her forehead resting against his. \"Every night, Vikram. In this house. With him three feet away some nights, and I still thought about you.\"<\/p>\n<p>He pulled her closer, his own composure fraying just as badly as hers. \"Tell me the rest of it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I will. Later. Right now I don't want words.\" She drew him closer instead, and something in the way she held him, unguarded and entirely without shame, went through him like heat finding dry grass.<\/p>\n<p>They came together on the narrow bed with none of the caution the danger around them should have demanded, both of them twenty-two and certain, the way only that age can be certain, that nothing outside this room mattered as much as what was happening inside it. He took his time with her that night, unhurried despite the urgency underneath it — everything about her already familiar to him from Gurugram and somehow still worth learning all over again, slower this time, with no clock counting down to a bus or a return journey.<\/p>\n<p>She was freer in her own house despite the greater risk, as though the familiarity of these walls gave her permission the rented room never quite had. For a long while neither of them needed anything from the world beyond that small, weak-lit room — two people who already knew the shape of this and had missed it anyway.<\/p>\n<p>They didn't sleep for a long while after, tangled together on the narrow bed, her fingers tracing idle patterns against his chest while they talked in low voices about nothing that mattered and everything that did. Somewhere past midnight they found each other again, slower this time, quieter, the urgency of the first round settling into something deeper and more deliberate, and it was well past one by the time exhaustion finally claimed them both.<\/p>\n<p>She did fall asleep eventually, somewhere past two, her head against his chest, one hand still loosely holding his even in sleep. Vikram lay awake a long while after, listening to a night he had no business being part of settle into its ordinary sounds — a dog somewhere, the wind moving through the neem tree outside, her breathing slow and even against him.<\/p>\n<p>There was something almost unbearable in watching her like this — how unguarded she looked, all the careful control of her waking hours simply absent, her face younger somehow in the weak bulb-light, the sharpness gone soft. He found himself thinking, watching her, that this — the watching, the trust of it — was the thing he would remember longest about that night, more than anything they'd done in the hours before it.<\/p>\n<p>He woke her himself, gently, when the sky outside the small window had only just begun to grey.<\/p>\n<p>\"It's time,\" he said quietly.<\/p>\n<p>She woke quickly, straight into full awareness within a breath. \"Already?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"The sky's starting to change. I promised you before the sun, and I meant it.\"<\/p>\n<p>At the door, she caught his sleeve before he could step past her into the dark. \"Vikram.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Yes.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Thank you. For staying the whole night, here, in this house, instead of somewhere we had to pay for and leave.\" Her voice had dropped, quieter than he'd heard it in a while. \"I don't think you understand yet what that meant to me. Every morning of this marriage, I wake up to a husband who's already turned away, or already gone, or still drunk from the night before and I'm the one who has to decide whether to wake him. Waking up in my own house, in my own bed, beside someone who wanted to be there — I don't think I'll ever stop being grateful for that, even if it's only ever going to be a few hours at a time.\"<\/p>\n<p>He kissed her forehead, once, careful and brief. \"Then I'm glad I could give you that much.\"<\/p>\n<p>One kiss. One promise, unspoken but understood by both of them.<\/p>\n<p>He left through the fields the way he'd come, the sky lightening behind him with every step. He didn't look back at her gate. He didn't need to. He could still feel the weight of her sleeping against his chest, and it stayed with him the rest of that day, and for a good many days after.<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them said it aloud that morning, standing at the door with the sky already turning grey. But both of them had already started counting toward the next time Ramfal's absence would open a door they had no business walking through.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 7, "title": "Chapter 6 — The Mustard Fields", "content": "<h1>Chapter 6 — The Mustard Fields<\/h1>\n<p>Vikram came home from 23 RR the way winter always seemed to send him back — four months gone, ten days sanctioned this time. Already, before the first evening was even over, some quiet accountant in him had begun rationing the days out, counting how many he could spend elsewhere before his own family's questions found their way to him.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita called two days in. \"I'm not going to Ramfal's this time,\" she said, no preamble, the flatness in her voice telling him the decision had been made long before she'd dialled. \"I've told them I need rest, that the winter's been hard on me. I'm at my mother's instead. If you want to see me, it has to be here.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Isn't that riskier?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Everything about this is risky, Vikram. My own village at least knows which risks to expect from me by now. Just come.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her mother's house sat in Kosli, nearly seventy kilometres off, the better part of two hours by road, far enough that he took the car instead of the bike. He told his own family only that he was driving out to meet an old course-mate — one more small, practised lie laid quietly onto a pile neither of them had ever properly counted.<\/p>\n<p>Winter turned the fields gold before it turned them anything else that year — mustard in full, uneven bloom, stretching toward a horizon gone pale and thin with cold. Mornings arrived wrapped in a thick, low fog that sat over the fields until well past nine, swallowing the far tree line whole and turning the nearer stalks into soft, indistinct shapes. The sun finally burned through around mid-morning, revealing the whole land dressed in a colour it would only wear for a handful of weeks. Woodsmoke drifted low over the rooftops from early cooking fires, mixing with the fog until the two became indistinguishable, and every breath outdoors came back visible, hanging a moment before dissolving into the general haze.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita had come to her mother's for those few days the way married daughters sometimes did in the slow season, when the fields were still weeks from any real labour and a visiting daughter drew no one's suspicion — a house where nobody watched her comings and goings too closely, a mother more given to her own work than to accounting for her daughter's hours.<\/p>\n<p>Meeting here had been Kavita's idea entirely. She knew this land the way she knew almost nothing else in her life so completely — every furrow, every irrigation channel, every fold in the ground where a person could stand and disappear from the road's line of sight.<\/p>\n<p>\"There,\" she said, pointing toward a slight rise where the mustard grew thick enough to close clean over a person sitting among it, gold folding in on itself like a curtain drawn shut. \"I used to hide there as a girl, whenever I wanted an hour that belonged only to me — away from my mother's calling, away from chores, away from everyone's idea of who I was supposed to be that particular day.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"And now?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Now I'm choosing what I hide from, instead of just needing an hour away from all of it.\"<\/p>\n<p>At the rise, the field opened around them exactly as she'd promised — a small clearing worn soft into the crop, walled on every side by stalks the colour of struck matches, the last thinning wisps of the morning's fog still clinging low between the rows before the sun could burn them off entirely. The morning cold sat close to the ground, sharp enough that their breath hung visible in it, mixing with what remained of the fog until it was hard to tell one from the other, and Kavita drew her shawl tighter before settling in beside him, near enough that their shoulders met, nearer than the cold alone could account for.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're allowed to sit closer than that,\" she said, faintly amused, when he left a polite few inches of winter air between them out of some old instinct that hadn't yet caught up with where they actually were. \"Nobody can see us here. And I'm cold.\"<\/p>\n<p>He closed the distance, and she leaned into him properly then, her head finding his shoulder the way water finds the lowest point of a field. For a while neither of them said anything at all — just the shared warmth of two people trading what little heat the morning offered, the sharp, faintly bitter perfume of mustard flowers pressing thick against the cold air around them.<\/p>\n<p>\"Tell me something you've never told anyone,\" she said, out of nowhere, the way she sometimes did — a question dropped into the quiet the way a stone drops into still water, rings spreading long after the stone itself has vanished.<\/p>\n<p>He considered it a moment. \"I used to think Kashmir would kill something in me. The cold, or the waiting, or the specific shape of the fear up there. It didn't. It only made the parts of me that were already hard, harder still. I don't know, even now, whether that's better or worse than what I expected.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's not a small thing to hand someone.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You asked for something I hadn't told anyone. I gave you the truest one I had left.\"<\/p>\n<p>She fell quiet a while, watching a hawk trace slow, patient circles somewhere above the far tree line, as though it, too, had all the time in the world and nowhere urgent to spend it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I used to think your village would kill something in me too,\" she said eventually. \"The waiting. The tray held a beat too long. A look I never once let myself say out loud.\" She turned to face him properly. \"It didn't kill it. It only taught it patience — the way water doesn't stop wanting to run downhill just because someone's built a wall in front of it; it only learns to wait for rain enough to rise over the top. I don't know if that's better or worse either.\"<\/p>\n<p>He understood, sitting there in the gold-walled quiet, the full weight of what she'd just set down in front of him — not the words themselves, versions of which he'd heard before in other forms, but the ground she'd chosen to say them on. A childhood hiding place, kept secret even from a husband of three years, handed to him now as simply as if he'd always had a right to stand there. He found his hand reaching for hers before he'd properly decided to, and she let him take it, her fingers cold and small inside his, something in his own chest settling quietly at the plain fact of being trusted with a place that had clearly mattered to her long before he'd ever existed in her life at all.<\/p>\n<p>They sat with the silence a while longer, the hawk still circling, the cold still sharp, before Kavita turned toward him properly, her cold fingers finding his jaw and tilting his face gently down to hers. \"Four months,\" she said quietly, the number carrying the whole weight of everything unspent inside it. \"I'm not walking back into that house with this still sitting unfinished in me.\"<\/p>\n<p>They came together in that narrow clearing, the mustard closing over them on every side like something deliberately built to hold two people and nothing else, gold stalks bending and springing back into place around them — four months of distance finding, at last, somewhere urgent to go. Four months. One field. Nothing left standing between them at all.<\/p>\n<p>They sat close afterward, shoulders touching, hands joined loosely in her lap, saying little else, both of them understanding without needing the words for it that some things mattered more held in silence than they ever would have spoken aloud. They stayed until the sun climbed high enough to risk discovery, until the first distant sounds of other people moving through other corners of the field reminded them both, gently but firmly, exactly what they were risking by staying any longer.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 8, "title": "Chapter 7 — The Last Time, Though He Didn't Know It", "content": "<h1>Chapter 7 — The Last Time, Though He Didn't Know It<\/h1>\n<p>The idea had come from Kavita herself, on a call weeks earlier — a rare, unlikely stretch where both their schedules aligned like two hands finally finding the same rhythm, his leave and her own accumulated days off falling into the same window for the first time in longer than either of them could properly remember.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to go somewhere neither of us has to watch the road behind us,\" she said. \"Somewhere far enough that nobody from either of our lives has any reason to be.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram drove the whole way, choosing the longer route through Moradabad's bypass over a main highway that had, that particular year, fallen into such disrepair that most sensible travellers avoided it outright. It was a quieter, narrower road threading through Kashipur, Bazpur, Kaladhungi, agricultural land surrendering slowly to forest as the road climbed — the air changing its texture with every thousand feet of altitude, the plains' thick heat thinning gradually into something crisper and cleaner, carrying the first faint resin-breath of pine long before the trees themselves came into view.<\/p>\n<p>The drive took the better part of a day, both of them stopping twice for tea at roadside stalls built for exactly this kind of traveller, the tea growing sharper and more heavily spiced the higher the road climbed, as though the hills themselves demanded it.<\/p>\n<p>Nainital arrived the way hill towns always seemed to — suddenly, around one final bend, the lake spreading out below them like something poured rather than built, ringed by pine-covered slopes. The air was noticeably thinner and colder than either of them was used to, carrying woodsmoke from somewhere and a mineral cold rising off water that never fully warmed even in the height of summer.<\/p>\n<p>They checked into The Pavilion, a modest hotel on Mall Road close enough to the lake that its sound found their window every morning, the gentle, persistent lap of water against the shore braided together with the calls of unfamiliar birds neither of them could name.<\/p>\n<p>They spent two days doing very little that mattered beyond simply being together without urgency: walking the lake's edge, eating at small restaurants that catered more to tourists than locals, sharing plates of hot momos that steamed in the cold air between them, sitting on their room's narrow balcony each evening wrapped in a single blanket against a chill neither of them had properly packed for. Her hands stayed tucked inside his jacket long after the actual cold had passed, as though the warmth itself had become the point rather than the excuse for it.<\/p>\n<p>It was on their second evening that she asked him, quietly, almost carefully, to order drinks from the small bar downstairs — two large glasses each, green apple vodka, the only cocktail on the menu that had sounded to her more like a fruit than a warning. Not for the room, she said, but for the balcony, where the lake could watch them instead of a stranger's ceiling.<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't drink,\" he said, not quite a question, though something in his voice already understood he was about to learn otherwise.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't,\" she agreed. \"I never have. Not once, in twenty-two years, not at a wedding, not alone, not out of curiosity, not out of grief, though grief has certainly asked me to more than once.\" She looked out at the lake instead of at him, the water gone the colour of pewter in the dying light. \"I've watched what it does to men in my own house my whole life, and told myself young that I would never let it get anywhere near whatever was left of me to protect.\" Something harder crossed her face for a moment, a flicker of old, buried resentment.<\/p>\n<p>\"And then somewhere along the way I started asking myself a different question. If Ramfal can drink himself half-senseless most evenings, on money that started out as mine as much as his, why exactly have I spent this whole marriage denying myself even one evening of the same freedom? I don't know when I stopped having a good answer to that. Tonight, apparently, I've decided I don't need one anymore.\" She turned to him then, and there was something almost apologetic in it now, as if she were confessing to a small betrayal of her own younger self.<\/p>\n<p>\"But I need to say something to you tonight that I don't think I have the courage to say with a completely steady voice. And some part of me has decided, however foolishly, that if I'm going to hand you both truth and courage in the same evening, I should at least be honest about needing help carrying one of them.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't ask her to explain further. He simply went down and came back with four glasses in two careful trips, the pale green of the vodka catching the last of the light the way the lake itself was catching it, gold going slowly to grey.<\/p>\n<p>She drank slowly, the way a person tests unfamiliar water with one foot before committing the rest of themselves to it: a small sip first, a slight cough she tried to disguise as a laugh, her eyes watering briefly before she blinked it away and tried again, steadier the second time, and steadier still by the third.<\/p>\n<p>He watched her the whole while without comment, understanding without being told that this was not indulgence but ceremony — a woman deliberately loosening the careful architecture she'd spent a lifetime building around her own voice, brick by brick, on purpose, because what she needed to say tonight would not survive being spoken by the version of her that kept every door locked.<\/p>\n<p>By the time her first glass sat empty and the second half gone, something in her shoulders had come undone that he had never once seen undone before — not recklessness, nothing so simple as that, but a kind of permission she seemed to be granting herself for the first time in longer than either of them could measure. She laughed once, softly, at nothing in particular, and pressed her cold fingers briefly against her own flushed cheek as though checking that the warmth she felt was real and not merely borrowed.<\/p>\n<p>\"There,\" she said, quiet and a little unsteady, setting the empty glasses down at last on the small table between them. \"Now I can say it properly.\"<\/p>\n<p>They came together that second night the way they always eventually did, unhurried despite the cold, the small room barely warm enough between them and the thin mountain air pressing at the window outside. Afterward, she lay with her head against his chest, quiet for a long while, her fingers tracing slow, absent patterns against his skin, as though writing something she wasn't yet ready to say aloud.<\/p>\n<p>\"This is difficult for me to say, Vikram,\" she said finally, her voice smaller than he was used to hearing it. \"I need you to hear all of it, not just the part that's easiest.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm listening.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not leaving you. I want that clear before anything else. I'll always be with you, in whatever way this life actually allows two people like us to be with each other.\" She fell quiet a moment, gathering the rest of it the way a person gathers something heavy before lifting it properly. \"But I need you to try to move on. Properly. It's time for your marriage, and I mean that as someone concerned for you, not someone trying to push you away.\" Her fingers stilled against his chest, as if even her hands understood the weight of what was coming. \"Until you've actually moved past this — properly, not just in name — I'll still be here. I want you to know that too. It's difficult for me as well, imagining a version of this where I'm not the one you call. But for my own sake, I can't keep doing this to you either. It isn't fair to you, Vikram, and I think some part of you already knows that as well as I do.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Kavita—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"We both already know this has no future. We've met more than fifty times over these two years, Vikram, and I've counted every single one, even the ones neither of us bothered marking on any calendar. We've known where this ends for a while now, I think, and simply never said it out loud in exactly these words.\" No future. Just this. Just the two of them, in a cold room, saying it plainly at last. She lifted her head to look at him properly, her eyes bright in the low light, holding something that was neither quite tears nor quite their absence. \"One more thing, and then I'll stop talking. Whatever else changes — I'll always be your friend. That part isn't negotiable, whatever else this conversation costs either of us.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a long moment, something tightening in his chest that had nothing to do with the mountain cold. Part of him wanted to argue — to tell her she was wrong, that whatever this was could somehow still become something else if only they wanted it badly enough. But some steadier, more honest part of him understood, listening to her, that she'd only said aloud what he'd spent years avoiding admitting to himself.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're right,\" he said finally, his voice rougher than he'd intended. \"I've known it too. I think I've known it since Gurugram, if I'm honest, and simply never let myself sit with it properly until now.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That doesn't make it easier to say.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"No,\" he agreed. \"It doesn't.\" He pulled her closer against him, needing the contact more than he wanted to admit, the way a man reaches for something solid right before the ground under him shifts. \"Thank you for saying it anyway. For both of us, since I clearly wasn't going to.\"<\/p>\n<p>She smiled faintly against his chest, though something in it stayed sad, a smile with its own quiet weather passing behind it. \"Someone had to. I'd rather it be me, saying it with love, than life eventually saying it with cruelty.\"<\/p>\n<p>He held her a long while after that, saying little else, because there was nothing honest left to add that wouldn't simply repeat what she'd already, more bravely than him, said first.<\/p>\n<p>It was the following morning, over breakfast — parathas and curd on a small table overlooking the lake, morning mist still clinging low over the water like something reluctant to leave — before they began the long drive back, that she reached across the table and picked up his phone without asking.<\/p>\n<p>She saved a number into it herself, her fingers moving with a quiet, deliberate certainty, before sliding it back across to him, as though it weighed considerably more than it looked.<\/p>\n<p>\"A girl I trained with,\" she said. \"Naina. From Gurugram, posted back near home now. Sharp-tongued, a little lonely, I think, in the particular way even your own city can feel once you've seen enough of the world outside it.\" She held his gaze a moment, something searching in it. \"I don't know why I'm giving this to you, except that some part of me thinks you deserve someone who isn't always counting the cost of loving you against everything else in her life.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Kavita—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't make this bigger than it is,\" she said, though something in her voice suggested it already was. \"I'm simply handing you a door, in case you ever want to walk through one that isn't attached to a husband and years of careful counting.\"<\/p>\n<p>A faint, tired smile pulled at his mouth despite everything the morning had already cost him. \"So now you're choosing my future wife for me as well? Is there anything you don't take it upon yourself to handle, Kavita?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Someone has to,\" she said, and there was real warmth in it this time, cutting through the weight of everything else. \"You've never once shown good judgment left entirely to yourself.\"<\/p>\n<p>He took the number. He didn't yet understand, taking it, exactly how large a door it actually was.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 9, "title": "Interlude — The File, First Page", "content": "<h1>Interlude — The File, First Page<\/h1>\n<p><i>Years later, the file that had been quietly opened after Vikram's name first crossed a certain desk had grown by exactly one page.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>A woman named Kavita. Two years, roughly, though the file's language was carefully clinical about what that actually meant. Ended without incident, without scandal reaching anyone who mattered, the woman herself later handing him — the file noted this detail with something close to genuine interest — a phone number belonging to someone else entirely, as though closing one door only to point, deliberately, toward the next.<\/p>\n<p>The officer who'd been assigned to keep the file updated made a small note in the margin, nothing more than three words, before closing it again and sliding it back into its drawer.<\/p>\n<p><i>Interesting. Keep watching.<\/i><\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 10, "title": "Chapter 8 — Wrong Number, On Purpose", "content": "<h1>Chapter 8 — Wrong Number, On Purpose<\/h1>\n<p>The number sat in his phone for longer than he'd ever admit to Kavita, and the reason had far less to do with strategy than he liked to tell himself.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita had grown harder to reach in the weeks after handing it to him — not from any quarrel between them, nothing either could have named outright as a problem, but the ordinary, quiet erosion of two lives running along tracks that had simply begun to drift. Some evenings she didn't answer at all, the phone ringing out into whatever version of her evening he no longer belonged to. Other times she picked up only long enough to say she couldn't talk, a minute of rushed, half-present words before the line went dead again, like a door closing before he'd fully stepped through it.<\/p>\n<p>What he didn't know was that a fair share of those unavailable evenings now belonged to someone else entirely — a constable at her own posting named Kamal.<\/p>\n<p>It started, as these things usually did for her, with a tray she barely noticed carrying: an extra flask of tea taken out to the perimeter post on a night colder than the roster strictly required her to endure it, because the constable on duty out there had looked, from a distance, like a man nobody had checked on properly in hours.<\/p>\n<p>Kamal had taken the cup with both hands, grateful in a plain, unguarded way, and said the kind of thing that shouldn't have landed anywhere particular and somehow did.<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't have to walk all the way out here for this.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Nobody else was going to.\" She'd shrugged, already turning to leave.<\/p>\n<p>\"Stay a minute. It's a long shift when there's no one to talk to.\"<\/p>\n<p>She'd stayed longer than a minute, that first night, the two of them talking about nothing important under a sky gone hard and bright with cold stars. She walked back to the barracks afterward by the longer route without either of them quite proposing it out loud — past the old banyan near the gate rather than straight down the main path, five extra minutes neither of them needed and both of them, it turned out, wanted.<\/p>\n<p>It became a habit after that, without either of them naming it one. An escort back some evenings when their shifts happened to overlap, the scenic route quietly established as simply the route they took, no explanation required anymore. Kamal asked questions the way Vikram once had, back in the courtyard, before either of them had a name for what they were building — small, patient, unhurried questions that made a person feel, for the length of an answer, like the only thing worth paying attention to that day.<\/p>\n<p>\"You go very quiet sometimes,\" he said, on one of those walks, \"like you're carrying something you don't set down even when you're standing still.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Everyone in this uniform carries something.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Not like you do.\" He said it simply, without pressing further, and something in her had answered the familiar rhythm of being noticed before she'd fully decided to guard against it.<\/p>\n<p>She told herself it was harmless, mostly true, and didn't examine too closely why her evenings had begun rearranging themselves around his schedule the same patient way they'd once rearranged themselves around Vikram's leave.<\/p>\n<p>Kamal was, physically, not entirely unlike Vikram at first glance — tall in the same easy way, broad through the shoulders, though his skin was not quite as fair as Vikram's, a shade warmer, weathered from years spent posted in open country under an unforgiving sun. Where Vikram's face held a soldier's practiced stillness, Kamal's was sharper-cut, more immediately striking, framed by a stylish, deliberately kept haircut that clearly cost him more attention than most men in that uniform bothered giving their appearance.<\/p>\n<p>It wasn't the face that did the real work, though, however much it helped. It was everything underneath it — an easy, unhurried humour that made the whole posting feel lighter simply for having him in it, the one constable who could make even the duty officer laugh mid-inspection. He was, without exception, courteous toward every woman on that posting, constables and senior officers alike, a plain, unforced respect in how he spoke to them that made him easy to trust long before anyone thought to wonder if he deserved it.<\/p>\n<p>He noticed, without fail, when someone in the mess hadn't eaten properly, and simply pushed his own plate an inch closer without comment. He had a way of looking at a person — fully, without hurry, as though there were nowhere else he'd rather be — that Vikram, for all his intensity, had never quite managed to offer her on the rare, stolen evenings duty allowed him.<\/p>\n<p>Their evenings, too, had begun stretching further than the perimeter walks that started them. A café near the market once, when both their shifts happened to end early. A weekend, eventually, at a small resort two hours from the posting, booked under the thin pretext of a training course neither of their families thought to question. Long, unhurried conversations that ran late into the evening — the kind she had simply never been able to have with Vikram at all.<\/p>\n<p>With him, there had only ever been the phone: snatched minutes between duties, a voice without a face for weeks at a stretch, entire seasons of his life she'd only ever been told about rather than witnessed. With Kamal, she had his actual, physical presence, evening after evening, in a way two years of loving Vikram had never once allowed her.<\/p>\n<p>It was three weeks into their walks that she understood, with a small, private jolt, that she was no longer simply enjoying his company. She caught herself, one evening, laughing properly at something small he'd said — not the careful, contained laugh she kept for most company, but something looser, something that surprised her with its own volume — and saw him watching her laugh with an open, undisguised pleasure that had nothing calculated in it at all.<\/p>\n<p>\"There it is,\" he said. \"I wondered how long it would take to see that one properly.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"See what?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That laugh. The real one. You ration it out like it costs something.\" He said it lightly, though something steadier sat underneath the words. \"I like being one of the people it doesn't cost you anything to give it to.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't have an answer for that, not one she trusted herself to say aloud, and the silence that followed carried a different weight than their usual comfortable quiet — charged, unmistakable, the kind neither of them could pretend, after that evening, not to have noticed.<\/p>\n<p>What drew her, in the end, was not any single thing she could have named cleanly to another person, had anyone ever asked. It was the plain, cumulative fact of a man who was simply, entirely present: who didn't disappear for months at a time into a silence she wasn't permitted to ask about, who didn't carry a whole separate marriage and a whole separate war folded invisibly behind his eyes, who offered her, without ever once making her ask for it, the ordinary, unremarkable gift of being fully seen on an ordinary Tuesday.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram had given her two years of something urgent and consuming and impossible. Kamal, without ever trying nearly so hard, was quietly giving her something she hadn't realised, until now, she'd been starving for just as badly — the simple, undramatic comfort of a man who was actually, entirely there.<\/p>\n<p>It was one such evening, the two of them alone on the resort's small back veranda, the market-town lights spread out dim and distant below them, that he finally said the thing they'd both been circling for weeks without either of them naming it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I like you, Kavita. Not in the way that stays comfortable and undefined forever. Properly. I've liked you for a while now, and I'm tired of pretending the walking-the-long-way-back was ever really about the walk.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't refuse him. She couldn't find, in that moment, a single honest reason to. Whatever careful resistance she might have managed on an ordinary day simply wasn't available to her that evening.<\/p>\n<p>She moved toward him the way a dry, weathered log finally moves when a strong enough current of river water finally reaches it — not choosing the direction so much as simply, at last, giving up the effort of staying still against something that had clearly already decided where she was going.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram understood some of it — Ramfal's suspicion had sharpened lately, or perhaps only her own caution had, he was never quite sure which — but understanding a silence never made it easier to sit inside.<\/p>\n<p>He'd also just come off a stretch of duty that had left him quieter than usual, two operations back to back that he discussed with no one, the kind of weeks that hollow a man out from the inside without leaving a single visible mark.<\/p>\n<p>His post in the high reaches of the Kashmir valley that winter carried a particular kind of cold — not the dry, brittle cold of the plains, but something damper and heavier, the sort that worked its way through a man's boots and settled into his bones long after he'd come back inside. The mountains around him, on the rare clear mornings he caught sight of them, sat wrapped in mist so thick they seemed to be holding their breath, snow already dusting the higher ridgelines though the season hadn't properly turned yet.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere below the post, a river ran fast and full even in that cold, its sound climbing up through the mist in a low, constant roar that never once let up, day or night — loud enough, some nights, to feel less like water and more like something alive down there, something that never slept. The men posted there stopped hearing it after the first few weeks, the way you stop hearing anything that never changes. It was only on the rare morning the sound briefly dropped, for no reason anyone could explain, that they noticed it at all — and that silence, each time, unsettled them far more than the roar ever had.<\/p>\n<p>He'd stand at the perimeter some evenings, his breath fogging in front of him, and think about how far away everything warm in his life actually was — his own village, its mustard fields gone dormant for the season, Kavita's increasingly rare voice on the phone. All of it existed somewhere he couldn't currently touch, on the far side of a distance that had very little to do with kilometres.<\/p>\n<p>It was on nights like that — the generator humming somewhere behind the barracks, the cold pressing at the window like something patiently trying to get in — that he first thought of the number sitting unused in his phone. He wasn't looking for anything in particular when he finally sent that first forwarded message to Naina's number, three days after Kavita had pressed it into his palm and told him, plainly, to stop wasting whatever this was on her. There was no plan behind it, whatever story he'd later tell himself, or anyone else, about strategy and patience. He was bored, and a little numb, and curious in the aimless way a man gets curious when he has more empty evenings than he knows what to do with — a small, weightless act, like tossing a stone into a well simply to hear how far down the water was. A woman Kavita had described as sharp and impossible to impress seemed, in that particular mood, like exactly the kind of small, harmless distraction worth trying.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p><i>Sorry, wrong number,<\/i> he wrote, deliberately vague, his thumb hovering over the send button a full ten seconds longer than the message warranted.<\/p>\n<p><i>It's fine. Who were you calling?<\/i> came the reply, faster than he expected, and something in his chest loosened at the speed of it — small proof, but proof all the same, that a stranger's evening had room enough in it to spare a moment for his.<\/p>\n<p><i>No one in particular.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>She didn't answer again that night, and he lay awake longer than he'd admit, replaying the exchange, certain he'd already ruined something before it had properly had the chance to begin. Three days later, a message arrived without warning, and he felt it land somewhere just beneath his ribs before he'd even opened it: <i>Are you the wrong-number man again, or has someone else's phone confused you this week?<\/i><\/p>\n<p>He smiled at his own phone like a fool, alone in a room that suddenly felt considerably less empty than it had a minute earlier. <i>Same man. Same confusion, apparently.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>You're either genuinely bad at dialling, or doing this on purpose. I haven't decided which insults me less.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>Would it help if I admitted it was the second one?<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>It would help if you told me who gave you this number, actually. I'm not in the habit of continuing conversations with strangers who won't say how they found me.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>He hesitated over that one, the old, familiar weight of Kavita settling briefly across his chest like a hand placed there to slow him down, unwilling to drag her into an explanation that wasn't his to give, understanding even then that some doors needed to stay quietly shut for more than one person's sake. <i>A mutual friend. I'd rather not say more than that, for her sake as much as anything.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>Mysterious. I don't love mysterious.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>I promise it's a boring mystery. Nothing worth losing sleep over.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>I'll be the judge of that.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>Three more exchanges followed that evening, each one shedding a little more of its caution than the last, the way a fist slowly unclenches once it stops expecting to be struck, until she asked the one question that would, without either of them yet understanding it, quietly begin rearranging the whole architecture of his life.<\/p>\n<p><i>What do you actually do? For work, I mean.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>Army.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>Of course you are.<\/i> A pause long enough that he found himself staring at the screen, waiting, the silence stretching taut between two cities. <i>That explains the mysteriousness. You people are trained to withhold information like it's a competitive sport.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>Only the important things. Everything else I'm happy to hand over freely.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>We'll see about that.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>It was only once the messages had gone on long enough to become their own small habit — a few lines here, a longer exchange there, a rhythm building itself quietly in the background of both their days — that he noticed something had shifted without his quite deciding it should. He wasn't managing her the way he'd once have managed a woman he was trying to win over. He was simply enjoying her, the plain, unhurried way one enjoys a person whose mind moves at exactly the pace one's own does. It surprised him more than he let on, even to himself, how little effort any of it took, and how sharply that ease stood apart from everything complicated waiting on the other end of Kavita's increasingly rare calls.<\/p>\n<p>By the time he finally worked up the nerve to ask for a real conversation, it wasn't strategy driving him anymore, whatever he might have called it earlier, in the easier days before he'd noticed himself caring. It was simply that he wanted to hear her voice, and had run out of patience waiting for a good enough reason to ask for it. Whatever this careless first message had started, some quiet, stubborn part of him had no real intention of letting it end quietly.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't know, in those early calls, that the voice on the other end wasn't always hers. Someone else picked up more than once in her place, close enough in cadence over a bad line that he never once caught the difference — only noticing, vaguely, that she seemed to be in a different mood some evenings than others.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 11, "title": "Chapter 9 — Eight O'Clock, A Different Kind", "content": "<h1>Chapter 9 — Eight O'Clock, A Different Kind<\/h1>\n<p>The calls became a habit faster than either of them admitted out loud, the way certain habits always do — not arrived at by decision but grown quietly out of absence, out of an evening that hadn't yet had its call and felt, for that reason alone, unfinished, like a sentence missing its final word.<\/p>\n<p>Not every night — his duty didn't allow that, and hers didn't either. But most nights, somewhere between eight and nine, one phone or the other would ring. He usually took the call from the same spot each time — a narrow strip of corridor outside the barracks mess, one of the few places with steady signal, the cold seeping up through his boots from the concrete floor while, further off, someone's radio played the same tinny film songs it always did at that hour. He found himself, more evenings than he cared to examine too closely, watching the clock the way he'd once watched the horizon before a dawn patrol — alert, waiting, unable to fully settle into anything else until the appointed hour finally arrived and released him into it.<\/p>\n<p>He learned the shape of her days without ever asking for it directly. Mondays were always worse at the police station, some senior officer who saved his pettiest instructions for the start of the week like a man hoarding small cruelties. She took her calls sitting cross-legged on her room's bed, back against the cold wall, the smell of someone's evening dal still drifting faintly down the corridor from the mess two floors below. And she read the same three pages of the same novel most nights before sleep, always the same three, because sleep always claimed her before she got any further — some quiet part of her found this less frustrating than endearing, a private joke she'd been playing on herself for months without anyone else to share it with.<\/p>\n<p>She learned him in turn. He went quiet for exactly a beat before answering anything that actually mattered to him, as if weighing each word's precise cost before releasing it into the air between them. And he laughed at her jokes a half-second later than most people did, needing that extra fraction of a moment to fully believe she'd meant to be funny.<\/p>\n<p>That delay, rather than irritating her, had become the very thing she found herself waiting for — proof that her humour had actually landed somewhere real in him, rather than simply being received and set politely aside.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're slow,\" she told him once, after a joke landed and he only laughed properly a full breath after she'd expected him to.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm careful. There's a difference.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Careful men are also occasionally very boring.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll try to be less careful, then. Just for you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't strain yourself.\"<\/p>\n<p>By the second month, the calls had stopped being a pleasant habit and become, instead, the axis the rest of the day quietly arranged itself around — the way a house settles slowly around its own foundation. Naina finished her evening duties a little faster so she wouldn't miss it, timing her last patrol so she'd be back in her room with a cup of tea in hand well before eight. Vikram cut short the usual joking and banter with his fellow officers more than once, unwilling to waste time on idle laughter when eight o'clock finally arrived and asked its usual, patient question of him.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>It was during this same stretch that Sunaina left for the UK. Four weeks into their conversations, Naina had finally told him the truth behind the mysterious \"different mood\" he'd occasionally noticed on the line — that the woman he'd sometimes spoken to was her younger sister, handed the phone a few times as a joke Naina had simply never bothered to correct. The three of them talked together properly after that, an apology arranged over speaker and delivered with more laughter than contrition, and Vikram liked Sunaina immediately — sharp in her own particular way, entirely unbothered by anyone's approval, the kind of person who seemed to carry her own weather with her wherever she went.<\/p>\n<p>On the night before her flight, the three of them still crammed onto speakerphone over the noise of Sunaina's half-packed suitcase, Naina admitted, her voice thinner than he was used to hearing it, \"I keep telling myself 2 years isn't that long. It doesn't feel true yet.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Look after my sister,\" Sunaina told him, over the sound of a zipper being forced shut. \"She pretends she doesn't need looking after.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I intend to.\"<\/p>\n<p>After that, Sunaina was simply gone from the daily texture of things — a name mentioned occasionally, doing well abroad, folded into the background the way absent people always eventually are. Something in Naina's voice those first weeks carried a new, quieter ache underneath its usual sharpness, the sound of a house with one fewer person moving through its rooms.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram, listening to it down a phone line from a cold corridor a few states away, understood that this particular silence was one he could hear but never properly fill.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 12, "title": "Chapter 10 — The Park in Gurugram", "content": "<h1>Chapter 10 — The Park in Gurugram<\/h1>\n<p>Weeks passed that way after Sunaina left, the two of them growing steadily more essential to each other's evenings without either one naming it aloud — three months of calls behind them by now, easily. Neither of them mentioned meeting, not for weeks, both of them understanding without saying so that the moment they did, this would stop being a voice living safely inside a phone and start being something they'd actually have to answer for, in daylight, with names attached.<\/p>\n<p>Eventually he told her he had leave coming, that he'd be in Gurugram. He didn't ask to meet her. He simply said it, and let the words sit there, and let her decide what to do with them.<\/p>\n<p>She suggested Leisure Valley Park, in Sector 29. Neutral ground, she said. Somewhere ordinary, daylight, nothing that could be mistaken for anything it wasn't — as if either of them actually believed that for even a moment.<\/p>\n<p>He got there first, that quiet corner of the park settling into the hush of a weekday afternoon: the faint smell of cut grass and someone's roadside chaat drifting over from a vendor's cart near the entrance, a handful of joggers threading the paved path, an elderly couple sharing a bench, the distant, half-hearted sound of children playing somewhere just out of sight.<\/p>\n<p>He'd found the spot she'd named without needing to ask twice — a cement bench beneath a solitary gulmohar tree near the park's far edge, broad and old, its bark rough and grey, branches spreading wide enough to throw a generous circle of shade over the bench and the grass beside it.<\/p>\n<p>It wasn't in flower yet, the season still weeks from turning its canopy into the riot of red-orange it would eventually wear. But even bare of blossom, it stood apart from the neater, more disciplined trees lining the rest of the path — something in its size, its slightly wild, unpruned reach, that made it impossible to walk past without the eye catching on it.<\/p>\n<p>If a tree so old could be said to remember anything, this one had earned the right to. It had stood on that same patch of ground long before the park itself had a name, long before Sector 29 was anything more than open land waiting for a city to arrive and claim it — and in all those quiet decades, it had watched more beginnings than endings, the way old, patient things often do. Young couples had sat beneath it before either of these two had been born, whispering promises they half-believed themselves, stealing glances they thought no one else could see. It had held first confessions, first silences that meant more than words, the particular electricity of two strangers realising, mid-sentence, that they weren't strangers anymore. Whatever it was that trees do instead of feeling, this one seemed to do it well — a thousand small love stories folded quietly into its rings, each one certain, in its own moment, of being the only one that mattered. It had no way of knowing, and no need to, that it was simply beginning to hold one more.<\/p>\n<p>He found himself doing something he hadn't done before a mission briefing in years — checking his own posture, smoothing down a shirt that didn't need smoothing, running the last few months of calls back through his head as though there might be some version of himself in them worth living up to now. His hands, he noticed with some irritation, weren't entirely steady, a fact that struck him as faintly absurd in a man who'd once walked into ambush country without this particular tremor.<\/p>\n<p>Then she came around the path, and his heart did something he wasn't prepared for — a hard, single knock against his ribs, the kind he hadn't felt before an actual firefight in years, let alone before a woman walking toward a park bench. For a second neither of them said anything at all. Months of voice traveling down a phone line, and now here she was, whole and unfiltered, and both of them needed a moment simply to let the fact of each other catch up to itself, his pulse refusing, for several full seconds, to settle back into anything resembling its ordinary rhythm.<\/p>\n<p>She was taller than he'd expected — five-five, but carrying herself like more, shoulders set back, a long, confident stride that closed the distance between them faster than the path strictly required.<\/p>\n<p>He let himself look at her properly for the first time, the way a phone line had never once permitted, and something in his chest tightened at the sight of her: the fairness of her skin catching the afternoon light, the sharp, clean lines of her face, dark eyes that found his and held them without flinching, her long, thick hair loose today instead of pulled back for duty, moving slightly with the same confident stride that carried the rest of her.<\/p>\n<p>She wore plain civilian clothes — jeans, a simple kurti, fitted close enough to make its own quiet case, her figure full where it mattered without ever needing to announce itself. His eyes held there a beat longer than a first look strictly permitted, before he caught himself and dragged them back up to her face, something warm rising in his own chest at having been caught looking at all.<\/p>\n<p>He had spent months building a picture of her out of voice alone, and the picture, he understood now, had not come close to doing her justice. Something in his chest, still not fully recovered from that first hard knock, tightened further still — an ache that had nothing to do with pain.<\/p>\n<p>She, in turn, was doing her own quiet accounting of him, and made no effort to hide it, though her own heart had picked up its pace the moment she'd spotted him from the path — a quick, unexpected flutter she hadn't braced herself for, given how ordinary she'd told herself all morning this meeting would be.<\/p>\n<p>She had built a version of him in her head over these months of voice alone, and the man standing in front of her now made that version feel almost modest by comparison: taller than she'd pictured, his build carrying an obvious, disciplined strength that no phone call could have properly conveyed, and something in the steadiness of his gaze that suggested a mind working several steps ahead of whatever he actually chose to say aloud.<\/p>\n<p>Her eyes moved over him without hurry, even as her pulse refused to match that unhurried pace — the height of him, the set of his shoulders, the way he stood, still and grounded, like a man who'd learned long ago how to occupy space without needing to announce that he did. She took in the sun-weathered colour of his skin, the faint white scar along one forearm she filed away to ask about later, the overgrown edge of his hair that clearly hadn't seen a proper cut in weeks.<\/p>\n<p>Something in her own chest shifted at the sight of him, an approval she hadn't expected to feel quite this plainly, this fast, and she let herself sit with it a moment longer than was strictly comfortable before covering it over with words.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're staring,\" she said, by way of greeting, stopping a few feet from the bench.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm allowed one look. I've only had a voice for months.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"One look. Generous of you to ration it.\" But she was fighting a smile as she said it, and lost the fight within a second, and sat down on the bench a careful, deliberate distance from him — close enough to talk properly, far enough that neither of them had to account for anything more than conversation just yet.<\/p>\n<p>For the first few minutes, neither of them found the same easy rhythm the phone had given them so freely. It was strange, they both admitted within the first quarter hour, how much harder it was to simply talk once there was a face attached to the voice, a whole physical person sitting there to be self-conscious in front of.<\/p>\n<p>\"This is oddly difficult,\" she said. \"I talk to you every night without thinking twice about it. Now I can't decide where to look.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Look at the tree. Everyone finds trees easy to look at.\"<\/p>\n<p>She laughed at that, properly, and something in both of them loosened at the sound of it — the same laugh he'd known down a phone line for months, now confirmed, now real, now sitting three feet away from him instead of arriving through a speaker like a signal traveling a long distance to reach him.<\/p>\n<p>They talked for the better part of two hours after that, the awkwardness burning steadily off the longer they sat, the way morning mist burns off a field once the sun properly commits to rising. She told him about a case that had frustrated her that week, gesturing with her hands the way she never quite managed to over the phone.<\/p>\n<p>He told her about a posting two winters back that had tested every last ounce of patience he had. Partway through the story, he found himself watching her listen rather than paying attention to his own words — the way she leaned in slightly at the parts that actually interested her, the small furrow that appeared between her eyebrows when something he said didn't quite add up and she wanted to press him on it.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're different in person,\" she said, somewhere past the first hour. \"Not different-bad. Just — more still. On the phone you talk more. Here you watch more.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm taking inventory.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Of what?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Whether the voice matches the person. So far it does. Better, actually.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something crossed her face at that — brief, unguarded, gone before she fully let him see it — and she looked away toward the path a moment, composing herself with visible effort before turning back with her usual sharpness restored, like a curtain drawn back over a lit window.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't say things like that so plainly. I don't know what to do with plain honesty. I'm used to men who circle it for an hour before landing anywhere near the point.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't have the patience for circling.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Clearly.\" She studied him a moment, something decided settling quietly behind her eyes. \"I looked forward to this more than I let myself admit, driving over. I want you to know that, since you've decided plain honesty is the order of the day.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I looked forward to it more than I let myself admit either. For what it's worth.\"<\/p>\n<p>They sat with that a while, the ordinary noise of the park continuing around them — children somewhere, a vendor's call, the late afternoon light beginning to soften and lengthen across the path like something being slowly poured. They didn't touch. They barely sat close, the careful distance from the start of the conversation never quite closing, though something between them had closed regardless, some space that had nothing to do with the physical gap on the bench.<\/p>\n<p>\"I should go,\" she said eventually, though she made no move to. \"I have an early shift.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You should.\"<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them moved for another few minutes.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to do this again,\" she finally said, plainly, the same directness he'd already come to recognise as entirely hers. \"Not as a phone call. Like this. I don't know what to call whatever this is turning into, and I'm not going to pretend I do, but I know I want more of it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"So do I.\"<\/p>\n<p>She stood then, and so did he, and for a moment they simply looked at each other, the two hours settling into something neither of them needed a name for yet to already feel its weight.<\/p>\n<p>\"Same tree, then, whenever we manage to find the time again?\" she asked.<\/p>\n<p>\"Same tree.\"<\/p>\n<p>She left first, walking back the way she'd come, and he stood there a while longer under a gulmohar that hadn't yet flowered, understanding, with a clarity that startled him slightly, that he had no intention of letting whatever this was end at friendship. He never told her how he'd gotten her number. Not that day. Not for a long time after. He let her believe it was chance. Fate. Whatever a person wants to believe about the beginning of something they don't yet know is going to matter this much.<\/p>\n<p>The tree said nothing, of course, having no way to. But something in it, that afternoon, had already marked this one down as different from the thousand that came before it — not because it could know the future, which it couldn't, but because of the particular quality of the silence the two of them had left behind them, walking away in opposite directions. Most beginnings under its branches faded quietly, the way most beginnings do. This one, the tree seemed to sense without any way of proving it, was going to ask more of it than the others had.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 13, "title": "Chapter 11 — What They Learned About Each Other", "content": "<h1>Chapter 11 — What They Learned About Each Other<\/h1>\n<p>The weeks after the park settled into something neither of them had a name for yet — not quite courtship, not simply friendship, a careful, suspended space where they were still, in the truest sense, learning the actual shape of each other, the way a hand learns a new instrument before it earns the right to call itself skilled.<\/p>\n<p>The first of it came on a bike ride out to Highway Moods, a small resort off the Jaipur road. This visit carried none of the charged stillness of later ones — just two people talking easily over tea on an empty lawn, still cautious enough with each other to keep both hands visible on the table between them, like players showing they carried no hidden cards.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've always wanted to be part of the force, and I always will be,\" she said, watching the fields rather than him, as though the confession required somewhere else to look while it made its way out of her. \"It's not something I fell into. It's something I chose, and something I'll keep choosing, whatever else my life ends up holding alongside it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That doesn't surprise me at all,\" he said. \"You wear it the way people wear something they were always meant to carry.\"<\/p>\n<p>She studied him a moment, something warm settling behind her eyes, as though she hadn't quite expected to be understood this easily, this quickly, by someone who'd known her only a matter of weeks.<\/p>\n<p>He told her, in turn, about his own family — the father who measured a man's worth in land and discipline, the mother who prayed daily for a son's safe return without ever once asking him to choose a safer life. The farm. The mango trees his grandfather had planted, still bearing fruit long after the man himself had gone.<\/p>\n<p>\"You talk about your family like you're homesick even when you're not away from them,\" she observed.<\/p>\n<p>\"I think I've been homesick since the day I first left for training.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I understand that better than you'd think.\" She was quiet a moment, something firm settling into her voice. \"The force is the same for me, in its own way. Whatever else changes in my life — you, a family, children someday, if that's what it comes to — I will always be part of it. At any cost. I've never once imagined a version of myself standing outside it.\"<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>The second time, he took her to a cinema in Ambience Mall, a film neither of them cared much about beyond the excuse it gave them to sit together in the dark for two hours, shoulders barely touching, both of them more aware of the other's breathing than of anything unfolding on screen. Afterward, over coffee, she picked the film apart with a ruthlessness that made him laugh harder than the movie itself had managed all evening.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're a harsh critic.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm an honest one. There's a difference, and it's the difference that gets you actually useful opinions instead of polite ones.\"<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>The third time, they simply rode — no destination, the bike carrying them out past the city's edge and back again, an argument breaking out somewhere along the highway over whether duty or personal happiness deserved priority when the two conflicted, the wind snatching half their words away before they could properly land.<\/p>\n<p>\"You agree with me,\" she said, sounding faintly betrayed by the realisation. \"You're just arguing to see how I'd defend it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Guilty.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's an infuriating habit.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You'll get used to it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You'll get used to my habits too, Vikram,\" she said, something quieter and more deliberate entering her voice now, no longer teasing. \"And I should warn you — once you're used to me, properly used to me, you won't be able to leave. I'm not the kind of habit a person simply grows out of.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't correct the assumption folded quietly into her own sentence — that there would be a getting-used-to at all, on both sides, permanently — and he noticed the omission the way one notices a held breath, and let it sit between them, unspoken but entirely understood by both.<\/p>\n<p>The fourth time, without either of them quite planning it, they ended up back at the park — the same bench, the same gulmohar tree, though a light winter fog had settled over Sector 29 that evening, blurring the streetlamps into soft, private halos and emptying the paths of the usual joggers and evening walkers. The tree itself stood mostly bare still, one or two stubborn buds showing at the tips of its highest branches, as if it too were only cautiously testing whether the season had actually turned.<\/p>\n<p>\"It's colder than the other times,\" she said, drawing her shawl tighter, sitting closer to him than she had on any previous visit, close enough that their arms pressed together the whole length from shoulder to elbow.<\/p>\n<p>\"You could have suggested somewhere warmer.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I could have. But I like this bench.\" She looked up at the bare branches above them, her breath showing faintly in the cold air. \"I think I've decided it's ours now. Is that strange to say, four visits in?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"No,\" he said, surprising himself with how easily the word came. \"I think I decided that the first day, actually. I just hadn't said it out loud yet.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't answer that directly. But she let her head rest against his shoulder, and they sat like that a long while, saying very little, watching the fog move slowly through the empty park.<\/p>\n<p>Both of them understood, without needing the words for it, that something between them had quietly stopped being new and started, instead, being simply theirs — the way a place becomes a place only once enough has happened inside it to earn the name.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>The weeks between that first meeting in the park and his next proper leave stretched into nearly two months — no more single-day visits possible, just the calls continuing every evening as before, the relationship deepening entirely through voice rather than presence, the way a root deepens underground long before anyone above notices the plant has grown. It was only when his next leave finally came through — sixty days this time, longer than anything he'd been given before, a stretch of time that felt almost unearned — that they found themselves able to properly build something in person, day after day, rather than snatching single afternoons between long silences.<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them said it aloud, but both of them understood, in their own quiet way, exactly what sixty uninterrupted days actually meant for two people who had only ever learned each other in careful, rationed pieces. There would be no hiding behind distance now. Whatever this was, it was about to be tested properly, for the first time, in full daylight, with nowhere left to retreat to.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 14, "title": "Chapter 12 — Two Months", "content": "<h1>Chapter 12 — Two Months<\/h1>\n<p>The leave came through two weeks after the park — sixty days, longer than any stretch he'd been given in years, the kind of leave that usually meant a posting was about to change and someone above him was clearing his plate before it did. He didn't ask too many questions about why. He took it the way a man takes an unexpected gift, quickly, before whoever had given it changed their mind.<\/p>\n<p>He told Naina on the phone that evening, and she was quiet for a second longer than he expected, the silence stretching thin across the line like something being measured.<\/p>\n<p>\"Sixty days,\" she repeated, as if testing the number for weight. \"I don't think I've ever had sixty consecutive days of anything in my life that wasn't training.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You still have your duty. I'm the only one on leave.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I have evenings. I have Sundays. I intend to make unreasonable use of both.\" A pause, and then, in a voice that tried for careless and didn't quite manage it: \"I'm glad it's this long, Vikram. I don't think I'd have said that a month ago.\"<\/p>\n<p>He held onto that sentence longer than he probably should have, turning it over on the walk back to his quarters, the cold, quiet hills pressing close around him in their usual indifferent way, as though they hadn't just been standing witness to something worth noticing.<\/p>\n<p>They settled, without ever discussing it as a plan, into something that looked almost like a routine within the first week. He would call her a little before her shift ended, not to arrange anything specific, just to hear how the day had gone. Some evenings that was the whole of it — twenty minutes on the phone, her account of some absurdity at the station, his own quieter observations about a city he was still, in some ways, a visitor in. Other evenings it became a reason to meet, usually decided within the last hour before it happened, neither of them committing too far in advance, as if naming the plan too early might somehow curse it, the way old superstitions warn against speaking a wish aloud.<\/p>\n<p>The first evening he took her somewhere properly — Roots, the small cafe tucked into the renewable energy park off Leisure Valley Road, all rustic wood and open air, tables scattered loosely under a grove of trees as though the furniture itself had grown there alongside them. The evening had cooled enough that the outdoor seating felt like a mercy rather than a risk, birdsong settling into the gaps between conversations at the other tables, the faint smell of something frying drifting out from the kitchen. He arrived twenty minutes early and spent most of it deciding, then reconsidering, which table to choose. One near the entrance felt too visible. One tucked deep into the grove felt like he was hiding her, which was worse. He settled, in the end, on something in between — close enough to the trees for shade and privacy, far enough from the path that they wouldn't become a spectacle for anyone walking past.<\/p>\n<p>She arrived twelve minutes late, unhurried, sliding into the chair across from him with the unbothered confidence of a woman who had never once in her life apologised for being late and didn't intend to start now.<\/p>\n<p>\"You could have called,\" he said, standing briefly as she sat, an old habit from the mess that he immediately felt self-conscious about.<\/p>\n<p>\"I could have. I decided you'd survive the wait.\" She glanced around at the grove, the strings of small lights just beginning to glow as the light dropped, and something in her posture eased visibly, the way a person's shoulders drop once they realise a place isn't going to demand anything performative from them. \"This is a good choice, actually. I didn't expect that from you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Low expectations. I'll take the win.\"<\/p>\n<p>She picked up the menu, scanned it with the same brisk focus she brought to everything, and set it down again almost immediately. \"Order for me. I want to see if you actually listened to anything I told you in that park, or if you were just nodding along like every other man who thinks nodding is a personality.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's a serious test to spring on a man twelve minutes into dinner.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You've had a month to prepare. I don't feel sorry for you.\"<\/p>\n<p>He ordered: chilli mushroom to start, then shahi paneer for the both of them to share, veg raita on the side, naan for both — because he didn't bother asking her preference, missing only the detail about her wanting extra green chilli on the side, which she informed him, with real solemnity, was \"an acceptable start, nothing more.\"<\/p>\n<p>The food arrived slowly, unhurried the way everything at Roots seemed to be. In the easy stretch of eating it under the darkening canopy, strings of lights flickering on properly now overhead, they fell into something that surprised him with how effortless it was — not the careful conversation of two people still learning each other's edges, but something looser, quicker, threaded through with the kind of teasing that only works once a certain amount of trust has already been quietly laid down like a foundation neither of them had noticed pouring.<\/p>\n<p>She noticed the couple two tables over, deep in what was unmistakably a disastrous arranged-marriage meeting — the boy checking his phone every few minutes, the girl's mother doing most of the talking for her — and kept up a low, merciless commentary under her breath that had him fighting not to laugh loudly enough to draw attention.<\/p>\n<p>\"Watch,\" she murmured. \"He's going to check the phone again in eleven seconds. I've timed him twice already.\"<\/p>\n<p>He checked the phone again in eleven seconds. Vikram had to press his napkin to his mouth.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're a menace,\" he said.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm observant. There's a difference, and it's the difference that gets promotions in my line of work.\"<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in the middle of it, he began, out of old habit, cutting everything into small, precise pieces before eating any of it, and she watched him do it for a full minute before saying anything.<\/p>\n<p>\"You eat like you're being inspected.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Old habit.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Everything about you is an old habit. Sitting a certain way. Folding your napkin a certain way. I'm fairly sure you'd stand at attention in your sleep if someone gave the order.\" She leaned back slightly, studying him with an expression that sat somewhere between amusement and something gentler underneath it. \"I've decided it's going to be my personal mission to give you a few new ones. Habits, I mean. Before this leave is over.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That sounds like a threat.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It's an ambition. Try to keep up.\"<\/p>\n<p>When the plates were finally cleared, she ordered ice cream without consulting him, two scoops of something vanilla-adjacent with a name far too elaborate for what it actually was, and looked faintly betrayed when he declined to order any for himself.<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't like ice cream.\" Not quite a question.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've never seen the appeal. Cold, over too fast, mostly just sugar wearing a costume.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's the single most joyless sentence I've heard a grown man say in months.\" She took a spoonful anyway, watching him over it with open amusement. \"I'm going to fix this about you eventually. Consider it added to the list, right under the napkin-folding.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll brace myself.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You should. I don't lose battles I've decided to fight.\"<\/p>\n<p>They talked past the point where the restaurant had clearly hoped they'd finish and leave — about nothing important, mostly: Humshakals, which she'd seen and hated, a senior officer at her station who addressed the women constables as though they were slightly slow children, his own careful, half-funny account of a posting so cold that men had genuinely debated, with total seriousness, whether tea froze faster in a steel cup or a plastic one.<\/p>\n<p>She laughed at that harder than the joke probably deserved. He found himself pleased out of all proportion by having caused it, the way a man might feel about the first fire he ever managed to light without help.<\/p>\n<p>When the bill came, there was a brief, familiar skirmish over who would pay it. She won, the way she generally did, pulling a few notes from her bag and pressing them into the waiter's hand before he'd even finished reaching for his own wallet.<\/p>\n<p>Out on the pavement afterward, in the cooling evening air, neither of them moved to end the night quite yet.<\/p>\n<p>\"Sixty days,\" she said again, looking up at him. \"I intend to be unreasonable with all of them.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm counting on it.\"<\/p>\n<p>She let her hand find his on the walk back to where she'd parked, fingers lacing through his without comment, as if it were the most ordinary thing in the world.<\/p>\n<p>He understood, walking beside her through a city neither of them had grown up in, that something in the last two hours had quietly and permanently rearranged itself — the way a room feels different forever once you've learned a particular truth inside it.<\/p>\n<p>Most of his leave still remained. Neither of them said so aloud, but both of them had already begun, quietly, to dread the day it would eventually run out.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 15, "title": "Chapter 13 — What the Uniform Carries", "content": "<h1>Chapter 13 — What the Uniform Carries<\/h1>\n<p>Not every evening with Naina was easy or light. Some days she came to him already tired in a way that had nothing to do with the hour, a weariness that sat deeper than sleep could reach.<\/p>\n<p>It was a Thursday, roughly two weeks into his leave, when she called him straight from duty instead of waiting for the usual time. Her voice was flat, controlled in the careful way people control their voice when they are holding something back with both hands.<\/p>\n<p>\"Can you meet me,\" she said. It wasn't really a question. \"Not anywhere closed in. I need air.\"<\/p>\n<p>He knew where she meant before she said it. The park, the same bench, the same gulmohar tree that still hadn't flowered.<\/p>\n<p>She was already there when he arrived, still in uniform, which she never let herself be around him — always changed before meeting him, always particular about that line between the officer and the woman he knew. Tonight she hadn't bothered. Her cap was pushed slightly back, a few strands of hair stuck to her forehead where sweat had dried in the heat of a long day out in the sun. She looked tired in a way he hadn't seen on her before, sitting very straight on the bench, as though sitting straight was the only thing keeping the rest of her together.<\/p>\n<p>\"Bad day,\" he said, sitting down beside her, not asking.<\/p>\n<p>\"There was a case. A young couple, married a few months now, an inter-caste love marriage, no family blessing on either side. They came in this evening asking for protection.\" Her voice tightened slightly around the word. \"Her own father had shown up at their door that morning with three men from the family, threatening both of them in front of the whole street.\"<\/p>\n<p>He reached over and took the cap off her head himself, setting it down on the bench between them. Her hair, damp at the roots from the heat, fell loose around her face, and he brushed a strand of it back from her forehead with two fingers, gently, the way you'd settle something that had been out of place too long.<\/p>\n<p>\"What did she say, the girl?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That her father used to be the gentlest man she knew. That he'd carried her on his shoulders through every mela in that town, that he cried at her school graduation, that there wasn't a single wish of hers growing up he hadn't found some way to fulfil.\" Naina stared out at the darkening park rather than at him, something raw sitting just beneath her composure. \"And now he's standing outside her rented room with three other men, telling her, calmly, that he'd rather see her dead than see her disgrace him this way.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's not the same man, then.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's what I don't understand.\" She turned to look at him finally, and there was real distress in her face, distress that seemed to reach somewhere past the case itself. \"How does that much love turn into that much hate, Vikram? Not fade. Not cool off slowly, the way things do. Turn. Completely. Overnight, practically, the moment she did one thing he didn't approve of.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"What did you tell her?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That we'd file the complaint, that we'd note the threat formally, that we'd keep an eye on the address.\" Her jaw tightened. \"All true. None of it actually answers her question, or mine. She kept asking me, over and over, if I thought her father still loved her somewhere underneath all of it. I didn't have an honest answer for her. I still don't.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't try to fix it. He understood, without being told, that she hadn't come here for a solution — every solution she needed, she already knew was incomplete, and that the real question sitting now, sharp and unmoving, at the centre of her, wasn't procedural at all. He put an arm around her shoulders instead, and after a moment's hesitation she leaned into him, her head coming to rest against his shoulder, her cap forgotten on the bench beside them.<\/p>\n<p>\"Tell me the rest of it,\" he said. \"If there's more. I'm here to listen to all of it, whatever it is. Take your time.\"<\/p>\n<p>There was more. She talked for almost half an hour, sometimes circling back to the same detail three or four times, the way water circles the same drain before finally going down: the father's calm, terrible voice as the girl described it, the husband standing helpless beside his new wife with nothing to offer but his own two hands, the particular cruelty of threats delivered without a single raised voice. He didn't interrupt. He only listened, one hand resting lightly against her shoulder, letting her set the pace of her own unburdening.<\/p>\n<p>Something in him recognised the shape of what she was circling. He had done the same thing once, after his first posting, worrying the same three details of a bad night until an older havildar finally told him to stop chewing on it and let it go. He didn't tell her that. He only kept listening, glad, in some quiet corner of himself, that she'd chosen to come to him with it rather than carry it alone.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in the middle of it, still talking, she shifted slightly, her head moving from his shoulder down to rest against his chest instead, closer now, one hand coming up to hold loosely onto the front of his shirt, as if she needed something solid to keep her steady while she got the rest of it out. He didn't move to stop her or hurry her along. He simply adjusted his arm to hold her properly there, his palm settling flat against her back.<\/p>\n<p>A few minutes later, without breaking the flow of what she was saying, she drew her legs up onto the bench and turned her body further into him, until she was lying sideways across the bench with her head and shoulders settled directly onto his thighs, looking up at him now instead of ahead at the park. He let her arrange herself there, unhurried, one arm braced lightly across her middle to steady her, the other hand finding its way slowly into her hair, fingers moving gently through it, working loose the day's tangles one careful pass at a time.<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't have to stop,\" he said quietly, looking down at her, when she paused for breath. \"I'm here to listen to everything you can say. All of it. Take as long as you need.\"<\/p>\n<p>She continued from where she lay, her voice quieter now, looking up at him between sentences. \"I keep thinking about my own father, listening to her talk about hers. He's never once raised his voice at me. Never once threatened anything close to what hers did today.\" Something uncertain crossed her face. \"But I don't actually know what he'd do, Vikram, if I ever gave him a reason like that girl gave hers. I've never tested it. I don't think I want to.\"<\/p>\n<p>His hand kept its slow rhythm through her hair, patient, asking nothing in return. When she finally trailed off into silence, he cupped her face with his free hand, his thumb moving once across her cheekbone, the way you'd smooth a crease from something you meant to keep.<\/p>\n<p>\"You haven't said anything back,\" she said, still looking up at him from his lap.<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't think you wanted me to say anything. I think you wanted to say it, and have someone actually hear it, all the way through.\"<\/p>\n<p>She held his gaze a moment, something in her easing further at whatever she found there, and then closed her eyes, staying settled across his lap a long while under the tree that still hadn't flowered, neither of them in any hurry to be anywhere else.<\/p>\n<p>\"Thank you,\" she said, much later, eyes still closed. \"For not trying to fix it. Everyone always tries to fix it. My father does it. My colleagues do it. It makes me feel like the problem is that I'm upset, not that something is actually wrong.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Something is actually wrong,\" he agreed, his hand still slow in her hair. \"I'm not going to pretend otherwise just to make you feel better.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know. That's why I called you instead of going home.\" She managed a small, tired smile, eyes opening to look up at him again. \"Home would have told me to stop thinking about it. You just let me think about it, and sat here with me while I did.\"<\/p>\n<p>A long silence settled between them, not heavy anymore, just tired in the good way. Vikram tipped his head back slightly, looking up through the bare branches at the sky.<\/p>\n<p>\"Can I tell you something,\" he said, \"that will make you lose a little faith in the Indian Army's paperwork?\"<\/p>\n<p>She made a small sound against his shirt that might have been a laugh trying to happen. \"Go on.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"There's a rule — units posted above a certain height in the mountains get an extra rum ration, cold-weather allowance. My first posting up in the hills, our unit put in for it, quantity calculated properly, everyone happy. A few months later the audit people come through, and they're not happy. According to their measurement of the base camp's altitude, we were exactly twenty metres short of qualifying.\" He shook his head. \"Twenty metres. There was a serious discussion — genuinely serious, I promise you — about whether we could build a platform.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina lifted her head off his lap enough to look at him properly, disbelief spreading slowly across her face. \"You considered building a <i>platform<\/i>. For rum.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"We considered it very seriously. In the end someone pointed out the flagpole was already on a mound, and the mound alone got us most of the way there, and nobody wanted to explain a wooden rum-platform to a visiting brigadier. So we let it go. But for about a week, half the unit was ready to become structural engineers over twenty metres.\"<\/p>\n<p>She laughed — really laughed, head dropping back onto his thigh, one hand coming up to cover her own face. \"This is a true story.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Every word. There's a saying we had — every battle gets fought at the joint of two map sheets, printed at two different scales. Turns out every rum ration gets fought at the joint of two altitude readings too.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was still smiling when she quieted, wiping at her eyes. \"I don't know how you make twenty metres and a wooden platform sound like the funniest thing I've heard all week.\"<\/p>\n<p>The tree had been there longer than either of them. It had learned, in its own slow, rooted way, to feel the difference between an ordinary evening and one that carried weight.<\/p>\n<p>It had felt them arrive as strangers, months back — two voices finally given bodies, awkward and careful under its bare branches. Something in it, whatever a tree has instead of a heart, had leaned toward them the way old, patient things lean toward anything young and uncertain and trying.<\/p>\n<p>It had felt, since then, the small accumulating warmth of every visit after. A hand finding another hand. A laugh let out too loud. A silence that meant more than either of them said aloud.<\/p>\n<p>And now this — a woman curling herself into a man's lap beneath it, weeping quietly into his shirt.<\/p>\n<p>It held itself over that bench the way it had held itself over every version of this before — quiet, patient, gathering each thing it witnessed into itself the way roots gather water. It did not know, that evening, how this particular story would end.<\/p>\n<p>It only knew that it wanted, very badly, for this one to be different — for once, for these two, to be allowed to bloom all the way through.<\/p>\n<p>It did not know yet how much it was about to ask of them.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 16, "title": "Chapter 14 — The Call from Kashmir", "content": "<h1>Chapter 14 — The Call from Kashmir<\/h1>\n<p>Not everything about that leave was easy. There was a week, roughly three weeks in, when something between them went wrong, and it took both of them entirely by surprise, the way weather turns without warning on a day that had promised nothing but clear skies.<\/p>\n<p>It started with a phone call, the evening before, one he hadn't expected. Captain Rudra Pratap Singh, still up in Kashmir with the unit, RR — a name from the same commando years as Vikram's own, a friend from before either of them had gone their separate ways in the service. Captain to captain, the kind of call that didn't need small talk first.<\/p>\n<p>\"Reddy's gone,\" Rudra said. \"Operation two nights back. We lost him.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram didn't say anything for a while, the silence stretching long enough that Rudra could probably hear the shape of it down the line.<\/p>\n<p>\"How,\" he finally asked, though some part of him didn't want the answer.<\/p>\n<p>The details came anyway, in Rudra's flat, careful voice — an ambush, a ridge line, a decision made in under a second that could have gone either way and didn't. Gone, the word landing with a finality no amount of warning could have properly prepared him for.<\/p>\n<p>Reddy — Venkatesh Srinivas Reddy, from Lepakshi, a small village in Anantapur district, Andhra Pradesh, one of the best commandos Vikram had ever served beside in 23 RR, the kind of man who checked on the newest recruits before he checked on himself, who had once carried Vikram's pack an extra six kilometres without being asked, without ever mentioning it again. Gone, the word landing with a finality no amount of warning could have properly prepared him for.<\/p>\n<p>\"I thought you'd want to hear it from someone who was there,\" Rudra said, before he rang off. \"Not secondhand.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram thanked him, said the things you're supposed to say, and put the phone down, and something in him went quiet in a way it hadn't in a long time, a silence settling in that felt less like peace than like a held breath.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't eat that night. He told himself he wasn't hungry, and it was true enough not to feel like a lie.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't tell Naina either.<\/p>\n<p>He saw her the next evening anyway, because cancelling would have meant explaining why, and he didn't have it in him yet to explain. He sat across from her at a small table, food going cold between them, and said almost nothing, and by the third night of it, she'd had enough.<\/p>\n<p>\"You've barely said ten words to me all evening,\" she said. \"What is it?\" She looked at his plate, untouched from twenty minutes ago, then at the sharper lines of his face that she hadn't quite registered until now. \"And you haven't eaten. Not tonight, and I don't think you ate properly yesterday either. I watched you push the same three bites around your plate at lunch.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not hungry.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You're never not hungry. That's the other thing about you I've learned this month.\" Her voice had an edge to it now. \"I'm not asking because I'm bored, Vikram. I'm asking because you're somewhere else, and you won't take me with you, and now you're not even eating, and I don't know what to do with any of that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"There's nothing to take you to.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's not an answer. That's a wall.\" She put her fork down. \"I've told you things this month I've never told anyone. And when it's your turn to be somewhere difficult, you close the door. Quietly. And expect me not to notice.\"<\/p>\n<p>He almost told her, right then. The words were right there — Reddy's name, the ridge line, the six kilometres he'd never mentioned again. But something stopped him, something he couldn't fully name even to himself in that moment, and instead he said, \"It's old. From before you. It's not about you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't ask if it was about me. I asked you to let me in anyway.\"<\/p>\n<p>She left soon after that, angry in a way he understood and couldn't yet fix, and he sat alone with two plates of cold food, turning over exactly why he hadn't just told her.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>He didn't call her the next day. She didn't call him either, the silence between them settling in like something that had come to stay a while.<\/p>\n<p>It was on the second day, sitting alone with it, that he finally understood the shape of what had stopped him. It wasn't that he didn't trust her. It was that telling her meant making the risk real for her in a way it had never been real before — not an idea of danger, but a name, a face, a friend who'd quietly carried more for him than either of them ever spoke about, and was now simply gone. He was afraid that if he let her see that clearly, she would start being afraid every time he was posted somewhere she couldn't see him. He was afraid that fear, once it took root in her, would be the thing that eventually made her decide loving a soldier wasn't worth the cost. He had already lost Reddy. He didn't know what he'd do if being honest about that loss cost him her too.<\/p>\n<p>He hadn't eaten properly in two days by then, though he hadn't been keeping track closely enough to notice it himself.<\/p>\n<p>He went to find her instead of calling, because some decisions are easier said in a room than over a phone line.<\/p>\n<p>She opened the door and didn't look surprised to see him, only tired, arms crossed, waiting. Her eyes went to him properly for the first time in days, and something in her face changed.<\/p>\n<p>\"You look terrible,\" she said, not unkindly. \"When did you last eat something?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't remember exactly.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Vikram.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Two days, maybe. I haven't been keeping track.\" He said it plainly, without much weight behind it, as if it were an unimportant detail rather than something that had visibly hollowed out his face.<\/p>\n<p>She didn't say anything else about it right away. She went to her small kitchen instead.<\/p>\n<p>Outside, the evening traffic on the main road had thinned to its late hour hum, and somewhere below her window a vendor's cart bell rang twice, faded, and didn't come again. The smell of reheated dal and cumin drifted in from the kitchen before she did, warm and plain and entirely unlike the silence he'd been carrying for two days.<\/p>\n<p>She came back a few minutes later with a plate of whatever she had — reheated dal, some rice, nothing special — and set it down in front of him on the table without a word, the no-nonsense insistence of someone who intended to be obeyed on this point before anything else was discussed.<\/p>\n<p>\"Eat first,\" she said. \"Then talk.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Naina, I want to tell you something—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You can tell me while you eat. I'm not negotiating on this part.\" She sat across from him, watching until he actually picked up the spoon, and only then did her posture ease slightly.<\/p>\n<p>He ate slowly at first, then with more appetite than he'd expected, the plain food doing something simple and necessary that he hadn't let himself have in two days. Naina waited, patient, saying nothing until he'd made real progress on the plate.<\/p>\n<p>\"Now,\" she said. \"Tell me.\"<\/p>\n<p>He told her about Reddy. All of it — the call from Rudra, the ridge line, the six kilometres, the quiet that comes after losing someone you'd assumed, in the careless way soldiers do, would simply always be there. His voice stayed steady through most of it and broke only once, near the end, and he let it break, because pretending otherwise in front of her felt, for the first time, like a worse choice than showing her.<\/p>\n<p>When he finished, she was quiet, turning it over, and then she said, \"Why didn't you just tell me that night?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Because I was afraid,\" he said. \"Not of the grief. Of what it would do to you, hearing it. I didn't want you to start being afraid every time I go back. I didn't want that fear to be the thing that eventually made you decide this wasn't worth it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't get to decide what I can carry,\" she said, but gently, not as an attack. \"You don't get to protect me by keeping me outside the parts of your life that actually cost you something. That's not protection, Vikram. That's just a different kind of being alone. And starving yourself over it doesn't protect me either. It just means I end up worrying about two things instead of one.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know that now.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not going to pretend I'm not frightened, hearing that story,\" she admitted. \"I am. I'll probably think about it every time you're posted somewhere I can't reach you. But I'd rather carry that fear knowing the truth than be kept comfortable by a lie.\" She moved closer, sitting beside him now instead of across from him. \"You lost a friend. You don't have to manage my feelings about it on top of your own grief. Just let me be here for both. And eat properly. I mean it.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't have an answer for that beyond leaning into her, and she let him, drawing him in with an arm around his shoulders the way he had once drawn her in under the gulmohar tree. His head found her shoulder first, heavy, the tiredness of two days and two nights finally catching up to him all at once now that he'd let the weight of it out into the open. She held him there without hurrying him, one hand coming up to rest against the back of his neck.<\/p>\n<p>A few minutes passed like that, quiet, before he shifted further, his head sinking from her shoulder down to rest against her chest instead. She adjusted without comment, one arm settling around him properly, her other hand finding his hair and beginning to move slowly through it — the same unhurried, careful motion he had once used on her under that tree.<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't have to hold yourself together right now,\" she said quietly, her fingers still moving through his hair. \"Not with me. Not tonight.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't answer, but something in him loosened further at that. After a while longer he let himself sink lower still, until his head rested against her thighs, looking up at her the way she had once looked up at him from his own lap.<\/p>\n<p>She let him stay there, one hand cupping the side of his face, her thumb moving once, gently, along his cheekbone, tracing the tired lines she'd noticed at her door.<\/p>\n<p>\"Tell me about him,\" she said, eventually. \"Reddy. Properly. Not just how he died. I want to know who he was.\"<\/p>\n<p>So he did, looking up at her the whole time, her hand steady in his hair, her palm occasionally coming to rest against his cheek when his voice caught on some particular memory.<\/p>\n<p>They had come up together from the very beginning — the same commando training course, two boys from opposite ends of the country who'd found, somewhere in the shared exhaustion of those early months, a friendship neither of them had gone looking for. They'd stayed together after, both posted into the RR at nearly the same time, the kind of coincidence that quietly becomes a small mercy once you understand how rare it actually is.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram told her about the gate that first evening — the skull set into the arch above it, a long khukri driven straight through its jaw, its big teeth bared wide, grinning. Neither of them had been warned. For one unsettled second, both of them had thought the same foolish thing — that it was grinning <i>at<\/i> them, laughing at two boys standing there with their kit bags still smelling of the train. He remembered Reddy going very still beside him, and the sentry waving them both through without much ceremony, tilting his head toward the small brass plate fixed just below it, the engraving worn smooth by years of eyes that had paused there exactly the way theirs did.<\/p>\n<p><i>Smile a little. Welcome, you're in Head Hunters.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>It was only later, months in, that Vikram understood what that grin had actually been telling them. It wasn't laughing at anyone. It was a skull that had found a way to smile even in its own condition, and it was asking, every time either of them walked past it, why a man still breathing, still whole, still in a far better condition than that, couldn't manage the same.<\/p>\n<p>Reddy had laughed at that, right there at the gate, more from nerves than humour, and Vikram had laughed with him, and it had taken both of them months, and a few operations, before either understood it properly. Reddy, more than Vikram, had always managed it. Most days, right up to the end, he still could.<\/p>\n<p>Years of operations followed. Long stretches in the terrain of J&amp;K, resting between patrols on some cold ridge or another, sharing whatever the day had given them to eat. Reddy had a particular way with a tadka he insisted on making himself whenever the dal ration allowed it — a small ritual he took more seriously than the rank difference between them technically permitted. They traded pudis across freezing outposts like it was the most ordinary thing in the world. By then, it was.<\/p>\n<p>In the long, quiet hours of rest between operations, they'd told each other everything. Childhoods. Families. The small, specific shape of home each of them carried — the way men do when the mountains make everything else feel very far away and very fragile, worth saying out loud before it's too late to. He talked for an hour straight once about his mother's cooking, on a freezing watch, and told terrible jokes that landed anyway because of how hard he laughed at his own punchlines. He'd once given up his own turn at the fire on a night so cold Vikram still remembered the shape of his own breath, and never mentioned it, not once, not even when Vikram brought it up himself months later.<\/p>\n<p>Reddy was his parents' only child, Vikram told her. No brothers. Not even a sister to share the weight of what he carried for them.<\/p>\n<p>He was to be married soon — a girl from his own village named Sujatha Reddy, someone he'd studied alongside since they were children, their families already easy with each other in the way old village families become. Reddy had shown him a photograph once, folded soft at the corners from being carried too long in a chest pocket. Long, thick hair pulled into a simple braid. A smile that reached her eyes properly, the kind that doesn't need coaxing.<\/p>\n<p>He'd talked about her the way men rarely let themselves talk about anyone: her patience with his mother's moods, the quiet, unfussy way she looked after people without being asked, the recipe for her grandmother's sambhar she'd been perfecting for two years because it was Reddy's favourite, the kind of woman who made a house feel cared for simply by being in it.<\/p>\n<p>Reddy had been entirely, unguardedly in love with Sujatha, in the plain way a man is in love when he's stopped pretending otherwise even to himself. Her mother, Reddy had said, smiling in that particular way of his, had been happier about the match than anyone, practically counting down the months herself.<\/p>\n<p>Just before this leave, Reddy had told Vikram he'd be putting in for marriage leave soon too. The two of them had joked, in the careless way soldiers joke about their own futures, about whose wedding would actually happen first.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram found himself thinking of her now, more than he expected to — a woman he had never met, whose name he barely knew properly, who was somewhere in Lepakshi right now still arranging her own life around a wedding that was never going to happen. He didn't know how a person survived news like that. He only knew that somewhere, tonight, she was likely finding out, or had already found out, and there was nothing in the world he could do about it except carry the weight of imagining it.<\/p>\n<p>\"Now there's no wedding coming,\" Vikram said, his voice dropping lower. \"Only two parents back in Lepakshi who still can't fully make themselves believe it — that a son who earned a Sena Medal, whose photograph they've probably shown to every relative who visits this year, simply stopped existing on a ridge line neither of them will ever see. I understand exactly why they can't believe it. Some truths arrive too large and too final for a mind to hold all at once, however plainly the words are said.\"<\/p>\n<p>It took a while, and somewhere in the telling the grief loosened its grip on him just slightly, enough to breathe around. She was there for all of it this time — not just listening, but holding him through it the exact way he had once held her, her hand never quite still in his hair, her thumb never quite still against his skin.<\/p>\n<p>He understood, lying there in her lap under lamplight instead of a bare gulmohar tree, that strength had never meant not needing this. It had only meant not knowing, until now, that he was allowed to have it.<\/p>\n<p>But somewhere beneath the relief of finally being held, a small, cold thought surfaced that he couldn't quite put down again: grief, once you let it all the way in the door, rarely comes just the once. He didn't say it out loud. He only held onto her a little tighter, as though that alone might be enough to keep the thought from ever turning out to be true.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 17, "title": "Chapter 15 — A Friend's Wedding", "content": "<h1>Chapter 15 — A Friend's Wedding<\/h1>\n<p>Naina had booked the restaurant three days ahead, which wasn't like her at all — she was not, by nature, a woman who planned evenings, let alone booked them. But she'd decided, quietly and without telling him why, that before she dropped him into a wedding full of her colleagues, he deserved one evening that was entirely, uncomplicatedly good — no risk, no calculation, nothing either of them had to manage, a small gift she'd wrapped without a card attached.<\/p>\n<p>The restaurant sat on the upper floor of one of Gurugram's newer high-rises, floor-to-ceiling windows looking out over a skyline still half under construction, cranes lit up against the night like strange, patient birds.<\/p>\n<p>It was the kind of place that had opened in the last year or two, catering to the city's growing appetite for fine dining alongside its glass towers: white tablecloths, a menu Vikram barely glanced at before ordering, the quiet hush of a room where nobody needed to raise their voice to be heard, the faint smell of butter and roasting spices drifting from the kitchen every time a server passed. Naina had chosen it, which surprised him a little — she wasn't a woman given to caring much about appearances.<\/p>\n<p>\"I like watching the city build itself while we eat,\" she said, when he asked why this place. \"Half of what you're looking at right now didn't exist two years ago. There's something almost comforting in that. Nothing stays only what it was.\"<\/p>\n<p>It was, he realized partway through the evening, the first time they had eaten a meal somewhere that looked, to anyone glancing over, like precisely what it was — two people who mattered to each other, out for dinner, in no particular hurry to hide it.<\/p>\n<p>\"You keep looking around,\" Naina said, amused. \"Waiting for someone to object?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Old habit.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You can let it go tonight. No one here knows either of us. No one here cares.\" She reached across and took a piece of naan off his plate without asking, the way she always did. \"I want one evening where you're not calculating exits.\"<\/p>\n<p>They walked out of the restaurant into a Gurugram night gone quiet with the late hour, the city's glass towers lit up sparse and scattered around them, and Naina slipped her hand back into his without comment, and neither of them let go of it again until they reached wherever the night was taking them next.<\/p>\n<p>The invitation came from a friend of Naina's — a fellow constable from her training batch, getting married in Gurugram, a wedding big enough that one more guest wouldn't draw much attention. \"Come as my plus one,\" she said. \"Nobody from my family will be there. It's just her side and a scattering of our batch-mates. Easy crowd.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Easy crowd sounds promising.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't get used to it. This is the safest possible wedding to bring you to. I chose it on purpose.\"<\/p>\n<p>He wore the one good kurta he owned, and when he reached the venue and saw her waiting near the entrance, he stopped for a second before he could stop himself.<\/p>\n<p>She wore a deep green lehenga, heavy gold work running along the hem and across the sleeves of her blouse. Her hair, usually tied back or clipped up for work, was left open tonight, falling long and thick past her waist in loose curls, catching the light every time she turned her head. Her skin looked warmer in the glow of the string lights around the venue, her eyes lined dark, her lips a soft red. She was beautiful in a way he hadn't fully let himself notice before — not because it wasn't there, but because he'd always seen her moving too fast, arguing too sharply, laughing too suddenly to sit still and simply look at her. Tonight, standing there in green and gold with her hair down, she gave him no choice but to look.<\/p>\n<p>\"You look—\" he started.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't. You'll make me self-conscious in front of people I have to work with on Monday.\" She took his arm and pulled him toward the crowd, her bangles catching the light with the movement, her long hair swinging against her back as she walked.<\/p>\n<p>The evening was, exactly as promised, an easy one — a small circle of Naina's colleagues from her training batch, warm and curious without being invasive, more interested in teasing Naina about finally bringing someone than in interrogating Vikram directly.<\/p>\n<p>\"We'd almost given up,\" one of them said, grinning at Naina. \"Three years of watching you turn down every match anyone suggested, and here you are.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't turn him down. That's the difference.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Clearly.\" The woman looked Vikram over, friendly rather than assessing. \"Army?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Was. Still finishing out my time.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Good answer. Not too much detail, not too little. She trained you well already.\"<\/p>\n<p>They ate, and when the DJ started up near the mandap — speakers stacked high, coloured lights already flashing — the opening beat of a popular Haryanvi song thumped out over the crowd, Gajban Pani Ne Chali, and a knot of Naina's colleagues pulled her toward the open ground at once.<\/p>\n<p>\"Come on,\" she said, tugging Vikram's sleeve. \"You can't stand at the edge all night like you're on guard duty.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't dance.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Nobody's asking you to dance well. Just watch first.\"<\/p>\n<p>He watched. The crowd moved the way weddings moved these days — no live drums, no folk singers, just the DJ playing one popular song after another, everyone dancing together in one loose, easy crowd. Men and women mostly kept some space between them, nobody really touching, the whole thing carried by shoulders and hands thrown up on the beat, big expressions, a wink here, a serious face there.<\/p>\n<p>Naina pulled him in by the sleeve, not the hand. \"Just move your shoulders. Nobody's judging your technique tonight.\"<\/p>\n<p>He tried, badly, and she laughed at him openly, keeping her own much better rhythm a few feet away, never any closer than that. But the distance between them said more than touching would have — a look held a second too long, a raised eyebrow when he got a step wrong, her smile clearly meant only for him even while her feet stayed with the beat of the whole moving crowd. When the DJ moved into 52 Gaj Ka Ghagra, a proper cheer went up from the group around them, Naina's colleagues laughing and pulling each other into the dancing, none of them paying the two of them any particular mind, which was, he understood, exactly the freedom she'd chosen this wedding for.<\/p>\n<p>Watching her like this — unguarded, laughing at her own bad attempts to teach him rhythm, her hair catching the coloured lights with every turn — something settled in Vikram's chest that he didn't immediately have a name for.<\/p>\n<p>He had watched her be sharp. He had watched her be tired, be vulnerable on a park bench under a tree. He had never once watched her be simply, uncomplicatedly happy.<\/p>\n<p>The sight of it undid him more thoroughly than any of the careful, weighted conversations they'd had all month. He didn't try to name it. He only kept watching, memorising the exact tilt of her head when she laughed at her own bad dancing, as though some part of him already understood this was a version of her he'd want to be able to call back later, on some colder night, far from here.<\/p>\n<p>Naina caught his eye once, mid-turn, and something in his face made her miss a step of her own — not the polite, careful way he usually looked at her, but something unguarded, almost startled, as if she'd surprised him simply by existing in front of him like this. She filed the look away without saying anything about it, pleased in a way she didn't examine too closely, and let the music pull her back into the next beat.<\/p>\n<p>They left before the party ended, walking out into a parking lot still strung with leftover fairy lights from the mandap, the noise of the wedding fading behind them into something distant and warm.<\/p>\n<p>\"Thank you for coming,\" Naina said, as they walked toward the car. \"I know weddings aren't anyone's favourite way to spend an evening.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"This one wasn't bad. Your colleagues are kinder than I expected.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"They're good people. I got lucky with that batch.\" She smiled, something easy and content in it. \"I like that you got to see a version of this without six aunts asking about your intentions. There's time enough for that later, if it comes to it.\"<\/p>\n<p>He drove her home, the city quiet around them at that hour, and it was only once they'd reached her building, standing outside her flat under the single working streetlamp, that he finally said what he'd been carrying all evening, tucked somewhere under his ribs since the phone had rung that afternoon.<\/p>\n<p>\"I have to tell you something before you go up.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something in his tone made her go still. \"Tell me.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I got a call this afternoon, before the wedding. I have to report back early. I don't have the full sixty days after all.\" He watched her face carefully, bracing for whatever came next. \"I don't know the details yet. I won't know until I'm there.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet, processing it. \"When?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Tomorrow morning.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't argue, didn't ask the questions he'd half expected — where, why, how dangerous. She simply stepped closer, and put her arms around him properly, her cheek against his chest, and held on longer than either of them strictly needed to for a goodbye that wasn't even a real goodbye yet.<\/p>\n<p>\"Go,\" she said finally, into his shirt, her voice steady despite everything underneath it. \"Do whatever it is you have to do. Come back whole. That's the only thing I'm actually asking for.\"<\/p>\n<p>He held her a moment longer, his chin resting against the top of her head, breathing in the mix of her perfume and the wedding's leftover incense still clinging to her hair.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll come back,\" he said.<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\" She pulled back enough to look at him properly, her eyes bright but steady. \"I'm not going to make this harder than it needs to be by crying outside my own building. Just go, and be careful, and call me the moment you're able to.\"<\/p>\n<p>He nodded, and pressed a kiss to her forehead instead of her mouth — careful, deliberate, a promise rather than an ending. She let him go, watching him get back into the car, her arms wrapped around herself against the sudden cold of his absence already beginning, before she turned and climbed the stairs to her flat alone.<\/p>\n<p>Weeks later, on a visit home, Naina found herself telling her mother about him properly for the first time — that there was a man, that he was Army, that she liked him more than she'd expected to admit out loud. It happened almost by accident, her phone left open on the kitchen table while she stepped away, a photo from the wedding still on the screen, Vikram beside her in the green and gold light — and her mother, glancing down while clearing the plates, simply asked, plainly, who he was. Naina showed her the rest after that, a handful of others, and a short video her friend had taken of the two of them laughing badly through the dancing.<\/p>\n<p>Her mother watched all of it twice before saying anything at all, and when she did speak, her voice carried none of the easy warmth Naina had half hoped for.<\/p>\n<p>\"He looks like a decent man,\" her mother said finally. \"Steady. You can tell that much even from a photograph, if you know what to look for.\" She paused, and something more guarded crept into her tone. \"That's not the same as saying I approve of anything, Naina. You know that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't ask you to approve.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"No, but you showed me the pictures, which means some part of you wanted me to react to them a certain way.\" Her mother's voice softened, though the caution in it didn't fully leave. \"I'm not going to pretend I'm comfortable with this, beta. You're twenty-one. You think you know your own mind, and maybe you do, but I've watched enough girls your age fall hard for a man before they've had the chance to see what he actually is underneath the good manners and the handsome face.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"He's not just a handsome face.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not saying he is. I'm saying you're too close to him right now to tell the difference between what's true and what you want to be true.\" Her mother exhaled, the sound of a woman choosing her words with real care. \"Your father hasn't even heard his name yet. Once he does, there will be questions — his caste, his family, what people in our own circle will say about a daughter of ours going around with an Army man nobody arranged for her to meet. You know how this works, Naina. You've watched it work this way your whole life.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know how it works.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then you also know what I'm about to say next, so I'll say it plainly instead of dancing around it.\" Her mother paused, choosing the next words with visible care. \"Be careful with your heart before you're careful with anything else.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina said nothing, waiting.<\/p>\n<p>\"If you let yourself go too deep with this boy, and it doesn't end where you want it to — his family, our family, whatever obstacle decides to stand in the way — you will be the one left hurting. Not him. Not me. You.\" Her mother's voice didn't rise, but something in it sharpened all the same. \"And you're young enough still that a wound like that can shape years of you, if you're not careful how you carry it.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina was quiet for a moment, something in her chest tightening at the warning even as some other, more stubborn part of her wanted to argue against every word of it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I hear you,\" she said finally. \"I'm not going to pretend I'm not already in deep, Ma. I think I have been for a while now.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her mother sighed, a sound that carried more worry than judgment in it. \"Then all I can ask is that you keep your eyes open even while your heart is doing what it's doing. That's all any mother can ask, in the end.\"<\/p>\n<p>Sunaina, watching the same video days later on a screen many thousand miles away, forwarded to her by Naina herself along with the same handful of photos, said considerably more, though mostly to herself, filing away an opinion she wouldn't get to properly deliver in person for a while yet.<\/p>\n<p>She called Vikram that evening and told him all of it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I told her about you,\" she said. \"My mother. Everything — that there's a man, that he's Army, your face, your name, all of it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"How did she take it?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"About as well as I expected. She thinks you're decent, which is more than I expected her to say about anyone. She also thinks I'm too close to you to see straight, and reminded me exactly how many obstacles are standing between whatever this is and anything permanent.\" Naina's voice carried something careful underneath its usual steadiness. \"She asked when she'd meet you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'd like to meet her,\" he said. \"Whenever you're ready for that to be real and not just something you said to sound braver than you felt.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I am brave,\" she said. \"I just like you noticing when I have to work at it.\"<\/p>\n<p>She hung up still smiling, but the smile didn't quite reach the small, cold weight her mother's word had left sitting in her chest. <i>Obstacles.<\/i> She found herself, without quite meaning to, already counting how many there might turn out to be.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 18, "title": "Chapter 16 — Fifteen Hundred Kilometres", "content": "<h1>Chapter 16 — Fifteen Hundred Kilometres<\/h1>\n<p>He got the call on a Tuesday — Sunaina, of all people, calling from London at an odd hour, her voice tight with a worry she was trying, not entirely successfully, to keep from showing. \"Naina's in Medanta, in Gurugram. Nothing life-threatening, the doctors say, but she's been there three days already and she won't tell our parents how bad it actually was, and I can't get there myself, and I didn't know who else to call.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was in Manipur at the time, and got leave sanctioned within hours using an emergency clause he'd never had reason to invoke before. An Air Force transport was flying out to Delhi that same evening, and he managed a seat on it through a contact from his own unit, landing in Delhi by early morning and reaching the hospital in Gurugram within the hour after that, the whole distance behind him collapsing, in his mind, into a single unbroken stretch of urgency.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere over Uttar Pradesh, watching the dark fields slide past far below, he had let himself think, for the first time since Rudra's call about Reddy, about what it would actually mean to lose someone this way: not on a ridge line he'd never see, but in an ordinary hospital bed, from something as unglamorous as a fever nobody had caught early enough.<\/p>\n<p>He hadn't let the thought finish. He'd made himself watch the fields instead until the plane began its descent.<\/p>\n<p>She looked smaller in the hospital bed than he'd ever seen her look anywhere — an IV taped to the back of one hand, her face pale in a way that made his chest tighten the moment he saw her through the ward's glass door.<\/p>\n<p>The ward smelled of antiseptic and reheated tea, and somewhere down the corridor a trolley's wheel squeaked with each turn, rhythmic, uneven, the only sound loud enough to cut through the low hum of machines.<\/p>\n<p>Her eyes went wide the instant she saw him, disbelief crossing her face first, as though her mind needed a full second to catch up to what her eyes were telling her. \"Vikram?\" She said his name like a question, like she half expected the doorway to prove her wrong. Then, only after that first stunned beat passed, something in her whole face broke open into relief, and she said, \"You didn't need to come,\" though something in her voice betrayed how glad she actually was that he had. \"You were in Manipur, Vikram. That's twenty-five hundred kilometres. How did you even—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Air Force flight to Delhi. I asked around, got lucky with a seat.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Twenty-five hundred kilometres,\" she repeated, something in her voice catching, her eyes going bright in a way that had nothing to do with the fever. \"You flew twenty-five hundred kilometres because I was sick.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It's not quite that far,\" he said, a small, tired smile finally breaking through. \"Fifteen hundred, roughly, by air. The road distance is longer, but nobody drives from Manipur.\"<\/p>\n<p>She stared at him a moment, and then, despite everything — the IV, the fever, three days of frightened solitude — she laughed, a real laugh, her whole face lighting up with it. \"Fifteen hundred. You flew fifteen hundred kilometres to sit in a hospital chair next to my bed, and your only correction is the exact distance.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Accuracy matters to me. Even now.\" He shrugged, feigning great seriousness. \"Someone has to maintain standards while you lie there charming an entire ward of nurses with your fever.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I have charmed exactly one nurse, and only because she thought I was being brave. I was mostly just annoyed at the drip.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You're impossible.\" But she was still smiling, something in her whole body loosening at the sight of him, the way a held breath finally lets go, and she reached for his hand properly, needing the contact more than she'd admit out loud. \"Thank you for the fifteen hundred kilometres, then.\"<\/p>\n<p>He pulled a chair close to the bed and sat, taking her hand carefully in both of his. \"Tell me honestly. How bad was it, actually, before they got it under control?\"<\/p>\n<p>She hesitated, and then, slowly, told him the truth — the fever that had spiked dangerously high the first night, the doctors' initial uncertainty, the fear she'd sat with alone in that ward for hours before anyone reached her family. He listened without interrupting, his thumb moving slowly across the back of her hand, the small motion the only thing either of them needed to say for a while.<\/p>\n<p>\"I was frightened,\" she admitted, quieter now. \"More frightened than I let anyone see. I kept thinking about you, actually — wondering if this was even a fraction of what you feel, every time you're somewhere I can't reach you, not knowing if you're safe.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It's exactly that,\" he said. \"Every single time.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I never understood it properly until now.\" She squeezed his hand. \"I'm sorry. For every time I made it seem simple, when you'd call and I'd just say 'be careful' like those words meant anything against what you were actually facing.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't need to apologise for that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I do, a little. Because now I know what it costs, waiting like that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Come here,\" she said quietly, patting the narrow space beside her on the bed. \"Sit properly. That chair looks uncomfortable and I don't want you across the room right now.\"<\/p>\n<p>He moved carefully onto the edge of the bed, mindful of the IV line, and she shifted until her head found his lap, the same way she'd once done on a park bench, though this time her body carried a fragility that was illness, not mere exhaustion.<\/p>\n<p>It was only once she was settled against him, her cheek against his thigh, that the tears finally came. Quiet at first, then harder, her shoulders shaking slightly as everything she'd been holding together for three days alone finally found somewhere safe enough to let go — the way water finds the one low place in a field and simply gives itself over to gravity.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't say anything right away. He simply cradled her head gently in one hand, and let his fingers move slowly through her hair, the same unhurried rhythm she'd once used on him, and waited, patient, for the crying to run its course.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm sorry,\" she said eventually, her voice thick. \"I don't know why it's hitting me now, of all times, when the worst of it is already over.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't need a reason. You held it together alone for three days. You're allowed to stop holding it together now that you don't have to.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet a while, letting his hand keep moving through her hair, her breathing slowly steadying.<\/p>\n<p>\"I will always be there when you need me,\" he said softly, into the quiet. \"Wherever I am. Whatever it takes to get to you. I need you to actually believe that, Naina, not just hear it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I believe it,\" she said, her voice smaller than he was used to hearing it. \"I don't think I fully understood it until today. But I believe it now.\"<\/p>\n<p>He kept his hand moving gently through her hair, and she let herself stay exactly where she was, held and unhurried, until the tears finally stopped and something quieter, steadier, settled in to take their place, the way a room settles once a storm has finally passed over it.<\/p>\n<p>He stayed the next two days without once suggesting he leave, dozing badly in the cushioned visitor's chair by her bed, until the fever broke for good and the doctors finally cleared her to go home.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't tell her, that night, about the thought he'd had somewhere over Uttar Pradesh. Some fears were easier carried alone a little longer, even now.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 19, "title": "Chapter 17 — Sohna Fort", "content": "<h1>Chapter 17 — Sohna Fort<\/h1>\n<p>She'd been discharged from the hospital a week earlier, pronounced fully fit after a course of antibiotics and three days of observation nobody had strictly needed to extend but had anyway, out of the caution hospitals reserve when someone has flown fifteen hundred kilometres just to sit beside the bed.<\/p>\n<p>His leave was ending in two days. Naina called that evening, her voice carrying an urgency neither of them bothered disguising.<\/p>\n<p>\"Take me somewhere,\" she said. \"I don't care where. I just need one more day that isn't a hospital ward or a goodbye already half-arrived.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Sohna Fort,\" he said. \"You mentioned it once, months ago. Said you'd always meant to see it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Tomorrow, then. Before either of us has to think about anything else.\"<\/p>\n<p>With his leave nearly gone and both of them still carrying the rawness of a week that had asked too much of each of them, she wanted one more day that felt like it belonged only to them.<\/p>\n<p>\"It'll be quiet,\" she said, over the phone that same night. \"No crowds on a weekday. Just the two of us and a few hundred years of somebody else's ambition.\"<\/p>\n<p>They set out mid-morning under a sky the colour of old newspaper, and the fort, when they reached it, was as empty as she'd promised — weathered sandstone walls rising out of scrub grass, a single distracted caretaker who sold them two tickets and went back to whatever he'd been doing, no other visitor in sight.<\/p>\n<p>Whole sections of the outer wall had fallen long ago, leaving gaps you could walk straight through without ever finding a gate — as though the fort had simply given up defending itself somewhere along the way, and let the world walk in.<\/p>\n<p>They wandered without any real plan, climbing narrow stone stairs worn smooth by centuries of other feet, pausing in doorways that opened onto small empty courtyards.<\/p>\n<p>\"Someone lived their whole life here,\" she said, standing in what had once been a large hall, now open to the sky where the roof had long since given up. \"Married here, probably. Had children here. Worried about money, or in-laws, or whether their husband loved them the way they wanted to be loved. And now it's just this. Walls and grass and two strangers thinking about it centuries later.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's a heavy thought for a Tuesday.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It steadies me, actually.\" She turned to look at him. \"Whatever feels large to us right now — it puts it in some kind of proportion.\"<\/p>\n<p>The first drop of rain hit the back of his hand before he could answer.<\/p>\n<p>It came on fast — no gradual build, just a sudden, heavy downpour, dust turning to mud within a minute. Naina laughed, startled, and grabbed his sleeve, pulling him toward the nearest doorway, both of them soaked through by the time they reached it.<\/p>\n<p>The room they ducked into was small and roofed, one of the few in the fort that had survived whole — narrow stone walls, a single high window, the smell of wet earth rising up as the rain hammered outside.<\/p>\n<p>\"Well,\" she said, wringing out the end of her dupatta, breathless. \"That escalated.\" She looked around the small room, then back at him, and something in her face changed — the laughter still there, but underneath it something quieter, more direct. \"We're stuck here a while, I think.\"<\/p>\n<p>There was nowhere to sit except the stone floor, so they sat there, backs against the wall, close enough that their shoulders touched, the rain settling into a steady rhythm beyond the doorway.<\/p>\n<p>\"This isn't how I pictured today going,\" Vikram said.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not upset about it.\" She rested her head back against the stone. \"There's something about being forced to stop that I don't get very often. My whole life is built around not stopping.\" She glanced at him. \"You're one of the only things in my life right now that isn't a demand on me. I don't fully know what to call that yet.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't have to call it anything today.\"<\/p>\n<p>She turned toward him then, and there was nothing careful left in the way she looked at him — the guardedness that had taken months to wear down was simply gone, and what was left underneath it was plain and unhidden.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not thinking about being sure anymore,\" she said quietly. \"I've been sure for weeks. I was only waiting for the right moment to stop pretending I wasn't.\"<\/p>\n<p>He reached up and brushed a strand of wet hair back from her face, and she caught his hand there, holding it against her cheek for a moment before turning her face into his palm.<\/p>\n<p>His lips found hers without either of them saying anything to invite it, the same way the rain outside hadn't asked permission before it came. There was no caution left in it, no held-back edge — only the plain certainty of something that had been circling both of them for months, finally landing somewhere solid.<\/p>\n<p>He could feel her heartbeat where his hand rested against her collarbone, quick and unhidden, matching the one going just as fast under his own ribs, as if both of them had simply stopped pretending to keep separate time. Every small shift of her body against his said what neither of them had said out loud yet: her hands finding the front of his shirt and holding on, his coming up to frame her face, the two of them moving now without needing to think about it, the way people only move when the thinking has already been done somewhere underneath.<\/p>\n<p>The rain had only ever been the spark. What caught was something that had been sitting dry and ready in both of them for weeks, and once it caught, there was no putting it out again, no part of either of them that wanted to.<\/p>\n<p>For a long moment neither of them thought about the stone floor or the fort or the four hundred years of exactly this kind of thing it had likely already witnessed. She made a small sound against him when the kiss deepened, and he felt it go through him like something that had been held too carefully, for too long, finally let go.<\/p>\n<p>\"Naina—\" he started, pulling back slightly, some old instinct still checking itself.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't stop to ask if I'm sure again,\" she said, breathless, her forehead against his. \"I already told you. I've been sure.\"<\/p>\n<p>He kissed her again, slower this time but no less certain, and she pressed closer, one hand sliding up into his damp hair. When he finally moved his mouth from hers to the line of her jaw, then lower, to the wet, salt-and-rain-tasting skin of her throat, she tipped her head back against the stone wall and let out a breath that had nothing careful left in it at all.<\/p>\n<p>His hands moved along her sides, over the soaked cotton of her kameez, feeling the shape of her through it, and she arched into the touch rather than away from it, her own hands working at the buttons of his shirt, impatient now in a way she'd never let herself be before. He caught her wrists gently, not to stop her, only to slow her, and pressed a kiss to the inside of one before letting go.<\/p>\n<p>\"Slow,\" he murmured against her skin. \"You asked me for slow, remember. Even now.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Even now,\" she agreed, though her voice had gone unsteady. \"Slow. Just — don't stop.\"<\/p>\n<p>He held onto that word — slow — like a rope, because some part of him understood, even now, that wanting her this much and still choosing to wait was its own kind of promise, more honest than anything he could have said out loud.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't stop. His mouth found the curve of her shoulder where her kameez had slipped, his hands mapping her waist, her back, learning her by touch the way he'd learned her face over months of looking. She held onto him like something she'd been waiting a long time to be allowed to hold, her breath coming quick and uneven against his ear, her fingers pressed hard into his shoulders when his mouth found a particular spot at her throat that made her whole body go still and then shiver.<\/p>\n<p>Then, for a moment, neither of them breathed at all.<\/p>\n<p>They didn't go further than that. There was a line neither of them named out loud, and both of them understood it was still there even as everything up to it had come undone — his shirt half open, her dupatta long since abandoned on the stone floor, both of them breathing hard, foreheads together, the rain still coming down steady beyond the doorway.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want you,\" she said quietly, plainly, no performance in it at all. \"Not today. Not on a stone floor in someone else's ruin. But soon. I need you to know that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know,\" he said. \"I want that too. When it's right.\"<\/p>\n<p>She nodded against him. They stayed there a long while after that, wrapped around each other on the cold stone floor, her cheek against his bare collarbone, his hand slow and steady down the length of her damp hair.<\/p>\n<p>Both of them let the last of the urgency settle into something quieter — not finished, only paused, the way the whole of that year had taught them both to be patient with the things that mattered most.<\/p>\n<p>The rain thinned eventually, softening into something gentler, and by the time they finally stepped back out into the washed grey light, the fort around them looked entirely different — puddled, gleaming, the dust settled, the whole place smelling of wet stone and new growth. Neither of them said much on the walk back to the car. There wasn't much left that needed saying. On the drive home, she sat close against his side rather than across the seat, her hand resting on his knee the whole way, and neither of them moved to change that until they reached the city again.<\/p>\n<p>Five days from now, his leave would end again, and this time there was no telling how long the next one would be in coming. Neither of them said it out loud. But it rode back to the city with them all the same, quiet and unacknowledged, tucked somewhere between her hand and his knee.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 20, "title": "Chapter 18 — The Line They Finally Crossed", "content": "<h1>Chapter 18 — The Line They Finally Crossed<\/h1>\n<p>The leave that made it possible came through almost by accident — five clear days, no duty in between, both of them realising at the same time that this was the longest uninterrupted stretch they'd had since the whole thing began.<\/p>\n<p>\"Himachal,\" Naina said, over the phone, already sounding like she'd left. \"Not a hill station full of honeymooners taking selfies. Somewhere smaller. I have a place in mind.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll bring the car,\" he said. \"Six hours is a long drive otherwise.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"No car. I want the bike.\" She said it like it wasn't up for discussion. \"I've spent enough of my life being driven places by people who won't let me feel the road under me. I want to actually travel there, not just arrive.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't argue. He'd learned, by now, that arguing with a decision Naina had already made in her head cost more than it was worth.<\/p>\n<p>They left Gurugram before sunrise, the city still half-asleep around them, and settled into the long, six-hour stretch north with the easy rhythm of two people who didn't need to fill every hour with conversation. She rode behind him with her arms loose around his waist on the flat stretches and tighter through the hills once the road started climbing, her forehead occasionally resting lightly against his shoulder when she leaned to look at something off to the side.<\/p>\n<p>They stopped once, a couple of hours out of the city, at a dhaba run by a family that had clearly been feeding travellers on this road for decades. A woman worked the tandoor, her son took orders, and an old man dozed on a charpai in the shade, waking only to nod at them as they sat down.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram ordered without asking, the way he'd learned to over the weeks, and the dal came thick, the roti fresh off the tandoor, and for a while neither of them said much, just eating, the highway noise a steady hum behind them.<\/p>\n<p>\"My grandmother used to say something,\" Naina said eventually, breaking a piece of roti in half. \"That you can tell everything about a person by how they eat food that isn't fancy. Whether they're grateful for it, or whether they're only ever really comfortable with things that impress people.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"What does today tell you, then?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That you're the kind of man who says thank you to the boy who refills the water, even though he's twelve and doesn't expect it.\" She looked at him for a moment, something quiet and considering in her face. \"My grandmother told me once, years before she passed, to look for exactly this in a man — someone who thanks people the way you just did, without thinking about it. A pure heart, she called it.\" She laughed softly. \"I didn't understand what she meant until just now, watching you.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram thought of his own grandmother, briefly, a woman who had died before he was old enough to properly remember her, and found himself wishing, for the first time in years, that she'd lived long enough to sit across a dhaba table and form an opinion of a woman like Naina.<\/p>\n<p>\"I hope I'd have earned it,\" he said.<\/p>\n<p>\"You would have. I have a good sense for these things.\" She finished the last of her roti and stood, brushing crumbs from her kurta. \"Come on. We still have four hours, and I want to reach before dark.\"<\/p>\n<p>He paid before she could reach for her purse, a small, quiet victory he allowed himself, and they walked back to the bike together, unhurried, the mountains still a long way off but somehow, in that moment, already a little closer than they had been an hour before.<\/p>\n<p>The last stretch of road did exactly what a long day on a bike usually does to a body — left them both stiff, windburnt, glad to finally be arriving somewhere. The place turned out to be Shoja, a small village in the Seraj Valley Vikram had never heard of before Naina mentioned it, reached by a road that spent its last stretch narrowing steadily into something more suggestion than surface. They arrived as the light was starting to go gold and long, the village clinging to a fold in the mountain — wood-and-stone houses stacked up the slope, deodar and pine forest climbing away above the last rooftops toward peaks still holding snow.<\/p>\n<p>\"It's beautiful,\" Naina said, standing at the small balcony rail of the guesthouse once they'd finally parked the bike and hauled their bags up. \"I didn't expect it to actually be this beautiful. I chose it off a photograph. Photographs lie constantly.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"This one didn't.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Neither did six hours on a bike, apparently. My whole body is one long complaint and I still don't regret a minute of it.\"<\/p>\n<p>That first night, over dinner at a low table, Naina announced her plan to walk to the ridge the next day — four hours up, two back.<\/p>\n<p>\"Four hours up a mountain doesn't sound like a holiday to me,\" Vikram said. \"Not after today.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It sounds exactly like a holiday to me. You've spent your whole career walking up mountains because someone ordered you to. I want to know what it feels like to do it because I actually want to see what's at the top.\"<\/p>\n<p>Later, at the small window, looking out at a sky gone entirely dark and thick with stars neither of them ever saw properly in a city, she said, unprompted, \"I don't want this to be a holiday we talk about like it was the last good thing. I want it to be the first of many.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm hoping for the same thing,\" he said.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>The climb began before full light, the sky still holding onto the last of its indigo, the air sharp enough to sting in the lungs with every early breath. Dew clung to the grass along the first switchbacks, soaking the hems of their trousers before the sun had even cleared the ridgeline. Somewhere above them a lone bird call kept repeating itself, thin and insistent, echoing off slopes still deep in shadow.<\/p>\n<p>The trail climbed through deodar first, the trunks black-green in the half-light, needles carpeting the path soft enough to muffle their own footsteps. Slowly, as the light strengthened and the trees began to thin, the deodar gave way to open scrub and bare rock, the cold thinning out along with the trees until it turned into something cleaner, colder, almost metallic against the skin.<\/p>\n<p>Naina set a pace that surprised him — steady, unhurried, a pace her father had taught her on a different range, years before. They stopped once to eat parathas on a flat rock with the whole valley spread below them, the village already reduced to a scatter of small roofs and smoke.<\/p>\n<p>She talked about how small everything looked from up there — the village, the road, even the things that felt enormous when you were standing in the middle of them.<\/p>\n<p>The view, when it finally came at the ridge, arrived all at once: an entire range unfolding in front of them, peak after white peak stretching toward a hazy horizon, the wind carrying a cold that had clearly travelled a long way to reach them.<\/p>\n<p>There was no sound at all except that wind moving through the pass, threading between rocks with a low, constant hum that felt older than either of them standing there listening to it.<\/p>\n<p>They sat for the better part of an hour, sharing the last of a thermos of tea gone lukewarm in the cold, its steam thinning quickly into the mountain air. Naina shifted, leaning back until her head found his shoulder, then settled lower, resting against his chest as they both looked out at the range instead of at each other.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in the quiet, Vikram caught the faint, resinous smell of pine drifting down from higher on the slope, and something in his chest tightened unexpectedly.<\/p>\n<p>\"Doda,\" he said, almost to himself.<\/p>\n<p>\"What?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Doda district, in Kashmir. Where I did most of my operations, over the years. The pine smelled exactly like this.\" He breathed it in again, deliberately this time. \"I hadn't thought about that posting in months. The smell just brought it straight back.\"<\/p>\n<p>She lifted her head slightly, breathing in herself, brow furrowed. \"I don't smell anything unusual.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You will, someday. Maybe not here. Maybe somewhere else entirely, years from now, when you least expect it. But once this exact smell finds you a second time, you'll know it instantly — and you'll know, in that same instant, that it's found its way back to you from me.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet a moment, something amused settling into her expression. \"That's a strange thing to promise a smell.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Maybe. But some promises don't need a place attached to them. Just a scent, and enough time.\"<\/p>\n<p>She settled back against his chest, something quieter than amusement now in the way she exhaled. \"Then I'll wait for it. However long it takes to reach me.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Tell me something true,\" she said. \"Not something you've told the others as a joke. Something you've actually never said out loud.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a moment, one hand coming up to rest against her hair, moving slowly through it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I used to think love was something that happened to other people,\" he said eventually. \"Something arranged, managed, settled into rather than fallen into. I didn't think I'd ever feel the second kind.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"And now?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Now I think I was wrong about most of what I believed before you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I used to think I was too difficult to be loved properly,\" she said quietly, after a while. \"My mother used to say it like a warning — soften yourself, Naina, or you'll frighten away anyone decent. I spent years believing her.\" She tipped her head back to look up at him. \"You never once asked me to soften anything.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I never wanted you softer. I wanted you exactly the way you already were.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's the truest thing anyone's ever said to me,\" she said, and there was nothing sharp in her voice at all. \"I keep waiting for you to change your mind about that. Every week that you don't, I trust it a little more.\"<\/p>\n<p>They stayed like that a long while, her head against his chest, his hand slow and unhurried in her hair, the whole range spread out silent and white in front of them, neither of them in any hurry to fill the quiet with anything more than what had already been said.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>They came down from the ridge in the middle of the afternoon, legs tired in the good way, and by the time they reached the guesthouse again the light was starting to turn gold over the valley. Naina went quiet on the last stretch of the walk down, quieter than the climb itself had made her, and Vikram noticed it without saying anything, the way he'd learned to notice her moods without always needing an explanation for them straight away.<\/p>\n<p>It was over dinner, at the same low table as the night before, that she finally said what had been sitting with her since the ridge.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've been thinking,\" she said, setting her cup down. \"About what I said at the fort, before we left Delhi.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I remember.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I wasn't just saying it in the moment. I want you to know that. I've thought about it properly since then, not just once, many times.\" She looked at him, steady. \"I'm ready. I wanted to tell you plainly, not leave you guessing, and I wanted to tell you here, somewhere far from everything ordinary, so it would feel like something we chose rather than something that just happened to us.\"<\/p>\n<p>He set his own cup down. \"Naina—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Let me finish, or I'll lose my nerve.\" She took a breath. \"I know what this means. Not just the physical part. My family doesn't treat this lightly, and neither do I. Once I do this, I can't undo it or pretend it didn't happen. I'm not the kind of woman who does this and walks away unchanged. So I need you to know — if we do this, it's not casual for me. I've decided you're the one I want to give this to, whatever comes after.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I understand what it means to you,\" he said quietly. \"I wouldn't want anything less serious from you either.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know you wouldn't. That's why I trust you enough to say this out loud, up here, with nobody around to hear me be this honest.\"<\/p>\n<p>They didn't rush toward it after that. The rest of the evening moved slower than usual, both of them aware that something was coming and neither wanting to arrive at it carelessly. He'd noticed, the day before, a small stall near the guesthouse selling marigolds strung for the local temple, and he slipped out after dinner while she was washing up, and came back with a small loose bunch of them, nothing elaborate, and set them on the table in their room without saying anything.<\/p>\n<p>She noticed them the moment she came in, and something in her face softened.<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't have to do that,\" she said.<\/p>\n<p>\"I wanted the room to feel like more than just a room.\"<\/p>\n<p>She looked at the flowers, then at him. \"It already does. Even without them.\"<\/p>\n<p>They sat on the edge of the bed for a long while first, not doing anything beyond holding hands, talking about small things: her family, a story from her childhood he hadn't heard before, the way her father used to sing off-key on long car drives, the strange, unfamiliar sound the mountain made at night that neither of them could name properly.<\/p>\n<p>He understood this was her way of arriving slowly, letting her own nerves settle through ordinary talk before either of them said out loud why they were really there.<\/p>\n<p>\"I keep thinking about something my mother told me once,\" she said eventually, still holding his hand. \"That the first time should be with someone who makes you feel safe enough to be foolish in front of them. I didn't understand it when she said it. I think I understand it now.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Do you feel that? Safe?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I feel exactly that,\" she said. \"That's why I'm here, and not somewhere pretending to be braver than I am.\"<\/p>\n<p>He reached up and brushed a strand of hair back from her face, the same small thing he'd done at the fort weeks earlier. This time she leaned fully into his palm instead of just catching his hand there.<\/p>\n<p>\"We don't have to,\" he said again, softer now. \"Not tonight. Not ever, if you don't want it that way.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I want it,\" she said. \"I've wanted it. I just — I haven't. Before. You know that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then don't treat me like glass. And don't rush me like you're afraid I'll change my mind.\" A breath. \"I'm not changing my mind.\"<\/p>\n<p>They came together slowly, without any ceremony, everything unfolding at whatever pace she needed it to. He didn't look at her like a man who had won something. He looked at her the way a man looks when he knows he's being trusted with a first, and that trust matters more than the wanting does — his hands moving over her the way a man moves when he understands he's holding something rare, and chooses, deliberately, not to hurry it.<\/p>\n<p>She watched his face the whole time, searching it the way she searched most things, and found nothing there but tenderness, plain and unhidden, asking nothing of her except to be exactly as nervous and as certain as she actually was.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in that slow, wordless closeness, whatever fear either of them had carried into the room simply ran out of places left to hide.<\/p>\n<p>He touched her the way a man touches something made of glass he already knows could break — not out of fear, not out of worry, but out of care, reading her not only through her eyes but through his own hands, every small change in her breath and the give of her body telling him more, in that quiet, than any word could have.<\/p>\n<p>\"Cold?\" he asked, once there was nothing left between them but that same quiet tenderness, the mountain air still cool even indoors.<\/p>\n<p>\"A little. Not from the room.\"<\/p>\n<p>He pulled the sheet back, got her under it, and joined her there until she laughed once, soft and surprised at her own voice, and pulled him closer.<\/p>\n<p>For a moment neither of them moved, just the closeness of two people who had waited a long time to be exactly here, her cheek against his neck, his own pulse loud in his ears. Then she pulled him closer, and whatever careful distance either of them had been holding onto simply gave way.<\/p>\n<p>His lips found hers slow at first, one hand cradling the back of her head, and she answered with a warmth that surprised them both — her fingers finding his shoulders, a small sound escaping her that had nothing careful left in it. When something hurt, she told him, and he stopped, and waited, forehead pressed to hers, until the hurt eased into something gentler before he moved again. She held his gaze, unashamed, and pulled him back down to her.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't stop,\" she said, low, against his ear. \"I want all of it. I don't want you gentle out of fear. I want you close.\"<\/p>\n<p>He gave her that. What passed between them after was slow, but no longer cautious — unhurried, both of them finding a rhythm that belonged only to the two of them, her hands never quite still against his back. When they were finally as close as two people can be, watching her face the whole time, she said his name against his shoulder, and once, quietly, she said the word love too, like it had simply slipped out at the truest moment and she hadn't tried to stop it.<\/p>\n<p>They stayed joined together afterward, both of them breathing hard, her fingers moving along his shoulder blades like she was checking that he was still there.<\/p>\n<p>\"You all right?\" he asked.<\/p>\n<p>She thought about it properly, the way she thought about most things. \"Sore. Different. Glad.\" A pause. \"It didn't feel stolen. It felt like we finally got somewhere we'd been walking toward since a bench under a gulmohar tree.\"<\/p>\n<p>He kissed her forehead for that. She curled into him more fully, her cheek against his chest, and for a while neither of them said anything, just breathing together, the marigolds on the table filling the room with a faint, sweet smell.<\/p>\n<p>\"I meant what I said earlier,\" she said eventually, half into his skin. \"About giving this to you because I decided you were the one. I don't regret it. I don't think I ever will.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Neither will I,\" he said. \"I won't forget what tonight cost you to give me, or how carefully you gave it anyway.\"<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>In the morning, she woke before he did, and he found her, when his eyes opened, already sitting up beside him with a damp cloth, running it gently along his shoulder where her nails had marked him the night before.<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't have to do that,\" he said, voice still rough with sleep.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to.\" She continued, unhurried, wiping along his collarbone, his chest, the same careful attention he'd once shown her. \"You took care of me last night. It's my turn this morning.\"<\/p>\n<p>He caught her wrist gently when she finished, pulling her back down against him instead of letting her get up. \"Five more minutes,\" he said, his voice still low and unhurried.<\/p>\n<p>She laughed, soft and warm, and let herself be pulled, but instead of settling quietly into his arms the way she had the night before, she found his hand had already laced through hers, tightening, drawing her fully against him until there was no space left between them at all.<\/p>\n<p>\"Vikram.\" She smiled against his jaw, feeling exactly what five more minutes actually meant. \"What do you want?\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't answer with words. He only smiled back, unhurried, certain, and let her read the answer plainly enough on his face.<\/p>\n<p>She didn't need to be asked twice. Whatever tiredness the climb and the night before had left in her body gave way easily to something else entirely — she wanted this too, wanted one more morning of him before the world and his uniform came to take him back.<\/p>\n<p>This time there was none of the careful, deliberate slowness of the night before. He already knew her now — every place that made her breath catch, every touch that undid her faster than the last — and there was no hesitation left in his hands, no question in them either, only the sure, urgent want of a man who finally knew exactly what he was reaching for. She met him with the same urgency, her nails finding his back this time instead of just his shoulders, pressing harder than they had the night before, as though whatever careful restraint she'd started with had already burned off somewhere in the last few minutes. Her breath came fast and unguarded against his ear, neither of them holding back the way they'd both instinctively held back the first time.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in the middle of it, she started saying his name — just that, over and over, nothing else, as though in that moment it was the only word left in her entire vocabulary, the only word the universe had bothered giving her. <i>Vikram.<\/i> Not a sentence. Not even really a call for anything. Just his name, said the way a person says the one true thing they still have left to say when everything else has gone quiet.<\/p>\n<p>It was rougher than the night before, faster, less careful — and she wanted it exactly that way, telling him so without needing any other words, her body answering his with an eagerness that left no room for doubt in either of them.<\/p>\n<p>Afterward, breathless, tangled together in the narrow bed, she pressed her smile into his shoulder. \"I didn't know mornings with you could feel like that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Neither did I,\" he admitted, \"until about twenty minutes ago.\"<\/p>\n<p>She laughed properly this time, and curled fully into him, and they stayed like that a while longer, tangled together under the blanket, neither of them in any hurry to let the morning fully arrive.<\/p>\n<p>There was no pretending anymore, even to himself, that this was a relationship running alongside the rest of his life. This had become the centre of it.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in the packing that evening, folding the same kurta he'd worn to a wedding weeks earlier, he found himself already counting the days until his next leave. Not the way a man counts down a sentence, but the way he used to count days as a boy before Diwali — half-impatient, half-afraid of wishing time away too fast to notice it properly.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't mention it to her. He simply packed a little slower than he needed to, and let himself have the thought a while longer before the bag was finally zipped shut.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 21, "title": "Chapter 19 — I Am Waiting", "content": "<h1>Chapter 19 — I Am Waiting<\/h1>\n<p>The evening before he was due back on duty, Naina insisted on coming to the station herself, though he'd told her more than once she didn't have to.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not staying home while you leave,\" she said. \"I've decided. Don't argue with me about this one thing.\"<\/p>\n<p>They stood on the platform together, twenty minutes still left before the train came, the ordinary chaos of the station moving around them like something happening to other people, porters and vendors and families rushing past two people standing very still in the middle of it all.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know how I'm going to manage without you at all,\" she said.<\/p>\n<p>\"You'll manage. You've managed harder things than this.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's not the same as wanting to.\" She reached out and straightened his collar, a small, unnecessary thing that gave her hands something to do besides shake. \"I'm not going anywhere. Whatever happens between now and the next time you get leave — I'll wait.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Whatever happens,\" he said quietly, \"remember that. I will come back.\"<\/p>\n<p>The train pulled in with its usual noise, brakes screeching against the rails and the shouted names of stops still to come. He pulled her into a tight hug instead of anything else, both arms wrapping fully around her, holding on a long moment longer than the platform's chaos really allowed for, her face pressed into his shoulder, her hands fisted in the back of his shirt like letting go too soon might somehow make the leaving more real.<\/p>\n<p>\"Come back alive,\" she said, her voice thick against him. \"That's all I'm asking. Come back alive.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I will.\"<\/p>\n<p>He boarded, and found a window, and she stood on the platform as the train began to move, her hand raised, not quite waving, just held there in the space between them until the distance made it meaningless, the train shrinking down the track until it was only a sound, and then not even that.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>For fifteen days, there was nothing.<\/p>\n<p>She called his number the day after his last call, expecting the usual ring, and got only silence, flat and unbroken, the kind that gives no information at all, only the absence of it. She called again the next day, and the day after that, each attempt landing in the same dead space, telling her nothing about whether the line itself had simply failed or whether something far worse sat behind the quiet.<\/p>\n<p>By the fifth day, food had stopped tasting like anything worth finishing. By the eighth, sleep had become a place she visited only in fragments, waking every few hours certain her phone had rung when it hadn't. His last words — <i>whatever happens, remember that<\/i> — sat lodged somewhere beneath her ribs, refusing to soften into the ordinary reassurance he'd clearly meant them as.<\/p>\n<p>On the twelfth evening, something in her finally gave way beneath the weight of not knowing.<\/p>\n<p>The flat was silent in the particular way a place goes silent when its only other voice has been missing two weeks. No traffic sound reached up from the street at that hour, no fan ran — just the small, steady tick of the kitchen clock and her own breathing, too loud in a room built for two people and currently holding only one.<\/p>\n<p>She sat at her small desk with a blank sheet of paper in front of her, a single lamp throwing a narrow circle of light over the page and leaving the rest of the room in shadow, and reached, almost without deciding to, for the small blade she kept for sharpening pencils. She pressed it lightly to the pad of her thumb — barely a sting, barely anything at all measured against fifteen days of a different, far less locatable pain — and dipped the tip of her pen into the small bead of blood that rose there.<\/p>\n<p><i>I am waiting.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>You promised me you will come back.<\/i><\/p>\n<p><i>Come back alive, Vikram.<\/i><\/p>\n<p>The blood thinned before she could write more, and she didn't press the cut again to draw out any further. She sealed the envelope in ordinary ink after that — his name, his unit, written properly, steadily, the outward calm of good handwriting covering whatever had actually happened on the page inside — and posted it that same night, uncertain, even as she did, whether it would ever find its way to him at all.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>He came back on the fifteenth day, and called her the moment he was permitted to.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm safe,\" he said, before she could even ask. \"Not a scratch on me.\"<\/p>\n<p>Whatever had been holding her together for fifteen days simply released at the sound of his voice. Both of them breathed into a silence that finally meant something good instead of something feared, neither needing to fill it with more words than that for a long moment — the relief settling over the line like something arriving after a long, uncertain flight.<\/p>\n<p>\"I have to explain the silence,\" he said, once he trusted his own voice again. \"I was moved near the Myanmar border, without much warning, right after I reported back. No signal reaches that terrain, and no contact with anyone outside the unit was permitted for the whole stretch. I couldn't even send word that I'd gone dark. I'm sorry, Naina. I know exactly what fifteen days of nothing must have done to you, not knowing any of that.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her letter reached him seven days later, delayed through whatever slow channel Army post always travelled — arriving long after he already knew she was safe, and she already knew he was. He opened it standing outside the mess in the late afternoon light, and went very still the moment he saw the faint, rust-brown lines threading through her handwriting, understanding, before he'd even finished reading, exactly what he was looking at.<\/p>\n<p>Something in his chest went cold, and then, almost immediately, furious at himself. Fifteen days he'd been unreachable through no fault of his own, and somewhere in that silence she had sat alone with a blade and a blank page, and there had been nothing he could have done differently. That helplessness sat far worse in him than any wound he'd have taken himself instead.<\/p>\n<p>He called her that same evening, his voice tighter than he meant it to be.<\/p>\n<p>\"Your letter came today,\" he said. \"Naina — you wrote it in blood. Why would you hurt yourself like that?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It's nothing,\" she said quickly, too quickly. \"Physically, I mean. A pinprick. It didn't really hurt, not in any way that actually matters.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then why?\"<\/p>\n<p>She went quiet, and when she finally answered, her voice had lost its usual quick certainty. \"Because the pain I was actually carrying wasn't physical, Vikram. Fifteen days of not knowing hurt so much deeper than anything a blade could ever manage on its own. I think some part of me needed the outside of me to finally match what was already happening underneath. I don't know how to explain it better than that. I'm not sure it's the kind of thing that explains well in words at all.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her voice broke properly then, the delayed shock of the whole ordeal finally catching up to her all at once, and she cried — not for the thumb, barely healed by now, but for every hour of the fifteen days she'd had to carry silently, alone, with no one to hand any of it to.<\/p>\n<p>He stayed on the line through all of it, saying little, simply present in the only way the distance between them still allowed, letting his voice be the steady thing she could hold onto until the worst of it passed.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm okay now,\" she said eventually, quieter, steadier. \"I just needed you to know what those fifteen days actually did to me. Not the calm version I'd have given anyone else. The real one.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know now,\" he said softly. \"I won't forget it. Not the letter. Not what it cost you to write it.\"<\/p>\n<p>He folded the letter small afterward and kept it in his chest pocket, the same pocket Reddy had once kept a photograph in, soft at the corners from being carried too long — a girl who never got her wedding, a promise that never got kept. Vikram didn't say that part out loud, even to himself, not fully. He only knew, closing the flap of the pocket over it, that he intended to carry this one differently.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 22, "title": "Chapter 20 — Her Flat, His Leave", "content": "<h1>Chapter 20 — Her Flat, His Leave<\/h1>\n<p>The next leave came through two months after the operation — shorter this time, only four days, but four days felt like a fortune compared to the fifteen days of silence that had come before it.<\/p>\n<p>Naina met him at Delhi airport this time too, though the mood was entirely different from the platform where she'd said goodbye months earlier. She didn't cry. She simply walked straight into him the moment he came through the arrivals gate, duffel bag still over one shoulder, arms around his neck, holding on a beat longer than a normal greeting required.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're actually here,\" she said, into his shoulder. \"I keep needing to check that, every time.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm here. Four whole days.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Four days I intend to hoard like they're gold.\"<\/p>\n<p>The flat was a proper two-bedroom — a small living room with a kitchenette off to one side, a bedroom she used and a second, smaller one that had become more storage than anything else. Naina had lived in it for two years by then, and the whole space had become entirely her own, the way a place slowly absorbs its owner until the two become hard to tell apart.<\/p>\n<p>\"It's not much,\" she said, the first time he came properly, not just to drop her back after an evening out but to stay through an actual leave.<\/p>\n<p>\"It's yours,\" Vikram said. \"That makes it more than enough.\"<\/p>\n<p>What struck him most, that first proper stay, was how unremarkable it all felt — unremarkable the way a life felt when it had stopped needing to be dramatic to matter.<\/p>\n<p>She made tea in the mornings without asking if he wanted any, simply making enough for two because two was who was there. He picked up a habit of drying the dishes while she washed, neither of them ever discussing whose job that was. It simply became the shape of things by the second morning — the quiet architecture of two people learning to share small tasks without needing to negotiate them.<\/p>\n<p>The second evening, she cooked properly — a paneer curry she'd learned from her mother and rarely bothered making for just herself.<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't have to do all this,\" he said, watching her work.<\/p>\n<p>\"I wanted to. There's a difference between cooking because you're hungry and cooking because you want someone to taste something you're proud of.\"<\/p>\n<p>They ate sitting cross-legged on the floor, plates balanced on their knees, an old Hindi film playing quietly in the background, the small flat filled with the warm, ordinary smell of a meal cooked properly.<\/p>\n<p>\"I could get used to this,\" Vikram said, somewhere past the second helping.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't say that unless you mean it as something more than a compliment on the curry.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I mean it as more than a compliment on the curry.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Good,\" she said finally. \"Because I've already started imagining what a kitchen twice this size would look like. With actual chairs. And I don't imagine things I don't want.\"<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>It was the third afternoon, the two of them settled on the sofa with nothing particular planned, when someone knocked on the door.<\/p>\n<p>Naina frowned, getting up. \"I'm not expecting anyone.\"<\/p>\n<p>She opened the door to find her younger sister standing there, a duffel bag over one shoulder, grinning like she'd just won an argument.<\/p>\n<p>\"Sunaina? What are you—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I came straight from the airport. Nobody knows I'm here yet — not Ma, not Papa. I wanted to surprise them properly, so I'm staying with you first, then showing up at home tomorrow like I just landed.\" Sunaina breezed past her into the flat without waiting for a proper invitation, the way younger sisters do, and stopped short the moment she saw Vikram rising from the sofa. \"Oh.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't tell me you were coming,\" Naina said, still holding the door.<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't tell you because I wanted to surprise you. Which, it appears, has gone both ways.\" Sunaina's eyes moved over Vikram with open, unhurried curiosity — the same directness Naina had, though sharper somehow, less patient. \"You must be him.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Vikram,\" he said, extending a hand. \"And you must be the sister I've heard a great deal about.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"All of it flattering, I hope.\" She shook his hand, and he saw her taking him in properly now — the height of him, the set of his shoulders, the clean, strong lines of his face. Something shifted briefly in her expression, quick and involuntary, gone almost as soon as it appeared.<\/p>\n<p>\"Mostly flattering,\" Naina said, shutting the door and giving her sister a look. \"You could have called ahead.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Where's the fun in that.\" Sunaina dropped her bag by the sofa and settled into the armchair like she owned it, already entirely at home. \"Besides, now I get to actually meet him properly, instead of hearing about him secondhand for months.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"How's Bedfordshire treating you?\" Naina asked, settling back onto the sofa.<\/p>\n<p>\"Ironic, mostly.\" Sunaina laughed, shaking her head. \"Journalism programme, forty students in my main class — twenty-five Indians, five Pakistanis, four Bangladeshis, three Sri Lankans, two Nepalis, and exactly one British student, who mostly just looks confused every time someone brings samosas to the group project. Some days I genuinely forget I'm not in undivided India. It's the whole subcontinent, minus the British Raj, plus one very bewildered actual British boy.\"<\/p>\n<p>They all laughed at that — Vikram included, caught off guard by how easily the joke landed.<\/p>\n<p>But something shifted in Sunaina's expression a moment later, the humour draining out of it as quickly as it had arrived. \"It's not actually funny, if you think about it properly,\" she said. \"The demographics of entire countries are shifting like this, quietly, one classroom at a time, and nobody's really talking about what it means. Demographics change politics eventually — who votes, who gets represented, whose culture ends up being the loud one in a room that used to belong to someone else entirely. I don't think Britain's fully reckoned yet with what a shift like this actually does to a country over twenty, thirty years.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina looked at her sister for a moment, something surprised and faintly proud crossing her face. \"When did you get this serious?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I've always been this serious. I just usually save it for when the joke's already landed properly first.\" Sunaina shrugged, some of the earlier lightness returning to her expression, though not entirely, and shot a sly look between the two of them. \"Someone in this family has to think about the boring, important things, since you two are clearly busy enough staring at each other to have noticed a single demographic trend in months. Ma and Papa aren't much better — they think geopolitics is something that happens to other countries.\"<\/p>\n<p>They talked for the better part of an hour after that — Sunaina peppering him with questions the way she'd once teased him on the phone all those months ago, sharp, quick, testing him without quite seeming to. He answered everything plainly, without performing for her, and somewhere in the conversation he saw her decide something, the way Naina sometimes did, a private verdict arriving and settling behind her eyes.<\/p>\n<p>At one point, when Vikram excused himself briefly to the kitchen for water, Sunaina leaned toward her sister, lowering her voice.<\/p>\n<p>\"Well,\" she said. \"Now I understand what all the fuss was about.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't start.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not starting anything. I'm just saying — he's exactly what you'd want on paper and somehow better in person. Polite. Doesn't perform. Built like he could actually protect someone, not just talk about it.\" She glanced toward the kitchen, then back at her sister. \"Don't you dare let this one go, Naina. I mean that. Whatever happens, don't let him go.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't plan to.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Good. I'd hate to have to say I told you so from somewhere far less convenient than this flat.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram returned before Naina could answer that, and the conversation moved easily back to ordinary things, though something had settled a little more firmly in the room than before — an approval given, quietly, in the thirty seconds he'd been out of earshot.<\/p>\n<p>His and Naina's plans for the rest of the leave shifted with Sunaina's arrival, the private, quiet days they'd expected folding instead into something livelier, three of them instead of two, dinner stretching later than planned, Sunaina full of opinions about everything from the food to the film playing in the background.<\/p>\n<p>By the time evening came, Vikram had to take his leave earlier than he might have otherwise — a visit to Badshahpur still owed before his leave ran out, family expecting him, a duty he couldn't put off any longer no matter how much he'd rather have stayed.<\/p>\n<p>\"Go,\" Naina said, walking him to the door. \"Before my sister finds another reason to keep you here answering questions.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I heard that,\" Sunaina called from the sofa, not looking up.<\/p>\n<p>\"You were meant to.\"<\/p>\n<p>At the door, Vikram turned to Naina one last time. \"Four days wasn't enough, and now I'm losing half an evening of it to the road besides.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"There'll be more leaves,\" she said. \"I intend to keep believing that, loudly, until it stops being optional.\"<\/p>\n<p>He pulled her into a brief hug, mindful of her sister in the next room, and left for Badshahpur with the quiet warmth of a man who had just watched two women who mattered to him get along better than he'd had any right to expect. The sound of their easy laughter followed him all the way down the stairwell.<\/p>\n<p>It was only once he was in the cab, the flat's lit window shrinking behind him, that the warmth of the evening gave way to something quieter and less comfortable. There was a knowledge, not yet spoken to anyone, that his next posting orders were due any day now — and that this four-day leave, easy and domestic as it had felt, might turn out to be the last ordinary evening for a while.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 23, "title": "Chapter 21 — An Evening at Home", "content": "<h1>Chapter 21 — An Evening at Home<\/h1>\n<p>The drive to Badshahpur took just over thirty minutes, the last stretch turning onto a familiar road well after dark, headlights catching dust rising off the fields on either side, the dark pressing close on both sides the way it only does far from any city's glow.<\/p>\n<p>The house sat on close to an acre of the family's own land, mango and jamun trees lining the boundary, planted by his grandfather, their branches now grown thick and generous with age. A Mahindra Thar sat parked under a corrugated tin shelter near the gate, and beside it his brother's Royal Enfield Bullet. Further back, near the cattle shed, a Farmtrac 60 tractor sat cooling from the day's work, and a row of buffaloes stood tethered under a large neem tree nearby, their occasional low grunts the only sound in the settling dark.<\/p>\n<p>His father was still up on the veranda when Vikram's cab pulled in, the rest of the house already settling for the night.<\/p>\n<p>\"Aa gaya,\" his father said simply, standing. Not a question.<\/p>\n<p>\"Ji han.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Khana kha liya?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Ji han, kha liya raste mein.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Theek hai. Ja, so ja. Subah baat karte hain.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram touched his father's feet briefly, the way he always did, and his father rested a hand on his head for a moment, the whole exchange over in under a minute, everything that needed saying already understood without needing more words than that.<\/p>\n<p>It was only the next morning that he properly greeted the rest of the family.<\/p>\n<p>His elder brother was already up, checking on the cattle, and simply nodded at him across the courtyard. \"Got in last night?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Yes, it was late.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Right. Tea?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Sure.\"<\/p>\n<p>His younger brother emerged a few minutes later, a stack of books under one arm, offering only a brief nod and \"Morning, bhaiya\" before disappearing back toward his room, already muttering something about a chapter he needed to finish.<\/p>\n<p>He ate breakfast the next morning the way he always did at home: sitting on a low stool near the chullha itself, his mother crouched by the fire rolling out bajra rotis one after another, patting them thin between her palms before laying them onto the hot tawa, flipping each one carefully before finally holding it for a moment directly over the flame to puff it up, the smell of woodsmoke and roasting grain filling the whole kitchen.<\/p>\n<p>She piled his plate with the hot rotis slathered generously in fresh makkhan, alongside a bowl of sarson ka saag, a small lump of jaggery on the side, and a tall steel glass of chilled chhach she insisted he finish before leaving the table.<\/p>\n<p>\"You've gotten so thin,\" she said, refilling his plate before he'd finished the first serving, the same complaint every mother in Haryana seemed to keep ready at all times. \"That Army doesn't feed you properly, I know it. Eat, beta. Eat properly, for once, while you're actually home.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Ma, I eat plenty.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You eat what they give you. That's not the same as eating properly.\" She was already reaching for another roti before he could protest.<\/p>\n<p>His elder brother sat nearby recounting the season's harvest in more detail than anyone had asked for. His elder brother's wife quietly managed two small children who kept escaping toward their uncle's uniform bag in the corner. His younger brother occasionally interjected some fact he'd memorised for his exam that had nothing to do with the conversation — purely for the practice of saying it aloud.<\/p>\n<p>Later that evening, once the family had settled indoors, Vikram walked over to Chaudhary's farm, a short distance down the same road, the way he always did whenever he was home. Fateh Singh Chaudhary was out near the cattle shed when he arrived, and broke into a grin the moment he spotted him.<\/p>\n<p>Broad-shouldered and thick through the chest and forearms from years of physical farm work rather than any gym, his skin browned dark from the sun, a few early creases already forming at the corners of his eyes from squinting through long days in the fields. A heavy, untrimmed moustache sat over a wide, easy mouth built for laughing more than for careful speech, and his hands were already thick-knuckled and calloused, roughened by rope and plough far earlier than most men his age. He wore a simple kurta, sleeves pushed up past the elbow, dust from the fields still clinging to his feet. Chaudhary looked, standing there in the gathering dark, exactly like what he was — a man who had never once left the soil he'd been born on, and had never once wanted to.<\/p>\n<p>\"You've barely dropped your bags and you're already here,\" Chaudhary said, pulling him into a rough hug. \"Sit. I'll get the chai going.\"<\/p>\n<p>They sat on charpais outside Chaudhary's own modest farmhouse, the sky overhead deepening toward night, the sound of crickets starting up in the fields around them. Chaudhary's own buffaloes lowed occasionally from somewhere nearby, and a single bare bulb strung from the veranda threw a small circle of yellow light over the packed earth between them, moths already gathering in slow, drunken circles around it.<\/p>\n<p>\"So,\" Chaudhary said, handing him a glass of tea. \"How long is this leave?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Four days. That's not much of a leave.\" Chaudhary studied him a moment, something faintly curious in his expression, as though he sensed there was more to the visit than Vikram was letting on, but didn't press it. \"You seem different this time. Lighter, somehow. I can't quite place it.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram only shrugged, offering nothing further, and Chaudhary let it go, the way old friends sometimes do — noticing a change, choosing not to chase it.<\/p>\n<p>They talked long into the evening after that — old stories, old names, the easy rhythm of two men who had known each other long before either of them had anything as complicated as a career or a woman to account for.<\/p>\n<p>It was, Vikram thought, walking back to his own house much later under a sky thick with stars neither Delhi nor Gurugram ever properly showed him, exactly the kind of evening he'd needed without knowing he needed it. A reminder of the ground he came from, steady and unchanged, waiting patiently for however long the Army chose to keep him away from it.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't yet know that somewhere in that same stretch of village and memory, a name from his own past was about to resurface, in a conversation he had no part in and couldn't have predicted — the kind of small, ordinary phone call that changes nothing about a place, and everything about a man's sense of it.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 24, "title": "Chapter 22 — The Last Call", "content": "<h1>Chapter 22 — The Last Call<\/h1>\n<p>Kamal called him one evening, an old school friend, now with the police himself. Vikram was back at his post in the Northeast by then, leave long since over, sitting on the steps outside the officers' mess with his phone against his ear, watching the last of the daylight fade behind the forested ridgeline beyond the wire, the air thick with the damp, green smell that never quite left this part of the country.<\/p>\n<p>They hadn't spoken properly in over a year. The call started the way such calls always do — old names, old jokes, the easy rhythm of two men who'd studied together at the same school.<\/p>\n<p>\"So,\" Kamal said, after they'd covered the usual ground. \"Any love story cooking, yaar? You must have met some Kashmir girl up there. They say the women there are something else to look at.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Wrong theatre, Kamal. I moved out of J&amp;K months back — Northeast now, different terrain, different problems entirely.\" Vikram allowed himself a small laugh. \"But you're not wrong about beautiful, wherever I've been posted. Not for love, though. They need our protection, not our attention that way. We're there to be their protection and their care — not to fall in love with them, but to regard them properly, to protect them, to keep them safe. That's the duty, not the romance.\" He paused a moment, then went on. \"But looking at someone and actually loving them are different things anyway. Real love needs someone who understands you properly — same culture, same upbringing, someone who knows what you mean without you having to explain it every time. That kind of understanding, that kind of care — you wouldn't understand what I'm talking about.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Arre, I might not understand deep love, philosopher sahib, but I understand the live version of it well enough.\" Kamal laughed, unbothered. \"So which one is it for you? The deep kind, or are you still collecting the other kind like the rest of us?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"The deep kind,\" Vikram said, quieter now, and something in his own chest tightened just saying it out loud to someone else for the first time — a private thing, suddenly spoken, suddenly real in a way it hadn't been while it only existed between him and Naina. \"There's someone. It's serious.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Serious how serious?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"We went to Himachal together. Just the two of us, few days.\" He didn't say more than that, didn't offer a name, didn't need to. Some things a man kept for himself, even with an old friend.<\/p>\n<p>\"Himachal, no less. Sounds properly serious to me.\" Kamal whistled low. \"Good for you, yaar. About time.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"What about you? Any story of your own?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"There's someone.\" Kamal's voice took on a different note, something between pride and caution. \"Not a girl, exactly. A woman. Decent, but young enough still, full of life. Passionate, yaar, I won't lie to you about that part.\" A short laugh. \"Don't judge me for it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not judging. Who is she?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"From your own department, actually. Well — my department now, but she started where you'd know her from. And from Badshahpur too, if you can believe it.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something in Vikram went very still. \"Who.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Kavita.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram said nothing for a long moment. His free hand found the edge of the step and gripped it once, then let go, an old, familiar image surfacing without his permission — a courtyard, a tea tray set down between them, two years of Tuesday evenings he hadn't thought about in months. When he finally spoke, his voice came out steadier than he expected. \"That's — good. I'm glad for you, Kamal. Truly.\" And he was, mostly — the words weren't a performance, even if they'd taken him longer to find than a normal reply should have.<\/p>\n<p>Kamal, misreading the silence as simple surprise, laughed again and moved the conversation on to something else — a cricket match, a mutual friend's wedding — and Vikram let it go, answering at the right moments, his mind circling back, again and again, to a courtyard he hadn't stood in for a long time.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>Weeks later, Kamal mentioned the operation to Kavita on a phone call, the way it was in every news bulletin that month — a cross-border strike near the Myanmar border that had destroyed a terrorist camp, an old school friend of his had led it, he said, someone from her husband's own village, Badshahpur. Vikram.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita went still at the name, the way she still did, even now — and underneath that stillness sat a small, private shock she didn't let creep into her voice. In all this time, she had never once known that Kamal and Vikram knew each other, let alone that they'd studied together, shared a childhood, called each other by nicknames only old school friends still used. Two entirely separate parts of her life, kept apart so carefully for so long, had apparently always been standing closer to each other than she'd ever imagined.<\/p>\n<p>\"Did you tell him anything? About us?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Of course not. That's not something a man discusses.\" Kamal shrugged, unbothered. \"He mentioned someone else, actually. Serious, apparently. They'd gone to Himachal together.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something in Kavita's chest closed quietly at that, final in a way she hadn't expected. She had never been to Himachal with him. Whoever this woman was, she occupied a place in his life Kavita never had — a whole journey, taken freely, in daylight, hiding from no one.<\/p>\n<p>She said little more after that, and by the time the call ended, something in her had grieved, privately, for a version of his love that had never been meant for her.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>She called him three nights later, past ten, the department quiet around her by then except for a single tube light humming somewhere down the corridor and a constable's radio crackling low at the front desk.<\/p>\n<p>He picked up on the second ring, and something in him braced without quite knowing why. \"Kavita.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I heard about the operation,\" she said. \"From Kamal. He said you led it. Congratulations, Major Saab.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Thank you.\" He heard something underneath her voice — careful, a little too composed, the steadiness she'd always used when she was working hard to hold something back.<\/p>\n<p>\"The whole village has been talking about it. Your father must be walking around like he owns the district.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"He's enjoying it more than he'll admit.\" He allowed himself a small laugh, though his chest had gone tight, some instinct telling him this call carried more weight than its opening lines let on.<\/p>\n<p>\"How's the department treating you?\" he asked, mostly to give her somewhere to go with it.<\/p>\n<p>\"Same as always. Files and paperwork and senior officers who think shouting is a management technique.\" She laughed, but it came a beat too late, a fraction too bright. \"Ramfal's mother has finally stopped asking where I disappear to on weekends. I think she's given up trying to keep track.\"<\/p>\n<p>They talked a while longer, unhurried, about nothing that mattered much — a cousin's engagement, a road being repaired near the old bus stand. On the surface, it was exactly the kind of conversation two old friends might have, easy and warm. But under it, both of them were doing the same quiet work — filling the space between them with anything ordinary enough to avoid the two names neither of them said, Naina sitting unspoken in every pause on his end, Kamal sitting unspoken in every pause on hers.<\/p>\n<p>\"I should let you go,\" Kavita said eventually, something in her voice thinning despite her best effort to keep it light. \"I only called to say congratulations properly. Didn't want to just send a text for something like this.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I appreciate that.\" He meant it more than the plainness of the words let on. \"Kavita—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't,\" she said quickly, gently. \"Whatever you're about to say. Don't. Let's just leave it as it is tonight. Congratulations, and an old friend's voice, and nothing more complicated than that.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a moment, understanding, and let it go.<\/p>\n<p>\"Take care of yourself, Vikram,\" she said softly.<\/p>\n<p>\"You too, Kavita.\"<\/p>\n<p>The call ended a few seconds later. Vikram set the phone down on the step beside him and left it there a while, staring out at the dark ridgeline instead of picking it back up. The ordinary ten minutes replayed in his head — not the words themselves, but the small catch in her laugh a beat too late, the \"Congratulations, Major Saab\" that had sounded rehearsed.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't examine it further than that. Some doors, once a woman chose to close them herself, weren't his to reopen by asking too many questions.<\/p>\n<p>Kavita, for her part, sat with the phone in her lap long after the line had gone dead, the tube light still humming outside her door. She let herself, for just a few minutes, feel the whole of it — two years, a phone line, a courtyard, a man who had never once been fully hers and never would be now.<\/p>\n<p>Then she straightened her dupatta, switched off the desk lamp, and went back to being the composed, capable woman the rest of her life required her to be.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 25, "title": "Chapter 23 — A Question at Midnight", "content": "<h1>Chapter 23 — A Question at Midnight<\/h1>\n<p>His next leave came through a few weeks after the Kavita call, the operation's aftermath finally settling enough for the Army to let him go.<\/p>\n<p>It was past midnight, both of them lying in the dark of her flat, sleep still an hour away from either of them, a ceiling fan turning slow and almost silent overhead, the faint hum of traffic from the main road the only sign that the rest of the city hadn't gone to sleep with them. That was when Naina asked the question out of nowhere.<\/p>\n<p>\"How many children do you want?\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram didn't answer right away. The question sat in the dark between them, ordinary in its words and enormous in everything underneath them, and he felt something in his chest shift the way it does when a person asks you to imagine a future you've been too afraid to imagine out loud yourself.<\/p>\n<p>\"I hadn't let myself think about it,\" he said finally. \"Not properly.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Why not?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Because thinking about it makes it real. And I've spent a long time not letting myself want things that could be taken away.\" He was quiet a moment, feeling for the words the way a man feels for a light switch in an unfamiliar room. \"But if you're asking — two. Maybe three, if you wanted more.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Two,\" she repeated, testing the number in the dark. \"I always thought I wanted three. A big, loud house. But I don't know anymore. I think I just wanted a house that wasn't quiet the way my own childhood home sometimes was.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Was it quiet?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"My father was gone often. Postings, training, whatever the Army needed. My mother managed most of it alone.\" She shifted slightly, her shoulder finding his in the dark. \"I used to lie awake as a child, listening for the sound of his jeep on the gravel outside, and some nights it never came, and I'd fall asleep still listening for it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't want that for you,\" he said quietly. \"I don't want you lying awake listening for a jeep that doesn't come.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know you don't want it. Wanting isn't the same as being able to stop it.\" Her voice had gone smaller than usual, stripped of its usual sharpness. \"I've been telling myself for months that I can handle this life. The postings. The waiting. I handled fifteen days without a word from you and I nearly broke apart doing it. I don't know how my mother did it for twenty years without breaking.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't break.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I came close enough that I don't like remembering it.\" She was quiet a moment, her breathing uneven in the dark. \"I think about our children, if we have them, growing up the way I did — listening for a vehicle on the road, learning to read their mother's face for how worried to be that week. I don't know if I have the right to bring a child into that. Or if wanting you enough to try anyway makes me selfish.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram turned toward her in the dark, finding her hand and holding it, feeling the fine tremor in her fingers that her voice hadn't let show.<\/p>\n<p>\"It doesn't make you selfish,\" he said. \"It makes you exactly the kind of mother I'd want for them. Someone who thought about the cost before she agreed to pay it, instead of pretending there wasn't one.\" He brought her hand to his chest, over his heart, the way she often did to him. \"I can't promise you a jeep that always comes home on time. I couldn't promise my own mother that, growing up, and I can't promise you either. All I can promise is that whatever children we have, they'll know exactly how much their father loved coming home to them. Every single time. Even the times it took longer than it should have.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't answer right away. He felt her free hand find her own eyes in the dark, wiping at something she didn't want him to hear in her voice, and when she finally spoke, her voice had steadied again, though something in it stayed softer than before.<\/p>\n<p>\"Two,\" she said. \"I've decided. Two children, and a house that isn't quiet even when you're away, because I'll fill it with enough noise for both of us until you get back.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'd like that.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You'd better.\" She curled fully into him then, her head tucked under his chin, and for a long while neither of them said anything else.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in the quiet, Vikram found himself doing the math he'd been avoiding all evening — if postings ran the way they usually did, his next move could come with far less warning than this one had, and far less certainty about when leave would follow. He didn't say any of that out loud. He only held her a little closer, and let her fall asleep first, listening to her breathing even out before his own did.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 26, "title": "Chapter 24 — What She Never Said Out Loud", "content": "<h1>Chapter 24 — What She Never Said Out Loud<\/h1>\n<p>They were sitting on the floor of her flat one evening, sorting through a box of her old photographs. It had been Naina's idea, a rare quiet Sunday with nothing else demanding either of them — the smell of old paper and faint mildew rising off the box each time she lifted the lid, a stick of sandalwood incense burning low on the windowsill, its thread of smoke the only thing moving in the still afternoon light.<\/p>\n<p>Then she went still over one photograph and didn't say anything for a long moment.<\/p>\n<p>\"What is it?\" Vikram asked.<\/p>\n<p>She held it out to him. A much younger Naina, maybe sixteen, standing in a dusty field with a cricket bat over one shoulder, grinning at whoever was holding the camera, her hair short and unbothered, nothing in her posture asking anyone's permission to take up the space she was taking up.<\/p>\n<p>\"I used to be so loud,\" she said quietly. \"Not just my voice. All of me. I wanted things out loud. I argued with my teachers out loud. I told my father I wanted to join the police before I even knew what the job actually involved, just because I liked the idea of a woman doing something people didn't expect her to do.\" She traced the edge of the photograph with one finger, as though touching the girl inside it might somehow reach her. \"I don't know exactly when I got quieter. Not shy — I'm still not shy. Just more careful. More aware of how much space I'm supposed to leave for other people.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You're not quiet with me.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"No. That's exactly it.\" She looked up at him, something unusually unguarded in her face. \"I'm not quiet with you, and it terrifies me a little, because I don't know what happens to that once I'm someone's wife properly. Once there's a household to run, and his family's expectations to manage, and children who need a mother more than they need a woman who once wanted to be a police officer for her own reasons.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Why would any of that change who you are?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Because I've watched it happen to almost every woman I know.\" Her voice sharpened, and she looked past him at the wall instead of at him, as though the thing she was angry at was too large to aim at any one person in the room.<\/p>\n<p>\"My mother was sharp once. I've seen old photographs of her too — she debated politics with my father's friends, she had opinions about everything.\" Naina turned the photograph over once in her hands before setting it down. \"Somewhere along the way she just started being a wife instead of also being herself. I don't think anyone took it from her. I think she just kept setting pieces of herself down, a little at a time, until there wasn't very much left standing where all that sharpness used to be.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram was quiet, letting her get to wherever she needed to get to.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't want that,\" she said. Her voice had thinned, and her hands had gone still around the edges of the photograph, gripping it slightly tighter than a moment ago.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't want to wake up in fifteen years and not recognise the girl in this photograph as ever having been me. I want to still argue too loudly about things that don't matter. I want to still want a piece of land with my own name on the papers. I don't know how to ask a man to promise me that, because I don't know if it's even his to promise.\"<\/p>\n<p>He reached over and took the photograph from her hands, setting it carefully aside, and then took both her hands instead.<\/p>\n<p>\"I can't promise you the world won't try to take pieces of you,\" he said. \"I watched it try to take pieces of my own mother too. I can promise you I will never be one of the people trying. If you want to argue too loudly, I want to be in the room hearing it, not asking you to quiet down. If you want that land, we'll find a way to put your name on it, not just mine. Whatever you're afraid of losing — I don't want you to lose it for me. I fell in love with exactly the woman in that photograph, grown up. I don't want a smaller version of her. I never will.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina didn't say anything for a while. She simply looked at him, her thumb moving once, slowly, along the edge of the photograph in her lap, and when she finally spoke, her voice had steadied again, though it carried a new kind of weight underneath it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've never told anyone that,\" she said. \"Not even Sunaina. I don't think I even let myself think it in full sentences before tonight.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm glad you told me.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"So am I.\" She picked the photograph back up, looking at her sixteen-year-old self a moment longer before setting it down properly, on top instead of buried in the pile, as though giving that girl a small, deserved place of honour among all the rest. \"Keep me loud, Vikram. Whatever happens to us, whatever life decides to hand either of us — promise me you'll keep me loud.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I promise,\" he said, and meant it with everything he had.<\/p>\n<p>It was an easy thing to promise in a quiet room on a still afternoon, incense burning low and nothing yet asking either of them to prove it. He knew that even as he said it. He made the promise anyway, and let himself worry, only afterward and only privately, about which of them the world would test first.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 27, "title": "Chapter 25 — What They Fought About", "content": "<h1>Chapter 25 — What They Fought About<\/h1>\n<p>It started over something small enough that neither of them, arguing in the middle of it, could have said with certainty how it had grown so large so fast, the way a single spark sometimes finds exactly the right dry season to become something much larger than itself.<\/p>\n<p>Naina had made plans for his next leave without asking him first — a trip to Ujjain, for Mahakal darshan, a whole weekend committed before Vikram had even confirmed how much leave he'd actually be granted. It was a Thursday evening, the sabzi she'd started for dinner still simmering on the gas stove behind her, half-forgotten, the smell of it slowly turning from promising to slightly burnt as the conversation went on. Outside, the evening traffic on the Sohna road sent up its usual dull roar through the closed window, muffled but constant, the sound of a city that didn't pause for anyone's argument.<\/p>\n<p>\"You should have asked me,\" he said, when she told him, his voice carrying more edge than he'd intended.<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't think I needed permission to plan a temple visit.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's not what I meant, and you know it. I meant you committed both of us to something without checking if it even fit my schedule. What if I can't get that weekend? What if duty needs me instead?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then you'll say the Army needs you, the way it always does, and I'll go without you, the way I've gone to plenty of things without you.\" Something sharp had crept into her voice now, old and familiar, the bluntness that had once charmed him now landing like a blade. \"I'm used to building a life around your schedule, Vikram. I didn't realise I also needed your permission before I made a single plan of my own.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's not fair. And why Mahakal, specifically? You've never mentioned wanting to go before.\" He said it from the kitchen doorway, arms crossed, not moving closer.<\/p>\n<p>Something shifted in her face — the anger still there, but underneath it, something more exposed. She set down the spoon she'd been holding, the sabzi forgotten now entirely, and turned to face him properly across the small room. \"Because I'm afraid, Vikram. I'm afraid of what happens if my father decides to be against this — against us — the way fathers sometimes do. I've watched enough of my friends' love marriages fall apart under exactly that kind of resistance. I wanted to go and ask for something I don't fully have the right to ask for out loud yet — that whatever comes, we come through it together. I didn't want to explain all of that to you before I'd even sorted out how I felt about it myself. So I just planned it. Badly, apparently, since I forgot the one person it was actually about.\"<\/p>\n<p>He hadn't expected that, and it stopped him short, some of his own irritation draining out of him at once.<\/p>\n<p>\"You didn't tell me you were worried about your father.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't want to say it out loud and make it more real than it already felt.\" She looked away, arms still crossed, though the posture had softened. \"I don't get a say in the things that actually put your life at risk. The least I thought I could do quietly, for both of us, was ask for something at a temple. And instead we're standing here fighting about a weekend.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know how to give you a say in operations, Naina. That's not something the Army lets me negotiate.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not asking you to negotiate with the Army. I'm asking you to actually see me as someone whose fears matter as much as yours do. There's a difference, and I don't think you've fully understood it yet.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then tell me plainly what you need, instead of making me guess.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Ask first. Don't assume.\"<\/p>\n<p>Five words. Nothing more. She said them like she'd been holding them back for weeks, and Vikram felt the weight of their simplicity land harder than any longer sentence could have.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not trying to fight with you,\" she went on, her voice quieter now, some of the heat burning down into something more tired. \"I'm trying to tell you something that's been sitting in me for weeks, and it came out wrong, and now we're standing here shouting about a temple visit that was never really the point.\"<\/p>\n<p>They stood there a moment, both of them breathing harder than the conversation strictly required, the small flat suddenly feeling too small to hold whatever had just been said out loud.<\/p>\n<p>\"I need some air,\" Naina said finally, and left without waiting for him to respond, the door closing behind her with more force than a slam and less than a statement.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram sat alone in the quiet afterward, turning the whole exchange over, understanding with growing discomfort that she wasn't entirely wrong. He had gotten used to a certain shape of loving her — protective, careful, making decisions and delivering them gently rather than making them together. It had felt, from inside his own head, like care. From where she was standing, it had apparently felt like something closer to being managed. And underneath all of it, a fear she'd been carrying alone, quiet enough that he'd never even noticed the weight of it.<\/p>\n<p>Naina, meanwhile, walked the block around her building without any real direction, arms wrapped around herself against the evening chill, turning over her own version of the same fight. She hadn't meant to make it about a temple. She'd meant, somewhere underneath the planning and the silence, to ask him to promise her a future without ever having to say the word future out loud, and instead she'd handed him a travel itinerary and expected him to hear a prayer inside it. She understood, walking, that she'd been quietly rehearsing this exact fight in her head for weeks, waiting for an excuse small enough to let it out.<\/p>\n<p>She came back an hour later, quieter, her anger spent somewhere out on the street, the evening cool enough that it had clearly done some of the work neither of them had managed alone.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm sorry I raised my voice,\" she said, standing in the doorway.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't apologise for that. You were right about most of it.\" He stood, crossing to her. \"I've been making decisions and informing you of them instead of making them with you. I didn't see it clearly until you said it. And I didn't know you were carrying that fear about your father, all this time, by yourself. I'm sorry for both.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something in her shoulders eased at that, some of the guard she'd walked back in with lowering. \"I don't want to fight like that again. It scared me a little, how fast it went from nothing to everything.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It scared me too.\" He reached for her hand, and she let him take it. \"I don't want you to feel like you're waiting to be informed about your own life, Naina. I want to actually build things with you, not just deliver news to you after I've already built them alone. And I don't want you carrying fear about us alone either. Whatever's coming — your father, anything else — I want to carry it with you, not have you go asking for it at a temple by yourself.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's all I wanted to hear.\" She stepped into him properly then, resting her forehead against his chest. \"I still want to go to Ujjain. I'd just like you there too, if the Army allows it, and I'd like you to want to be there, not just show up because I told you the dates.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to be there,\" he said. \"For that, and for whatever comes after it.\"<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them said the word father again that night, though it sat in the room with them a while longer, patient, unhurried, the way a thing that hasn't happened yet always is.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 28, "title": "Chapter 26 — Mahakal", "content": "<h1>Chapter 26 — Mahakal<\/h1>\n<p>They took a cab from Gurugram to New Delhi railway station, reaching with enough time to spare before the Malwa Express pulled in — the 20:30 departure, scheduled to reach Ujjain by late morning, nearly fifteen hours end to end.<\/p>\n<p>Their reservation was AC 3-tier, coach B3, seats 41 and 44, both lower berths, which Naina had specifically requested. \"I refuse to climb a ladder at midnight in a moving train,\" she said, settling her bag under the seat. \"Lower berth or nothing.\"<\/p>\n<p>The coach filled slowly around them — a family with two young children two bays down, an elderly couple already unpacking a tiffin box before the train had even left the platform, the usual clatter of luggage being wedged into overhead racks. A chai-wallah passed through calling out before the train had even cleared Delhi's outskirts properly, and Vikram bought two cups more out of ritual than actual thirst.<\/p>\n<p>Naina had been quiet for most of the early evening, not the careful silence of someone still upset, but something more contemplative — watching the dark countryside slide past the window, her hand loosely in his on the berth between them.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've never actually done this properly,\" she admitted, somewhere past midnight, both of them still awake despite the hour. \"Gone somewhere just to ask for something. I usually go to temples out of habit, or because my mother wants company. I don't think I've ever gone with an actual request.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"What are you going to ask for?\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet a moment. \"I don't know if I should say it out loud before I've asked it properly. Some part of me feels like saying it too early uses up whatever luck is supposed to carry it there.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then don't say it. I'll ask for something too. We don't have to compare notes.\"<\/p>\n<p>She smiled at that, small and real in the dim light of the compartment. \"What will you ask for?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"The same thing you're not telling me, probably.\" He squeezed her hand. \"I don't think either of us needs to say it out loud for both of us to know what it is.\"<\/p>\n<p>They spread out a thin blanket each once the berths were made, the train settling into its steady rhythm somewhere past Mathura, the lights of small towns sliding past the window at increasingly longer intervals as the night deepened.<\/p>\n<p>Both of them were properly awake by the time the train pulled into Bhopal, the platform outside alive despite the early hour: a station announcement looping overhead in that particular clipped Railways-English, a chai vendor's chant rising above the general noise, \"chai, garam chai,\" repeated in the same rhythm chai-vendors across the country seemed to have inherited from some shared ancestor.<\/p>\n<p>They stepped down onto the platform just long enough to stretch their legs and buy two more cups, the tea scalding and over-sweet the way station chai always was. They drank it standing beside the open door as the platform's noise washed over them — vendors, announcements, the hiss of some other train's brakes further down the line.<\/p>\n<p>\"I could get used to mornings like this,\" Naina said, wrapping both hands around the small clay cup for warmth. \"Loud, unplanned, tea that's too sweet.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Better than the mornings we usually get.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Much better.\"<\/p>\n<p>The train pulled out again a few minutes later, and the rest of the journey passed easily — breakfast bought off a vendor somewhere past Bhopal, the countryside slowly shifting from Madhya Pradesh's flatter stretches into the slightly hillier country closer to Ujjain.<\/p>\n<p>They finally stepped off at Ujjain Junction close to noon, later than the schedule had promised but close enough that neither of them minded, the town already fully awake around them, temple bells audible somewhere even from the platform.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>The temple sat barely two kilometres from the station, and they took an auto the rest of the way, the town moving around them in the unhurried rhythm of a city that measured its days by temple bells rather than office hours. The Shipra river came into view briefly as they crossed toward the old town — wide, unhurried, ghats busy with the day's bathers and washing already well underway.<\/p>\n<p>The temple, when they reached it, sat low and imposing at once, its shikhara rising in tiers of carved stone against the pale morning sky. It was a blend of Bhumija, Maratha, and Chalukya styles that centuries of rebuilding had layered one on top of the other — none of it matching perfectly, and all of it somehow cohering into something that felt older than any single one of its parts.<\/p>\n<p>They joined the queue at the outer gate, already filling with the day's first wave of pilgrims, the air thick with incense and the low murmur of chanting. Inch by inch, they moved forward through a series of courtyards, the stone underfoot cool despite the growing heat outside.<\/p>\n<p>Naina grew quieter the closer they got, her hand tightening slightly in his. By the time they'd descended the last flight of steps down into the sanctum itself — the shrine set below ground level, the air changing texture entirely, thicker with smoke and the specific mineral smell of ash and centuries of ghee lamps — she'd gone almost entirely silent.<\/p>\n<p>The lingam itself, when they finally stood before it, faced south — the one detail about this particular Jyotirlinga that made it unlike any of the other eleven scattered across the country. It was a fact the priest muttered to the queue moving past almost as an afterthought, though it landed on Vikram with more weight than he'd expected a stray fact to carry.<\/p>\n<p>It was dark, ancient stone, barely visible beneath the ash and flowers and offerings already layered onto it that morning, lit by nothing but the flickering orange of oil lamps held by priests moving through their morning rituals with the unhurried efficiency of men who had done this exact sequence of motions ten thousand times before.<\/p>\n<p>Naina closed her eyes and stood there a while, her lips moving silently around words he couldn't hear and didn't try to, her free hand pressed flat against her own chest as though she were holding something in place there by force, her whole body very still in a way he'd never seen from her, not even asleep.<\/p>\n<p>He closed his own eyes beside her, and found, without planning to, that his own silent request wasn't complicated at all. He didn't ask for a promotion, or safety in his next posting, or anything the Army might reasonably be expected to threaten. He asked, simply, that whatever stood between them and the life they were quietly building — a father's fear, distance, duty, anything at all — would find its way to standing aside instead of standing in the way.<\/p>\n<p>When they climbed back up into the sunlight afterward, Naina's eyes were damp, though she wasn't crying exactly, just full in a way that needed a moment to settle.<\/p>\n<p>\"Are you all right?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm more than all right.\" She wiped at her eyes, laughing a little at herself. \"I don't know why it hit me like that. I've been to a hundred temples in my life. This one just felt different, somehow. Heavier and lighter at the same time.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Maybe it's the company.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Maybe.\" She took his hand again as they walked, unhurried, back through the narrow lanes toward where they were staying. The whole town was alive around them with the ordinary business of pilgrims and vendors and children running between both — a man selling prasad from a cart, a group of women in bright saris comparing photographs on a phone, the Shipra somewhere behind them still catching the morning light.<\/p>\n<p>\"I feel lighter, Vikram. I came here carrying something I didn't even fully admit to you I was carrying, and I feel like I've set at least some of it down.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm glad.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I am too.\" She stopped walking for a moment, turning to face him properly in the middle of the lane, strangers moving around them in both directions. \"I love you. I don't think I've said it plainly enough, often enough, for how much I actually mean it. I love you, and I'm not afraid to have said that here, in this exact place, in front of everyone and no one at all.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I love you too,\" he said, and meant it with the whole of what the last two days had taught him — that loving her properly meant carrying what she carried, not just what he chose to notice, and that some fears were better said out loud in a temple than carried alone in silence for months.<\/p>\n<p>They stayed two more days in Ujjain, wandering without much plan, eating at small local places, sitting by the river in the evenings and talking about nothing and everything in the unhurried way that comes easily to two people who have just set something heavy down together. By the time they boarded the train home, whatever had cracked open between them in that small flat days earlier had healed into something sturdier than before — not because the fight had never happened, but because they had let it teach them something instead of letting it simply pass.<\/p>\n<p>They went back to Leisure Valley one more time before his leave ended, to the same bench beneath the same gulmohar, an ordinary Tuesday evening with nothing particular planned, the tree now fully in bloom, its canopy a solid, fire-orange roof over their heads.<\/p>\n<p>\"We've never actually sat here in full flower before,\" Naina said, looking up at it. \"Every other time it's been bare, or barely budding. This is the first time it's actually looked like what it's supposed to look like.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Feels appropriate, somehow.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It does.\" Something soft settled into her expression as she looked at him instead of the tree now. \"I like this place, Vikram. I think this whole journey started right here, and some part of it has stayed close to my heart ever since.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet a moment, her voice thinning as she went on. \"You know I can't live without you. I've attached every single feeling I have to you, so completely that even the thought of a life without you makes my whole body tremble. I feel afraid, genuinely afraid, and something aches in my chest that I don't have a proper name for.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"If this tree is listening, let it be a witness — I don't think I've ever loved even myself the way I love you. I can't picture myself apart from you, not even for a single minute. That's why I wanted to go to Mahakal, why I asked for what I asked for there. And my heart feels at peace now, Vikram. Truly at peace.\"<\/p>\n<p>As if in answer, a single petal let go from somewhere high in the canopy, turning slowly on its way down before landing directly in her open palm, and she looked at it a long moment, something unreadable crossing her face, before closing her fingers gently around it.<\/p>\n<p>\"It's listening,\" she said quietly, almost to herself.<\/p>\n<p>He took her hand, the petal still caught between her fingers, and said nothing for a long moment, simply sitting with her under a tree finally wearing everything it had spent a year learning how to become.<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them noticed, in that particular moment, that a tree in full flower is also a tree that has begun, quietly and invisibly, the business of letting go.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 29, "title": "Chapter 27 — What She Told Her Mother", "content": "<h1>Chapter 27 — What She Told Her Mother<\/h1>\n<p>She chose an afternoon when her father was out. The kitchen was quiet except for the sound of her mother rolling out roti — the soft slap of dough against the board, the faint hiss of each roti hitting the hot tawa, the whole room carrying the warm, familiar smell of wheat and ghee that had filled this same kitchen every evening of Naina's childhood.<\/p>\n<p>Naina sat on the low stool beside her the way she had as a child, unable to find a way into the conversation except sideways.<\/p>\n<p>\"Ma. I need to tell you something properly this time. Not just photographs.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her mother didn't look up from the dough, though something in her hands slowed. \"About Vikram.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"About Vikram. I want to marry him.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her mother's hands stilled on the rolling pin, just for a moment, before starting again, slower now.<\/p>\n<p>\"I wondered when this conversation was coming,\" she said. \"I saw it in your voice, that day you showed me the wedding photos. I didn't think it would take you this long to say it plainly.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I didn't know how to say it plainly. Saying it makes it something you and Baba have to actually decide about, instead of just something happening quietly in my own life.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her mother set the roti aside on the tawa and wiped her hands on her dupatta, turning to look at her daughter properly. \"You're serious about this. Marriage, not just — whatever this has been.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Completely serious.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her mother was quiet a long moment, something moving behind her eyes that Naina couldn't fully read — not surprise, since she'd clearly half expected this for weeks, but something closer to a fear that had been waiting patiently for permission to surface now that the words were finally out loud.<\/p>\n<p>\"You know your father will never agree to this easily,\" her mother said finally. \"I warned you myself, the day I saw those photographs. Army. That word alone will close half his mind before he's even properly met the boy.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Because of you,\" Naina said quietly. \"Because of what it was like for you.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something flickered across her mother's face. \"You remember what I told you that day.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I remember all of it.\" Naina reached for her mother's hand. \"I need to know — was it truly as hard as you made it sound?\"<\/p>\n<p>Her mother didn't answer right away. She looked down at their joined hands, and when she finally spoke, her voice had gone quieter, older somehow.<\/p>\n<p>\"Harder, some years. Easier, others. It isn't a simple thing to explain to a daughter who hasn't lived it.\" She squeezed Naina's hand once. \"I told you before to keep your eyes open even while your heart was doing what it was doing. I don't think you kept them open enough, if I'm honest. But that's done now. What matters is what happens from here.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Will you talk to him? Baba?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I already know what I have to do.\" Her mother sighed, a long, tired sound. \"I won't pretend this will go well. I know your father, and I know almost exactly how this conversation is going to end before it even begins. But you deserve to have someone soften the ground a little before he hears the full weight of it directly from you. Let me carry that first blow, at least.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Ma, you don't have to protect me from this.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I am your mother. Protecting you from things is the entire job, whether you want it or not.\" She managed a small, tired smile, cupping Naina's face briefly with a floury hand. \"Let me talk to him tonight. Then, if it comes to it, you'll speak to him yourself. But let me go first.\"<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>That night, after dinner, once the house had gone quiet, Naina's mother sat with her husband on the veranda and told him plainly what their daughter had confirmed. Somewhere out in the dark garden, a stray dog barked twice and went silent, and the marigolds along the path gave off their faint, bitter smell in the cooling night air.<\/p>\n<p>He was silent for a long while after she finished, his jaw tight, his eyes fixed somewhere out in the dark garden.<\/p>\n<p>\"Army,\" he said finally, the word carrying the same weight it always did in that house.<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You, of all people, should have discouraged this the moment she first mentioned him to you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I did discourage it, in my own way, the day she showed me those photographs. I told her to keep her eyes open.\" His wife's voice was steady, though something tired sat underneath it. \"She didn't come to me tonight asking my permission to fall in love. She came to tell me what she'd already decided, weeks ago, in her own heart.<\/p>\n<p>\"I remember being twenty-one once too. I remember exactly how little I wanted anyone's permission for what my own heart had already settled on.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her husband said nothing to that, and she let the silence sit a moment before continuing.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not asking you to bless it tonight,\" she said. \"I'm asking you to speak to her yourself, properly, before you decide anything. She's earned that much, at least — a father's honest words, not a mother's secondhand warning.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a long while longer, and when he finally spoke, his voice had gone rougher, older.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll speak to her,\" he said. \"But I want you to understand something first, since you're the one who actually knows what I'm about to carry into that conversation.\"<\/p>\n<p>He turned to look at her properly, something raw in his face that he rarely let show. \"I watched what that life did to you. I lived it from my side and I watched you live it from yours, and I am not eager, even now, twenty-five years later, to watch our daughter take up the exact same weight.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't say the rest of it out loud. Somewhere underneath the fear was an older, more stubborn grief — the years he'd spent believing his own service had asked more of his wife than any love was fair to ask, a debt he'd never quite finished paying and had no wish to hand down to his daughter as an inheritance.<\/p>\n<p>\"I know,\" she said quietly. \"I carried it because I loved you enough to carry it. That's not nothing, even after everything it cost. Don't forget that part, when you talk to her.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't answer that directly, but something in his shoulders eased slightly, the way a held breath finally lets go somewhere out in the dark of a veranda no one else was awake to witness.<\/p>\n<p>Neither of them yet knew that the boy in question had already decided, on his own, to spare them both the wait — that within days, without being asked, he would be standing at their door himself, asking to be heard before either of them had finished deciding what to say to him.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 30, "title": "Chapter 28 — Meeting Her Father", "content": "<h1>Chapter 28 — Meeting Her Father<\/h1>\n<p>Vikram insisted on going himself first, before any formal process began. Naina had offered to arrange it more gently — a family gathering, some occasion where his presence wouldn't feel so deliberately loaded — but he refused.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to speak to him myself, once, before my own family approaches yours properly,\" he said. \"Man to man, without the weight of two families sitting in the room yet. If he's going to refuse this, I'd rather hear it plainly from him first, before my parents ever have to sit through that refusal themselves.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina's father lived in the family home outside Gurugram, a modest but well-kept house with a small garden in front, marigolds planted in neat rows along the path, the kind of garden that spoke of a man who valued order even in the things meant to be simply beautiful. He took the car for the drive that morning. He arrived on a Sunday morning, dressed the way an officer instinctively dresses for anything that matters — a plain, well-fitted shirt tucked into pressed trousers, black shoes polished to a proper shine, a matching leather belt, nothing elaborate, everything precise.<\/p>\n<p>The man who opened the door was not what Vikram had pictured, though he couldn't have said exactly what he'd expected instead. Naina's father was close to fifty, but carried none of the softness that age usually brought — broad through the shoulders still, his back straight in the unconscious way of a man who had spent decades being told to stand at attention until it stopped being an instruction and simply became his posture. His hair had greyed at the temples, but his eyes were sharp, assessing Vikram in the doorway with the quick, thorough once-over of a man who had spent a career reading other soldiers in under five seconds.<\/p>\n<p>\"Captain Rathore,\" he said. Not a question. He'd clearly been told this visit was coming.<\/p>\n<p>\"Sir. Thank you for seeing me.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Come in.\" He stepped aside, and Vikram followed him into a small sitting room, sparse and orderly, a few old photographs on the wall — a much younger version of the man in Army dress, medals Vikram recognised from a campaign two decades past, his wife standing beside him in one faded picture, both of them impossibly young.<\/p>\n<p>They sat across from each other, tea appearing a few minutes later, brought in by Naina's mother, who gave Vikram a brief, searching look before retreating again.<\/p>\n<p>\"You've come to ask for my daughter,\" her father said, without preamble, once the tea had been poured and the door had closed again. \"I'd rather you say it plainly than dance around it. I've never had patience for men who dance around things.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I have, sir. I want to marry Naina. I wanted to come myself first, before my own family approaches yours properly, so you could hear it directly from me before anything more formal begins.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father studied him without saying anything, and Vikram made himself sit still under it, understanding that this silence was itself a kind of test.<\/p>\n<p>\"You're a Short Service Commission officer,\" her father said finally. \"Ten years, if I remember what Naina's told her mother. What comes after that?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I haven't fully decided, sir. Something in government service, most likely. I have a few years still to think it through properly.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"A few years of postings, transfers, operations you won't be able to tell your own wife about until they're already over.\" Her father's voice was even, not unkind, but carrying the weight of a man who had lived every word he was saying. \"I know exactly what you are, Captain. I was you once, a lifetime ago now. Fit, capable, certain that love was strong enough to carry any weight this life put on it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Sir—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Let me finish.\" He held up a hand, not sharply, just firmly. \"I'm not questioning your character. From everything I've heard, you're a good man. Steady. The kind of officer other men trust with their lives, I'd guess, and I don't say that lightly.\" He set his cup down. \"This isn't about you, Captain Rathore. This is about what I watched this exact life do to my own wife, for over twenty years, and what I am not willing to watch happen to my daughter.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I understand the risk, sir. I've thought about it as honestly as I know how.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You've thought about it. That's not the same as living inside it.\" Her father leaned forward slightly, his voice dropping, something more personal creeping into it now. \"My wife spent half our marriage not knowing if I was alive on a given day. I missed things a father should never miss. I came home once with an injury I never fully explained to her, because some of it wasn't mine to explain. She carried all of that alone, every single time, and she carried it well, because she's a strong woman. But I watched what it cost her, piece by piece, year by year. I am not eager to hand my own daughter the same bill to pay.\"<\/p>\n<p>The room was quiet for a moment, the tea between them long gone cold, a ceiling fan turning slowly overhead the only sound besides their two voices. Vikram felt the full weight of the man's argument settle over him, understanding, plainly, that there was no clever answer that could dismantle it — because it wasn't built on prejudice or arrogance. It was built on decades of lived, specific truth.<\/p>\n<p>\"I can't promise you a life without that cost, sir,\" Vikram said finally. \"I won't insult you by pretending I can. You'd see through it in a second, and you'd be right to.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Do you imagine anything you say today changes what I already know?\" Her father set his tea down untouched, his hands folding together on the table between them.<\/p>\n<p>\"Twenty-eight years I gave 18 Grenadiers. Tololing, the thirteenth of June. Tiger Hill, three weeks after that. Between those two mountains my battalion lost thirty-four men. Carried seventy-eight more down on stretchers.\" He said the numbers flatly, the way a man reads out figures he's recited too many times to feel them fresh anymore.<\/p>\n<p>\"The whole country was drunk on it that year, Captain. Flags on every rooftop. Candles lit for boys whose names people learned off the front page and forgot within the month.\" He looked away, toward the window, then back. \"I felt that pride myself. Stood on that ridge the morning we took Tiger Hill and felt it same as anybody. But I stood between those two mountains again afterward, in the quiet that comes once the shouting stops, and I saw something the papers never printed. The mothers and fathers who lost sons in that josh — they never got repaired by it. The nation moved on to the next headline. Those families didn't move anywhere. They just learned to carry an emptiness the rest of us got to go home and forget.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a moment, turning his cup slowly on the table without drinking from it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I went back once, alone, after it was all over. Stood between those two mountains again. Tololing behind me, Tiger Hill ahead. Made myself do a proper accounting — what I'd actually won, what I'd actually lost that summer.\" He met Vikram's eyes squarely. \"What I'd won was the nation's pride. Villages sleeping safe now because a highway stayed open. What I'd lost sat a lot closer to my own chest. I was thirty-five that year, senior enough to have sent boys barely out of their teens up that mountain under my own command. Senior enough to have written more letters to more mothers than any man my age should ever have to write.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"A battalion isn't a posting, Captain, whatever the recruitment posters tell you. It's a family you didn't choose. I buried and carried out more of that family in three weeks than most men bury in an entire lifetime of ordinary grief.\"<\/p>\n<p>He paused, studying Vikram carefully now.<\/p>\n<p>\"Naina told me about your friend. Reddy.\" His voice had gone quieter, something almost gentle entering it. \"I know what losing a friend that close does to a man, Captain. I watched it happen to better men than either of us. Lived it myself, more than once. If losing one man cut you as deep as I suspect it did — imagine carrying that same loss thirty-four times over. Then imagine spending every year after wondering if the next mountain's going to ask you to carry it again.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a moment before going on.<\/p>\n<p>\"I was your age once too. Years before Kargil ever found me. Doing operations in the North East, winning small encounters that never made any newspaper, losing friends in the same quiet way nobody outside a battalion ever mourns properly. Fearless, the way only a man that age can afford to be. I look at you sitting there, and I'm not looking at a stranger. I'm looking at myself, decades ago, before I understood what all that fearlessness was going to cost the people who loved me.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't say this to frighten you, Captain. I say it because you're sitting in my house asking for my daughter, and you haven't done the arithmetic yet. I've spent my whole retirement doing it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Sir, I understand the risks of my service.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You understand the risk to yourself. That's not the arithmetic I'm speaking of.\" He studied Vikram with the patience of a man accustomed to reading recruits faster than they could read themselves. \"You're Para SF, Captain. A man who has made a home out of danger cannot simply hand the key to someone else and call it safety.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then tell me what would satisfy you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Resignation.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram was quiet a long moment, the word landing heavier than he'd expected it to. Resignation wasn't a small thing to offer — it meant walking away from the one identity he'd built his entire adult life around, the uniform that had shaped how he stood, how he spoke, how he understood his own worth. He turned it over properly before answering, unwilling to hand over a promise this large without actually weighing it first.<\/p>\n<p>\"That's not a small thing you're asking, sir,\" he said finally. \"But I can do it, for Naina. After the wedding. Give me that much time.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father's expression didn't change, though something behind his eyes went very still. \"You've decided, sitting there, that you mean it. I don't doubt the sincerity. I doubt the outcome. Duty doesn't wait politely for a man to finish keeping his promises, however sincerely he made them.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I've never broken a commitment in my life, sir.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I was an infantry soldier, Captain. Nothing grander than that in the years before Kargil. My family lived through everything a soldier's family lives through, and I only learned the full weight of most of it years after the fact. In fragments. The way a man learns things his own wife decided he didn't need to carry at the time.\" His voice had gone quieter now, something raw entering it that hadn't been there through all the talk of mountains and medals. He looked down at his own hands a moment before going on.<\/p>\n<p>\"Naina was admitted to hospital once, as a child. Nearly died, I'm told — though I only heard the word 'nearly' months later, softened for my benefit. I was in Manipur when it happened, fighting insurgents in terrain that swallowed weeks the way the plains swallow an afternoon. No leave sanctioned. And even if it had been, the better part of a week's travel just to reach a bedside two and a half thousand kilometres away. A bedside that might not have waited that long for me.\"<\/p>\n<p>He looked up again, meeting Vikram's eyes.<\/p>\n<p>\"My wife stood in that hospital alone, Captain. Signing forms she didn't fully understand. Making decisions no mother should have to make without a husband beside her. Watching our daughter fight for her life while I sat in barracks further from home than most men ever travel in their lives — entirely unaware there was anything to fight for at all, until a letter finally reached me, weeks late, telling me she'd already recovered.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then you've simply never been tested against this one.\" Her father leaned forward slightly. \"I raised Naina the way I did — loud, unafraid, arguing with her own father without flinching — because it was the one thing in my entire disciplined life I got to build exactly as I wanted, without the Army's permission. If she marries you, I watch that get taken from her too, slowly, the same patient way the Army takes everything else from the families standing behind men like us. Not because you'd ever raise a hand to her. Because a woman married to fear learns to make herself smaller, on her own, without anyone teaching her how.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram sat with that a long moment, understanding, somewhere beneath his own protest, that he had no honest answer left to give.<\/p>\n<p>\"I can't promise she'll never make herself smaller, sir. I won't pretend to have an answer good enough to erase that fear entirely.\" He held the older man's gaze steadily. \"But I can tell you what I've already told Naina. I intend to do everything in my power to make sure she's never carrying that cost alone. Not the way your wife did. I've already made mistakes in that direction — I let her sit through fifteen days not knowing if I was alive, because I didn't think to warn her properly, and it nearly broke her, and it taught me something I won't forget. I'm not a finished man yet, sir. I'm still learning how to love her the right way. But I am learning, and I don't intend to stop.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father regarded him steadily, something unreadable moving behind his eyes.<\/p>\n<p>\"That's an honest answer,\" he said finally. He was quiet a moment longer. \"It doesn't change my answer, Captain. I'm sorry. I believe you mean every word of what you just said. I believed I meant every word of the promises I made my own wife too, all those years ago. Meaning it isn't always enough to change what this life demands of a man, and of the woman standing behind him.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Sir—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I've already begun arrangements for her marriage elsewhere,\" her father said, quiet but final. \"A family I've known for years. A man in a stable profession, home every evening, no operations, no fifteen days of silence. I know it isn't what she wants right now. I am asking her to trust that I know something about what she'll want in twenty years that she can't fully see from where she's standing today.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram sat with that, understanding that the conversation, for all its respect on both sides, had already reached its end.<\/p>\n<p>\"May I ask you something, sir?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Go ahead.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"If it had been your daughter standing where your wife once stood — if she'd been the one asking you to trust her enough to choose this life anyway — would you have said no to her too?\"<\/p>\n<p>Something moved across her father's face then, brief and complicated, gone before Vikram could fully read it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know,\" he said finally, quieter than before. \"That's the honest answer, Captain. I don't know. But it isn't a question I have to answer today, because it isn't my daughter standing on that side of it. It's Naina, standing where my wife once stood, and I know exactly what that side costs a woman. That's the only answer I have for you.\"<\/p>\n<p>He stood, and Vikram understood the meeting had ended.<\/p>\n<p>\"Thank you for hearing me, sir,\" Vikram said, standing as well. \"Whatever you decide, I want you to know I respect the reasons behind it. I don't think you're wrong to be afraid. I only wish it didn't cost us what it's about to cost.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father walked him to the door, and paused there a moment, his hand on the frame.<\/p>\n<p>\"For what it's worth, Captain,\" he said, \"if things had been different — if I hadn't already lived what I lived — I think I would have liked you for a son-in-law. That's not nothing, coming from a man who doesn't say such things easily.\"<\/p>\n<p>It was, Vikram understood, walking back to the car a few minutes later beneath a sky that had gone entirely indifferent to what had just been decided beneath it, the closest thing to a blessing he was ever going to receive from that house, and it did nothing at all to soften the refusal underneath it.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 31, "title": "Chapter 29 — What Naina Said to Her Father", "content": "<h1>Chapter 29 — What Naina Said to Her Father<\/h1>\n<p>The veranda hadn't changed. The chair, the dusk, the garden going quiet under it — all exactly as before, as though the evening itself remembered where this conversation had started. Only the marigolds seemed to know something had shifted, their smell thinner now, more bitter, carried on air a distant temple bell was already dividing into hours. She did not wait for an invitation this time. She simply sat.<\/p>\n<p>\"He came to see you,\" she said. Not a question.<\/p>\n<p>\"He did.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"And you said no.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father set down the newspaper he wasn't really reading. \"I said no.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina sat across from him, her back straight, her hands steady in her lap despite everything churning underneath. \"I want to hear it from you directly. Not through Ma. Not secondhand. I want to hear the actual reason, in your own words, looking at me.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father studied her closely, and something in his face softened slightly at whatever he saw there — not defiance exactly, something steadier than that.<\/p>\n<p>\"I told him what I told your mother,\" he said. \"What this life did to her. What I'm not willing to watch happen to you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's your reason. I want to know if it's a fair one.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Fair has very little to do with it, Naina. I'm not trying to be fair. I'm trying to protect you.\" His voice had an edge of frustration now, though it wasn't directed at her exactly — more at the whole impossible position he'd put himself in. \"You think I enjoy this? Sitting here, watching you look at me like I've taken something from you? I know exactly what I'm taking. I lived the alternative for twenty-five years.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then let me live it too. Let me make that choice with my own eyes open, the way you and Ma made it with yours.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You haven't lived it yet. You don't know what you're asking for.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Neither did you, Baba, when you married Ma. Nobody knows what they're asking for until they're already inside it. That's what choosing someone means.\" Naina leaned forward, her voice steady despite the tightness in her throat. \"I watched him sit across from your friend's daughter's wedding photographs and answer every question your side of the family could invent. I watched him carry grief for a friend he lost, and still show up for me the next evening because he didn't want his pain to become my burden. I watched him go quiet for weeks worrying about what a fifteen-day silence had done to me, and apologise for it more than I asked him to. I know exactly what kind of man I'm choosing, Baba. I don't need you to protect me from finding that out. I've already found it out.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father was quiet a long moment, something working behind his eyes that she hadn't seen there before — not stubbornness exactly, something closer to grief.<\/p>\n<p>\"You sound like your mother,\" he said finally, quietly. \"She used to argue with me exactly like this. Same stubbornness. Same refusal to let me have the last word just because I was older and thought I knew better.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Maybe she was right to argue.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Maybe she was.\" He rubbed a hand over his face, suddenly looking older than she'd ever seen him look, the evening light doing him no favours. \"That doesn't change what I watched her carry, Naina. Being right about wanting me didn't make the twenty years that followed any easier for her to live through.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Was she happy, Baba? Underneath all of it — was she happy she chose you?\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't answer right away, and when he did, his voice had gone rough. \"I believe so. I've never had the courage to ask her plainly, in case the answer was something I couldn't bear to hear. But I believe so.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then let me have the chance to answer that same question for myself, twenty-five years from now. Don't take that chance away from me because you're afraid of what my answer might cost me. Let it be my cost to weigh, the way it was Ma's to weigh.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father sat with that for a long time, the evening going dark around them, neither of them moving to turn on another light, the garden's marigolds fading slowly from colour into shape into shadow, a lone dog barking somewhere several houses away, the sound carrying easily in the still, cooling air.<\/p>\n<p>\"I hear you,\" he said finally. \"I want you to know that much — I hear every word of what you're saying, and none of it falls on deaf ears, whatever I decide.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"But you're still not changing your answer.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know yet,\" he said, and it was the most honest thing he'd said to her in the entire conversation. \"Give me time, Naina. Not forever. But some time, to sit with everything you've just said, instead of answering you out of an old wound before I've properly thought it through.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something in her finally gave way at that, the composure she'd been holding onto through the whole conversation cracking at last, and tears she'd refused to let fall in front of him began slipping free despite everything.<\/p>\n<p>\"Come here,\" her father said, his voice suddenly gentler than it had been the entire evening, patting the space on the charpai beside him.<\/p>\n<p>She hesitated only a moment before moving to sit next to him, and he put an arm around her, drawing her in the way he had when she was small enough to fit entirely against his side. His other hand came up to rest against her head, smoothing her hair back slowly, the same steady, careful motion he'd used to calm her as a child when nightmares had woken her crying in the middle of the night.<\/p>\n<p>\"I know you think I'm being cruel,\" he said quietly, his hand still moving gently over her hair. \"I promise you I'm not. Whatever else I am tonight, I am not cruel to you. I am afraid for you, and fear makes fathers do things that look like cruelty even when the heart behind them is breaking just as much as yours is.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It doesn't feel like love right now, Baba.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know it doesn't. Love doesn't always feel like the thing you want it to feel like, especially from a father who's watched too much to trust easily.\" He pressed a kiss to the top of her head, the way he had a hundred times in her childhood. \"You are the best thing I have ever had a hand in making, Naina. Every part of this — the arguing, the fear, even the no I gave that boy — comes from loving you more than I know how to say properly out loud. I need you to hold onto that, even while you're angry with me. Especially while you're angry with me.\"<\/p>\n<p>She cried properly then, into his shoulder, and he held her the way he had when she was a girl, saying nothing more. He just let his hand move slowly and steadily through her hair until her breathing evened out and the worst of it had passed.<\/p>\n<p>The whole veranda settled into a stillness that held both their griefs without needing to choose between them.<\/p>\n<p>It wasn't a yes. It wasn't even a real promise. But it was more than the flat refusal Vikram had carried out of that same door days earlier, and underneath the pain of the evening, Naina held onto something else too — the certainty, at least, that whatever her father decided, it would never once come from anything less than the whole of his love for her.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 32, "title": "Chapter 30 — The Final Answer", "content": "<h1>Chapter 30 — The Final Answer<\/h1>\n<p>He gave it a week before he spoke to her again, and in that week Naina watched him more closely than she ever had before — the way he sat a little longer over his morning tea, the way he seemed, once or twice, to be on the verge of saying something before deciding against it.<\/p>\n<p>He called her to the veranda on a Sunday evening, the same chair, the same low light, the ceiling fan overhead ticking faintly with each slow rotation, the season's first mosquitoes already testing the evening air, and she knew before he said a word that whatever verdict a week of thought had produced, it hadn't changed.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've thought about everything you said,\" he began. \"I want you to know that. I didn't dismiss a single word of it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"But.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"But my answer is still no, Naina.\" He said it plainly, without the harshness of the first conversation, something gentler and more exhausted in his voice instead. \"Not because I doubt him. Not because I doubt you. Because twenty-five years of living the exact fear you're asking me to set aside isn't something a week of your very good arguments can undo in me. I wish it were. I've tried, this whole week, to let it be undone. It isn't.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina sat very still, absorbing it, some part of her having already known this was coming and some part of her still not fully prepared for how much it would hurt to hear it confirmed.<\/p>\n<p>\"The family I mentioned,\" her father continued, quieter now. \"I've already spoken with them again this week. They're good people, Naina. The boy is decent, steady, home every evening of his life. I know that means nothing to you right now. I'm asking you to trust that it will mean something, later, when the version of you that's simply in love has had time to also become the version of you that has to actually live inside a marriage, day after day, for forty years.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You're asking me to trust a version of myself I haven't become yet, over the version of myself sitting right in front of you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I am. I know exactly how unfair that sounds.\" He reached over and took her hand, the way he had the week before. \"I know it will be difficult for you to stop loving him, Naina, even after everything I've decided. A heart doesn't obey an order the way a soldier does, however much a father might wish it did. I'm asking you to let this marriage happen anyway, and to let time do what time does — soften some things, clarify others. I've watched it happen to enough people to believe it, even if you can't yet.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"And if it doesn't soften? If I spend the next forty years missing a life I never got to choose?\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father didn't have an answer for that. He simply held her hand, his jaw tight, his own eyes bright in the low veranda light, and for a long moment neither of them said anything at all.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm sorry,\" he said finally, and his voice broke slightly on the words, the first real crack she'd heard in him through the entire ordeal. \"I know an apology doesn't undo the decision behind it. I need you to hear it anyway. I am sorry for the pain this is going to cost you. I am not sorry for trying to protect you from a pain I understand better than you do yet. Both of those things are true at once, and I don't know how to make them simpler than that.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina stood, and before her father could react, she moved to kneel in front of him, her hands finding his knees. It was the way daughters in that house had always shown their father a certain kind of reverence — not touching his feet, the way a son might, but close enough, low enough, that the gesture carried its own unmistakable weight.<\/p>\n<p>\"Baba, look at me,\" she said, her voice unsteady but her eyes lifted to his, refusing to look away even now. \"I am not asking you as Captain Rathore's future wife. I am asking you as the little girl who used to fall asleep on your chest waiting for your jeep, the one you promised, more than once, that you would never make choose between loving you and living her own life. Do you remember that promise? I do. I've carried it since I was maybe seven years old, some evening you probably don't even remember saying it.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father's hands, resting on the arms of the chair, had gone white-knuckled, his jaw working, though he said nothing.<\/p>\n<p>\"You taught me to be loud,\" she went on, her hands tightening slightly on his knees. \"You taught me to want things and say them out loud instead of swallowing them the way Ma had to. I am doing exactly that right now, Baba — the thing you raised me to be capable of doing — and you are the one telling me to swallow it instead. I don't understand how both of those things can come from the same man.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Baba, please.\" Her voice broke on the second word, all the composure she'd carried into that room dissolving at once, tears already slipping free before she could stop them. \"I am asking you as your daughter, not as someone arguing a case. Please. I have never asked you for anything the way I am asking you for this.\"<\/p>\n<p>She wiped at her face, and forced herself to keep going, unwilling to let the tears be the only thing she left him with. \"But I'm not asking you to ignore everything you know either, Baba. Think about it plainly. He has already offered to resign after the wedding — that's not nothing, that's a man handing over the one identity he's built his whole life around, for me. I am not walking into this blind the way you're afraid I am. I have watched him for months now, not weeks. I have seen how he carries grief, how he carries guilt, how he corrects his own mistakes the moment he sees them. You spent a lifetime learning to read a man's character in five seconds at a doorway — you said so yourself. Look at what you actually saw when he sat across from you. Was it a reckless boy, or was it a steady one who told you the truth even when a lie would have served him better?\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father did not look away from her, though something in his own face had gone rigid, held together by will alone. \"I know you haven't,\" he said quietly. \"That is exactly why this is so difficult, beta. If you were asking me for something small, I could give it without a second thought. You are asking me to risk you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then risk me. Let it be my risk to take. I am not the same as Ma was at nineteen, walking into this with no warning at all. I have your whole life as a lesson already, Baba. I know exactly what I'm choosing, with both eyes open, in a way you never had the chance to.\"<\/p>\n<p>Her father was quiet a moment, and when he spoke again, his voice had dropped lower, something almost pleading underneath the weight of it now. \"Do you actually know, Naina — do you know who Vikram is, in the way I know him, from the file, from men I've spoken to who've served near his unit? He isn't a normal soldier standing behind a desk somewhere counting down a peaceful posting. He's Para SF. You have no real idea what that means for the rest of a service life like his.<\/p>\n<p>\"It means active operations, year after year, not the occasional posting to somewhere quiet. It means always somewhere near the firing, and not always inside our own borders either — I don't need to say more plainly what that implies, and I won't, not in this room.<\/p>\n<p>\"Men like him don't get a family life in any ordinary sense. They get leave, snatched between operations, and a wife who learns to live inside the gaps. And every single time he goes back, the odds of him coming home whole get a little worse than the last time, not better — a serious injury, if he's fortunate enough for it to only be that.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a moment, choosing his next words carefully.<\/p>\n<p>\"Most men take the safer route through an SSC — a steady posting, a clean ten years, out the other side with their nerves mostly intact. He chose the harder road on purpose. Twice over.\"<\/p>\n<p>Naina said nothing, waiting, her hands still resting on his knees.<\/p>\n<p>\"That tells me something his own words never will, however carefully he chooses them. It tells me he's an ambitious man. The kind who doesn't know how to take a step back once he's decided to climb. Men like that don't stop climbing for a wife, or a child, or a father-in-law's fear. They keep moving forward, because that's the only direction they've ever properly learned. And that forward motion, Naina, however much glory it earns him — that's exactly what costs a family everything it never signed up to lose.<\/p>\n<p>\"It comes for the ones who'll give it everything. Without ever once being asked to choose between the mission and the wife waiting at home. I've watched dozens of young officers exactly like him, serving alongside them, and watched every one of them make that same silent choice, year after year, long after the wedding photographs had faded on some mantelpiece.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It has never only been yours to take. Not from the day I first held you.\" His voice, usually so measured, had thinned into something rawer than she'd ever heard from him. \"I have made my decision. I will not pretend it was easy, and I will not pretend I am certain of it either. But it stands.\"<\/p>\n<p>She stood there a long moment, tears running freely now, understanding — not with acceptance, but with something closer to grief arriving ahead of it — that this door had actually, finally, closed.<\/p>\n<p>\"Baba,\" she said once more, quieter now, almost a whisper, the fight gone out of it entirely. \"Please.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't answer that time. He simply reached out and drew her into an embrace she hadn't expected, holding her the way he'd held her as a child, and she felt him trembling too, however briefly, before he let her go.<\/p>\n<p>By the time she'd cried herself quiet, something in her had settled into a heavier, more resigned stillness than the raw grief of the moment before — not acceptance exactly, but the particular calm that arrives once a person has finally stopped fighting a wall and simply started deciding what to do standing in front of it.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll need to tell him,\" she said.<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It won't be easy.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Nothing about this has been easy, beta. I don't imagine that part will be either.\" He squeezed her hand once, and let it go. \"Whatever you decide to say to him, and however you decide to say it — I trust you to do it with the same grace you've shown through this entire conversation. That's not nothing, coming from a daughter I've just broken the heart of.\"<\/p>\n<p>She stood eventually, her legs unsteady beneath her, and walked back into the house without another word, understanding that the fight, in the end, had never really been winnable — not against an argument, which she could have countered forever, but against twenty-five years of a wound that no single conversation, however honest, was ever going to fully close.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 33, "title": "Chapter 31 — What She Had to Tell Him", "content": "<h1>Chapter 31 — What She Had to Tell Him<\/h1>\n<p>She asked him to meet her at the gulmohar park, the same bench, though neither of them said out loud why she'd chosen that particular place.<\/p>\n<p>He knew before she said a word. He'd seen enough of her face over the last months to read what a week of silence and a heavy walk to a park bench meant, and he sat down beside her already braced for it.<\/p>\n<p>\"He said no,\" Vikram said quietly. Not a question.<\/p>\n<p>\"He said no.\" Her voice was steady, though he could hear what the steadiness was costing her. \"Final. I made every argument I had. I made some I didn't know I had until I was standing in front of him making them. It didn't change anything.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram was quiet, looking out at the same tree that had once been bare and was now, again, in flower.<\/p>\n<p>\"What happens now?\" he asked.<\/p>\n<p>\"The family he's already spoken to. The boy is decent, apparently. Stable. Everything my father has decided I need instead of what I actually want.\" Her jaw tightened. \"I don't have a plan beyond that, Vikram. I don't know what I'm supposed to do with an answer that final.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"We could still fight it. Together, properly. Elope, if it came to that. People do.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't.\" She said it gently, but firmly, cutting him off before the idea could fully form between them. \"I've thought about that too, more than I'd like to admit. And I can't do it, Vikram. Not because I don't love you enough. Because I know what it would cost my mother, watching her husband and her daughter become strangers to each other over a decision she'd be caught in the middle of forever. She raised me with nothing but love and patience my whole life, every single day of it, without once asking anything back for herself. I can't repay all of that by breaking the family apart entirely.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"So we just — let this happen. Let him choose for you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm not letting him choose for me.\" Something sharp came into her voice now, though it wasn't aimed at Vikram. \"I'm choosing to honour a father who has never once, in twenty-one years, given me a single reason to distrust his love for me, even when I hate the decision he's made. That's different from being chosen for. I need you to understand the difference, because I need to believe it myself right now.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet a moment, gathering herself before she continued.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've spent my whole life as the girl who doesn't bow to tradition simply because it asks her to,\" she said. \"You know that about me better than almost anyone alive. I've broken rules I thought deserved breaking, and never once lost a night's sleep counting the pieces.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"This isn't that, Vikram. I need you to understand the difference, because I think you're standing there waiting for me to break one more rule, the way I always have.\" She held his gaze, something fierce and unwavering in it despite the tears she wasn't quite managing to hold back. \"This was never about tradition. It was never about what a good daughter owes a family, or what a village expects of her, or any of the small, borrowed rules I've never once cared to follow blindly. This is about two people who raised me loud enough, free enough, to be standing here right now with the courage to love you this completely in the first place.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Naina—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Let me finish.\" Her voice caught, briefly, before she steadied it again. \"Everything I am — every piece of me you've ever loved, every ounce of the loudness you once asked me to keep — none of it is something I built alone, out of nothing. It was given to me. Deliberately. Carefully, over years. My father, who could have raised a quiet, obedient daughter and chose instead to raise one who argues, who questions, who was never once asked to shrink. My mother, who never told me to make myself smaller than the room could hold.\" She wiped at her eyes, impatient with her own tears. \"How am I supposed to take everything they poured into me, and spend it walking straight through the one door they've asked me not to open? What would that make me, if I did? Not brave. Not free. Just a daughter who took everything she was given and spent it the first time it actually cost something to keep.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"So you're ready to destroy yourself instead,\" Vikram said quietly, something raw underneath the words. \"That's what this is, isn't it? You're choosing to burn your own happiness down so no one else has to carry the ash.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"No.\" Her answer came fast, certain, cutting through whatever he'd been about to say next. \"I'm not destroying myself, Vikram. I'm refusing to be the one who sets my family on fire. There's a difference, even if it doesn't look like one from where you're standing.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Explain it to me, then. Because from here, it looks exactly the same.\"<\/p>\n<p>She took a breath, steadying herself before she continued. \"My father gave this country the years a man is supposed to keep for himself — the ones spent watching his children grow, sitting at a table that's never once, in all my memory, been full without an empty chair at it. He missed half my childhood on purpose, Vikram, and never once let any of us feel the weight of what that cost him. That's where his respect comes from. Not something handed to him. Something he built, quietly, out of exactly the years most men refuse to give away.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know what your father sacrificed.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't. Not the way I do, having grown up inside that empty chair.\" Her voice dropped, slower now, more deliberate, each word laid down like something she'd carried a long way to say. \"This was never a simple scale, Vikram — my family's pride sitting on one side, my own happiness on the other. It was always about a father and a mother who chose, on purpose, to raise a daughter loud enough to want exactly this — in a world that spends every single day teaching girls to take up less room than they're given. Do you understand how rare that actually is? What it costs a middle-class Haryana household to raise a daughter who argues back, who questions, who was never once asked to shrink?\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't answer, letting her continue.<\/p>\n<p>\"If I walk away from him now, for a love he's afraid of — do you know what that actually teaches every other girl watching us from some other house, some other father's shadow? It doesn't teach them love wins. It teaches their fathers that a loud daughter is a dangerous thing to raise. That the very freedom mine gave me is what finally broke his family apart. I would become the proof every cautious father needs — that a daughter given too much sky eventually flies straight out of it.\" Her eyes were bright, fierce, unwavering despite the tears threatening to spill. \"I won't be that lesson, Vikram. I won't be the reason some father, somewhere, decides his own daughter doesn't deserve to be raised this loud. That isn't a sacrifice I'm willing to make. Not even for you. Not even for this.\"<\/p>\n<p>Vikram sat with that, something in his chest going very still and very heavy at once.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know how to accept this,\" he said finally. \"I don't think I want to pretend I can.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't need you to accept it easily. I don't think I have either.\" Her eyes were bright now, close to spilling over, though she held them steady through sheer will. \"I need you to know that this isn't the end of what I feel for you, even if it has to be the end of what we're allowed to build together. I don't think there's a version of me, ten years from now or twenty, that doesn't still carry some part of this. Of you.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Naina—\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Let me finish, please, because I don't think I can say this twice.\" She turned to face him properly, her hand finding his on the bench between them. \"You gave me the best months of my life. You taught me what it feels like to be loved by someone who actually sees me, not a smaller, easier version of me. Whatever happens after this — whoever I end up married to, whatever life I end up living — that's going to be true forever. Nobody can take that back from either of us, not even my father.\"<\/p>\n<p>He didn't trust his voice enough to answer right away. He simply held her hand, and they sat there together under the flowering tree, both of them carrying the shared grief of two people who had done everything right and lost anyway, not to a villain, not to a betrayal, but to a father's twenty-five-year-old fear that neither of them had the power to undo.<\/p>\n<p>\"When?\" he finally asked. \"The wedding.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Soon. He wants it settled quickly, I think, before either of us finds a way to change his mind.\" She wiped at her eyes, composing herself with visible effort. \"I wanted to tell you myself, properly, before it became something you heard about secondhand. You deserved that much.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Thank you,\" he said, though the word felt entirely inadequate to what he actually meant.<\/p>\n<p>They sat there a long while after that, neither of them in any hurry to be the one who stood up first and made the moment officially over. Both of them understood that whatever came next — however many evenings still remained before her wedding took this particular version of them away for good — this bench, and this tree, and this exact grief, would stay with them for the rest of their lives.<\/p>\n<p>Somewhere in that long, unhurried silence, Naina finally let go of whatever composure she'd been holding since she'd sat down. She leaned sideways, her head finding his lap the way it always eventually did, and this time the tears came properly — not the careful, contained kind she'd managed through most of the conversation, but something deeper, her shoulders shaking with it, years of being the strong one finally giving way now that there was nothing left to protect.<\/p>\n<p>He didn't say anything right away. He simply let his hand settle into her hair, moving slowly through it, the same unhurried rhythm she'd once taught him, and waited, patient, for the crying to run its course. He felt his own throat tighten more than once, the ache of it climbing dangerously close to the surface, but he held it back, deliberately, understanding without needing to think it through properly that she needed him steady right now, not matching her grief with his own.<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm sorry,\" she said eventually, her voice thick against his leg. \"I told myself I'd get through this without falling apart.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't need to apologise for that. Not to me. Not ever.\"<\/p>\n<p>She let him hold her a while longer, his hand still moving gently through her hair long after the worst of the tears had passed, and neither of them said anything more, both of them understanding that some grief simply needed to be sat with quietly, rather than explained.<\/p>\n<p>I stood beside them in full, uninterrupted bloom that evening, every branch of mine weighed down with the same fire-orange flowers I had been too withheld, too cautious, too bare to offer on the first afternoon these two ever sat beneath me.<\/p>\n<p>I remember that beginning the way something rooted and old remembers — not in pictures, but in feeling, the way a body remembers a wound long after the mind has stopped counting the years. Two strangers, unsure where to look, a voice finally given a face.<\/p>\n<p>I remember every ordinary evening after that one, gathered one on top of the other the slow, patient way soil gathers rain: a fog rolling through an empty park while she first called this bench hers, a woman still in uniform, her cap set down beside her, crying into his chest anyway, a grief handed over and held without flinching; and later, his own grief handed back to her in turn, held just as carefully, without her once needing to be asked twice; a promise about pine that travelled all the way to a mountain and back again.<\/p>\n<p>I remember, too, that quieter evening not so long before this one, when she told me she couldn't live without him, that she had never even loved herself the way she loved him, and asked me plainly to be her witness to it. I let a single flower fall directly into her open palm in answer, so she would know, without needing to be told twice, that I had heard every word.<\/p>\n<p>I held all of it, quietly, the way only a thing with no voice of its own can hold something completely, without once needing to interrupt it.<\/p>\n<p>If I could have spoken, I would have told them everything I had ever seen — every quiet, ordinary miracle of two people slowly choosing each other, again and again, across months that felt, from where I stood, like the fastest thing I had ever watched grow. I would have told them how rare this had actually been, how many thousand ordinary afternoons had passed beneath my branches over all my years, and how few of them ever looked anything like this. I would have told them not to let this be the end of it — that some things are only lost if the people carrying them agree to call it lost. But I had no voice for any of that, only flowers, and flowers were the only language left to me now.<\/p>\n<p>So I let them go, the way I had already let go of a hundred things I never once had the power to keep. A single blossom first, drifting loose from somewhere high in my own canopy, turning slowly on its way down before landing without a sound on the bench between them. Then another.<\/p>\n<p>Then, as though something long held had finally broken open in me, a whole slow scattering of them: petals falling steadily through the still evening air, catching in Naina's hair, settling on Vikram's shoulder, gathering at their feet in a small, fire-coloured drift neither of them noticed enough to brush away. It was the exact colour it had taken me an entire season to learn how to offer, falling now the way tears fall — unhurried, involuntary, entirely outside my own power to call back once I'd started.<\/p>\n<p>It felt, that evening, as if the whole park had finally learned how to speak to itself, word of what was happening on my one bench passing tree to tree the way only rooted things can pass a thing along without a single sound escaping into the air above us.<\/p>\n<p>Near the park's far gate, a lone palash, in bloom at exactly the same hour, began shedding its own flame-orange petals in the same slow rhythm as mine. And beside the path near the entrance, an amaltas — its cascading gold usually a colour of celebration, of weddings and good news — let its own long golden strings of flowers fall too, that bright yellow drifting down now like something wept out of an ordinarily happy thing that had, for once, nothing happy left to say.<\/p>\n<p>Within minutes the path stood under a soft, drifting fall of fire-orange and gold together. The whole park was grieving, each of us in our own colour, all in the same unspoken language — the small, ordinary evening looking, for those few minutes, like the earth itself had decided to mourn one particular ending alongside me.<\/p>\n<p>Two people who could not hear a single word of what any of us was trying to tell them sat beneath me anyway, wrapped in a rain that was, in its own wordless way, everything none of us had ever learned how to say out loud.<\/p>\n<p>I did not know, letting the last of my flowers go that evening, whether I would ever get to hold a love like this one again — whole, offered freely, undeserving of the ending it had been handed.<\/p>\n<p>But I stood there anyway, rooted in the one place I had ever been permitted to belong. And quietly, patiently, in the only way an old and voiceless thing knows how to hope for anything at all, I began waiting: for whichever two people came next to sit beneath me, hand finding hand the way these two once had, and this time, simply this once, let the story finish the way this one never got the chance to.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}, {"chapter": 34, "title": "Chapter 32 — The Last Evening", "content": "<h1>Chapter 32 — The Last Evening<\/h1>\n<p>It was a week after her father's final refusal that a new fear began working its way through Naina, quiet and separate from her own grief, settling in the way cold settles into a house after the fire's gone out.<\/p>\n<p>Vikram hadn't cried. Not once, through the entire conversation at the bench. She understood, turning it over since, that his steadiness wasn't strength so much as something being dangerously contained — and she knew enough about men like him, soldiers who lived inside operations, to know what unspoken grief eventually cost a man who felt he had nothing left to lose.<\/p>\n<p>She could not let that happen to him. Not because of her.<\/p>\n<p>So when she called him, three days before her wedding, she wasn't simply asking for one final evening to say goodbye. She needed to make sure whatever she said to him that night left him steadier, not more broken.<\/p>\n<p>The call came three days before the wedding, and Naina's voice, when Vikram picked up, carried a steadiness he'd learned by now to recognise as the sound of a woman holding herself together with everything she had left.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to see you,\" she said. \"Once more. Before everything changes.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Whatever you need.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Not a fight. I'm not calling to ask you to help me change my father's mind, or to run away with me, or any of the things some part of me has spent three sleepless nights considering and rejecting one by one. I just want an evening. Something to hold onto, after.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'll be there. Wherever you want.\"<\/p>\n<p>There was a pause on the line, long enough that he thought she might have started crying and didn't want him to hear it. When she spoke again, her voice had thinned slightly, though she kept it steady.<\/p>\n<p>\"Thank you for not arguing with me about it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't have the right to argue with what you need right now. Only to give it to you.\"<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>He arrived early, the resort he'd booked for the evening sitting on the edge of the city, its grounds sprawling and manicured, a fine-dining restaurant set within it that overlooked a landscaped lawn and a pool lit from beneath the water's surface like something holding its own quiet light in reserve. The whole place carried the hush of somewhere built for exactly this kind of occasion — anniversaries, proposals, evenings people wanted to remember properly.<\/p>\n<p>He'd chosen it carefully: somewhere she'd never been before, somewhere no memory of anyone else could crowd into this particular evening, somewhere large enough and beautiful enough that it might, in some small way, match the size of what this evening actually needed to hold.<\/p>\n<p>He sat at the table now turning his glass in slow circles, rehearsing and discarding a dozen different ways to greet her, none of them adequate to an evening that both of them understood, without saying it aloud, was the last one they would ever properly have.<\/p>\n<p>She arrived in green — not the wedding lehenga green, something softer, an ordinary kurta she might have worn on any evening that wasn't this one — and when she sat down across from him, he saw immediately that she'd been crying earlier and had carefully repaired the evidence of it before coming.<\/p>\n<p>\"You look beautiful,\" he said, because it was true and because he didn't trust himself to say anything more complicated yet.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't. Not tonight. If you start with things like that, I won't make it through dinner.\" But she was smiling despite the warning, small and real, and something in her shoulders eased slightly at having said even that much honestly.<\/p>\n<p>They ordered too much food, the way people do when they're using the ordering itself as a way to postpone the harder conversation waiting underneath it. For a long while they talked about nothing that mattered: a film she'd seen and hadn't told him about, a dog she'd had as a child that used to wait by the gate every evening for her father's jeep, a story from her training days he'd somehow never heard before.<\/p>\n<p>He watched her laugh at her own memories, watched the way she tucked her hair back when something amused her, and understood, with a clarity that ached in his chest, that he was memorising her — deliberately, desperately, the way you memorise something you already know you're about to lose.<\/p>\n<p>It was somewhere past the second course that she set her fork down and looked at him for a long moment, something decided settling into her face.<\/p>\n<p>\"I never told you what Sunaina said to me. That day she surprised us at my flat, before you left for your village.\"<\/p>\n<p>He remembered it instantly — Sunaina leaning close to her sister in the kitchen while he'd stepped out for water, the low voice, the words he'd caught only the edges of. \"I always wondered what she said.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know you did. I watched you wonder about it for weeks and never told you, because I wasn't ready to say it out loud yet, not even to you.\" She turned her water glass a slow half-circle on the tablecloth, not quite looking at him now. \"She said, don't you dare leave this one. You'll never find another man like him in your life. She said it had taken her less than an hour to work out what had taken me months to admit to myself — that you were exactly the kind of man who could actually handle me. Not manage me. Handle me. A weaker man, she said, would probably try to shrink someone like me down to something easier to love, and a lesser one still might simply find me too much to stand beside.<\/p>\n<p>\"You were neither of those, she said. The way you talked — quiet, certain, never needing to raise your voice to be believed — was its own kind of passion, and that if I let a man like that go, it would be the worst mistake I ever made in my life.\" She looked up at him finally, eyes bright. \"My little sister said that to me after knowing you for one afternoon. I've thought about it nearly every day since. I'm thinking about it right now, sitting here, about to prove her right in the worst possible way.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You're not proving her wrong by choosing your father,\" he said quietly. \"I don't think loving someone and losing them because of something neither of you could control makes her advice wrong. It just makes the world harder than she knew it would be, when she said it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"That's a generous way to let me off the hook.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It isn't generosity. It's what I actually believe.\"<\/p>\n<p>She held his gaze steadily, something working behind her eyes too large for the small restaurant table to hold, and then reached across and took his hand, her fingers cold despite the warm room.<\/p>\n<p>\"I keep trying to find the version of tonight where I feel less like I'm failing you,\" she said. \"I haven't found it yet.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You're not failing me, Naina. Don't carry that on top of everything else you're already carrying.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then what do I call this? What do I call choosing my father over the person I love more than I've ever loved anyone?\"<\/p>\n<p>He turned her hand over in his, tracing the lines of her palm the way he had a hundred times before, buying himself a moment before he answered. \"I call it the same kind of love you've been showing your whole family this entire time. You didn't stop loving me to choose them. You're choosing them because you love them too, in a different shape, and because you understand what breaking your father would cost your mother, and because you're a better person than the version of this story where you'd have simply taken what you wanted and let everyone else pay for it.\"<\/p>\n<p>He pressed her palm briefly to his own chest. \"I don't think that's failure, Naina. I think it might be the hardest kind of love there is — the kind that costs the person doing the loving the most.\"<\/p>\n<p>She didn't answer that right away. She simply held his hand, her eyes bright and unblinking, and when she finally spoke, her voice had dropped to something barely above a whisper.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know how I'm supposed to survive not having you in my life.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You will. Not easily. Not without missing me in ways that will catch you off guard for years, probably. But you will, because you're the strongest person I've ever known, and because whatever life you build now, some part of what we had is going to live inside it anyway, whether either of us names it out loud or not.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a moment, something deciding settling into his face.<\/p>\n<p>\"I want to tell you something,\" he said, \"and I need you to actually hear it, not just let it pass like something people say to make an ending feel softer.\"<\/p>\n<p>She looked at him, waiting.<\/p>\n<p>\"One day, years from now, without either of us contriving it, without our paths ever crossing again — I am going to reach you. Not in person. Not through a voice carried down a line. Something quieter than both, and infinitely more patient. I do not get to choose the hour. It will not knock. It will not announce itself the way a letter does, or a name does, spoken aloud. It will arrive the way the first monsoon arrives upon a land grown parched beyond memory of rain — uninvited, unscheduled, and yet somehow already known to the earth before the first drop has even fallen. You will be standing somewhere entirely ordinary, occupied with something that owes nothing to me at all, when something within you simply stills — the way the air itself stills, holding its breath, in the instant before the sky remembers how to weep. That stillness will be me, arriving the only way I will still be able to. And in it, you will finally understand exactly how much this was.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet, something unreadable moving behind her eyes.<\/p>\n<p>\"That's an impossible thing to promise.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Maybe. I'm promising it anyway.\" He held her gaze steadily. \"And when it happens — whenever it happens — I want you to remember this exact moment. This sentence. So you know, when it finally reaches you, that I kept my word.\"<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet a moment longer, something graver settling into his face. \"There is something else too. Those three lines you once wrote me, in your own blood, when I was away and did not know if I would ever come back to read them — <i>I am waiting. You promised me you will come back. Come back alive, Vikram.<\/i> I have not forgotten a single word, nor will I, for as long as I live. A debt like that cannot truly be repaid. But this much I can promise you: whatever it takes, however long it takes — I will answer them. Not three lines. Whatever the full size of an answer to something that size actually requires. I may never repay the whole of what these months with you have given me. But that part, I swear to you, I will.\"<\/p>\n<p>Something in her face broke slightly at that, hearing it named aloud after so many months of neither of them ever mentioning it directly.<\/p>\n<p>She didn't answer that directly, only nodded once, slowly, something in her face suggesting she'd decided to believe him without fully understanding why.<\/p>\n<p>He was quiet another moment, and then, more carefully, he said the last thing he needed her to hear.<\/p>\n<p>\"There's one more promise I want from you tonight, not the other way around.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"What is it?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Whatever happens in your marriage, however close you and your husband become, however much honesty you decide a good marriage deserves — don't tell him about us. Not ever, Naina. I'm not asking this to protect myself. I'm asking because I don't think most men carry a truth like this one well, no matter how fair or reasonable they believe themselves to be walking into a marriage. I've seen what jealousy does to a man's memory of a woman he loves, even when nothing about her actually changed. I don't want that risk anywhere near you.\"<\/p>\n<p>She studied him closely, something faintly resistant in her expression. \"You don't think a marriage should be built on honesty?\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I think a marriage should be built on whatever keeps two people safe enough to actually love each other properly,\" he said. \"Some truths cost more to give away than they're worth to the person receiving them. This is one of them. Promise me, Naina. Please.\"<\/p>\n<p>She was quiet a long moment, and when she finally answered, her voice carried the reluctance of a woman agreeing to something she didn't yet fully understand the weight of.<\/p>\n<p>\"I promise,\" she said.<\/p>\n<p>He held her gaze a moment longer, wanting to believe the words had landed as deeply as he needed them to, and let it go, because there was nothing more he could do with a promise except trust it, the way you have to trust every promise you're not going to be present to see kept or broken.<\/p>\n<p>After dinner, she asked if they could go somewhere quiet. Not to talk. Just to not be finished yet.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>He'd booked a room at the same resort — not like Gurugram had once been, not stolen, not rushed. She wasn't married yet; there was nothing to hide from, except the fact that in three days she would belong, in every way the world recognised, to someone else. The room was spacious, softly lit, floor-to-ceiling windows looking out over the same landscaped lawn they'd dined beside earlier, the lights of the pool still glowing faintly gold in the dark below.<\/p>\n<p>They didn't rush toward anything once the door had shut behind them. She kicked off her sandals and sat on the edge of the bed, and he sat beside her, and for a long while they simply talked — quietly, unhurried, both of them circling everything except the one thing neither wanted to name aloud yet.<\/p>\n<p>At some point, without either of them quite marking the moment, he reached over and began, slowly, to loosen the pin holding her hair back, working through it with careful fingers until it fell loose around her shoulders the way he'd always liked it best. She let him, saying nothing, something in her easing at the simple familiarity of it, and after a while she shifted closer and let her head come to rest against his chest, the way it had a hundred times before.<\/p>\n<p>His hand found her hair properly then, moving slowly through it, and hers traced idle shapes against his shirt. For a long while they simply lay back together like that — talking sometimes, quiet other times, both of them stretching the ordinary comfort of the moment as far as it would go, neither in any hurry to reach whatever came next.<\/p>\n<p>\"I need you to understand something,\" she said eventually, still lying against his chest, her voice steady though the steadiness cost her everything she had left to give it. \"I loved you. Completely. Not as a phase I'll explain away in ten years when the house is full and the work is hard. Whatever comes after — whatever life I'm about to walk into — this was real. Us. And I'm not going to let either of us pretend otherwise just because it's ending.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"It wasn't one-sided,\" he said, his hand still moving slowly through her hair. \"Whatever your father believed. Whatever the world will go on believing about a story it will never fully hear. I loved you too. The whole real shape of it. Not just the easy parts.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Then don't make this a performance,\" she said, sitting up slightly to look at him. \"And don't rush it like you're afraid the night will run out before we finish. The night is going to run out anyway. I want every minute stretched as far as it will go.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"All right.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"And Vikram.\" Her voice caught, just slightly, before she steadied it again. \"Treat me the way you did the first time. Not like glass. Not like a woman you're already mourning. Like someone who is still here, right now, choosing you, one more time, with everything she has left to choose you with.\"<\/p>\n<p>He drew her up properly then, and they came together slowly, without ceremony, the way they had the very first time, because some part of both of them needed the ritual of it — nothing rushed, nothing left half-finished for memory to argue with later. She let herself be seen completely, without hesitation, and he looked at her the way a man looks at something he understands he will spend the rest of his life trying, and failing, to properly remember.<\/p>\n<p>What passed between them that night carried none of the urgency of the mountain, none of the raw hunger that had once surprised them both in a small guesthouse room. It was slower than that. Deeper. Every touch deliberate, every moment lingered a beat longer than it needed to, because there was no next time to save anything for. She held onto him with her whole body, her face buried in his neck, and he felt, more than heard, the small broken sound she made — grief finding a shape it could survive being.<\/p>\n<p>\"Stay with me,\" she whispered, somewhere in the middle of it, not asking him to move, only asking him to be there, entirely, for as long as either of them had left. \"Just stay with me.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I'm here,\" he said. \"I'm not going anywhere tonight.\"<\/p>\n<p>They moved together slowly, unhurried despite everything urging them to hurry, both of them trying to make an ending last as long as a beginning once had. When it finally overtook her, it came quiet, almost silent, her whole body trembling against him, tears slipping free that neither of them tried to stop or explain. He held her through it, and when it took him too, he pressed his face into her hair and let himself, for just a moment, feel the whole unbearable weight of what he was about to lose.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>\n<p>They stayed joined together afterward for a long while, neither of them willing to be the one who moved first, her fingers tracing slow, aimless patterns against his back.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't regret a single day of this,\" she said eventually, her voice thick. \"Not one. Not even tonight, even though it's the hardest thing I've ever had to do.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Neither do I.\"<\/p>\n<p>They didn't sleep, not properly. They lay together through the last hours of the night, talking in low voices about everything and nothing: memories they wanted to make sure the other one kept, small confessions neither had gotten around to making before.<\/p>\n<p>She told him about the birthdays she'd celebrated without her father as a child, more of them than she could easily count, because leave was never granted for something as small as a birthday, and how she'd promised herself, one of those years, that she'd never let anyone she loved find out what that particular kind of waiting felt like. He told her, quietly, that she already had — that every silent day of his postings, she'd carry a version of that same waiting now, and that he hated himself a little for it, even loving her the way he did.<\/p>\n<p>\"Don't hate yourself,\" she said, pressing her palm flat against his chest, over his heart. \"Hate the situation. Hate my father's fear, if you need somewhere to put the anger. Just don't hate the parts of yourself that gave me these months. They're the best months of my whole life. I won't let you turn them into something to apologise for.\"<\/p>\n<p>It was sometime before that, in the deeper part of the night, that something in Vikram finally gave way completely.<\/p>\n<p>He had held himself together through the whole evening, through dinner, through everything that came after — steady, present, the way he always was for her. But lying there now, her earlier tears finally slowing against his chest, something in him simply stopped holding.<\/p>\n<p>He turned, without quite deciding to, and let his own head come to rest in her lap instead, the way hers had rested in his so many times before. Naina went still with surprise for only a moment before her hands found his hair automatically, moving through it the way he'd always done for her.<\/p>\n<p>And then he cried — properly, for the first time since she'd known him, his shoulders shaking silently against her thigh, years of careful containment finally breaking loose all at once. He made almost no sound. It was, somehow, worse for its silence, the grief of a man who had never once been taught how to do this properly, improvising his way through it now because there was simply nothing left to hold it back with.<\/p>\n<p>\"I've got you,\" she said softly, her fingers moving gently through his hair, her own tears momentarily forgotten in the strange, quiet privilege of being the one who got to hold him this time. \"Let go. I'm right here.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know how to do this,\" he admitted, his voice wrecked, muffled against her leg. \"I don't think I've cried properly since I was a boy. I don't know what to do with it.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"You don't have to know what to do with it. Just let it happen. I'm not going anywhere until it's finished.\"<\/p>\n<p>He stayed like that a long while, her hands steady in his hair, and slowly, gradually, the shaking eased, though neither of them spoke again for a long time after, both of them simply staying exactly where they were — her strength holding him now, the way his had held her through everything else.<\/p>\n<p>Near dawn, the sky outside beginning to lighten at its furthest edge, she began to cry properly — not the careful, contained tears of the restaurant, but something deeper, her whole body shaking with it, years of composure finally giving way now that there was no more evening left to protect.<\/p>\n<p>\"I don't know how to say goodbye to you,\" she said, when she could finally speak again, her voice raw. \"I keep trying to make my mind understand that this is the last time. Not the last time for a while. The last time. Ever. In this whole life. I don't think I'm capable of holding that thought properly. It keeps sliding away from me, like something too large to actually pick up.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I know.\" His own voice had gone unsteady now, the careful composure he'd been holding all night finally beginning to crack. \"I keep trying to hold it too. I keep failing.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"We're never going to sit across a table from each other again. Never going to argue about something small over dinner. Never going to fall asleep like this again, your hand in my hair. I'm never going to hear your voice at the other end of a phone line again, not really, not the way I've heard it every day for months.\" Her breath caught, broken. \"I don't know how a person is supposed to survive knowing every single one of those endings is happening tonight, all at once, in one room.\"<\/p>\n<p>He pulled her closer, both of them shaking now, neither trying to hide it from the other anymore.<\/p>\n<p>\"Then let's not spend what's left of it counting the endings,\" he said, his voice barely holding together. \"Let's just be here. For these last minutes. Nothing after. Just this.\"<\/p>\n<p>She nodded against his chest, unable to speak for a while, and they lay like that as the light kept changing outside, saying nothing, simply holding on. Both of them understood, with complete and unbearable clarity, that every second passing now was a second neither of them would ever get to have again, with each other, for the rest of their lives.<\/p>\n<p>\"I love you,\" she finally said, when the silence had stretched as far as it could hold. \"I need to say it one more time, properly, while I still have the right to say it to you. I love you, Vikram. Whatever I become after today — whoever's wife, whoever's mother — some part of me is going to keep loving you, quietly, for the rest of my life, whether I'm allowed to or not.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"I love you too,\" he said, and felt his own eyes finally give way completely, and didn't try to stop it. \"I will love you for the rest of my life, Naina. There won't be a version of me, ten years from now or thirty, that doesn't still carry this morning somewhere inside him.\"<\/p>\n<p>When she finally dressed, her hands were shaking too badly to manage the buttons on her kameez, and he did them for her, slowly, his own hands not entirely steady either. Both of them cried without any embarrassment left to hide it — two people dressing each other for the last time with the same tenderness they'd once used to shed everything careful between them for the first.<\/p>\n<p>At the door, she turned back to him one final time, her face wrecked and beautiful and entirely unguarded, and reached up to touch his face once, memorising it with her fingertips the way he'd once memorised hers with his eyes.<\/p>\n<p>\"This is goodbye,\" she said, and the word finally left her mouth whole, no longer avoided, no longer softened. \"Not until next time. Not see you soon. Goodbye, Vikram. For all of it. For every version of a life we don't get to have now.\"<\/p>\n<p>\"Goodbye, Naina.\" He held her hand against his face a moment longer before letting it go. \"Thank you for every single day of this. I will spend the rest of my life grateful I got to have them, even knowing how this morning was always going to end.\"<\/p>\n<p>She stepped into him then, one final time, her arms going around him with a force that had nothing careful left in it, and he held her back just as tightly, both of them clinging to each other in the doorway far longer than either had meant to, neither willing to be the one who let go first. Her tears soaked through his shirt, and his own fell freely into her hair, both of them crying openly now, without any composure left to protect, two people trying to hold on to a version of each other they both already knew they were about to lose for good.<\/p>\n<p>She left first, her steps unsteady despite everything she'd tried to hold together. He stood in the doorway long after she'd disappeared down the corridor, his own face wet, understanding that this exact moment — her hand slipping from his, her footsteps growing fainter, the grey light of that specific morning — was the last one either of them would ever get.<\/p>\n<p>No amount of memory, however carefully kept, was ever going to be enough to replace what he was actually losing — the way no photograph of a fire truly carries its warmth, only its shape.<\/p>\n<p class=\"scenebreak\">***<\/p>"}];
